\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Sold Heavily Ahead Of G7 Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 11:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The dollar has been hammered Thursday ahead of this weekend's meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao has made matters worse for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by making it clear that Beijing has no intention of allowing the yuan to appreciate faster.\par \par \uc2 Wen argued that a stronger yuan would reduce the already narrow margins of many Chinese exporters. The job losses among migrant workers would then risk "social and economic turbulence" that he said would be a disaster for the world.\par \par \uc2 His comments came as U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner called on emerging nations as well as China to show more flexibility in their exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 See the dollar's fall against the yen: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4696"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4696}}}\par \par \uc2 See the euro's rise against the dollar: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4695"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4695}}}\par \par \uc2 The dollar was already under pressure from rising speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve will increase quantitative easing next month. On Wednesday, the latest set of ADP employment numbers showed a 39,000 fall rather than an anticipated 20,000 rise, raising fears that the employment report for September will also prove disappointing.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was one of the main beneficiaries of the dollar's weakness, rising to its highest level--$0.9920--since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was first floated in 1983. The Aussie was helped by strong employment numbers showing a rise of 49,500 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 See the Aussie's rise: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4697"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4697}}}\par \par \uc2 The euro also benefited as investors continued to bet that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will retain its recent hawkish stance after its policy meeting Thursday. Unlike most other major central banks, the ECB has suggested that it is keen to start exiting its ultra-easy monetary stance despite continued concerns about euro-zone growth and the health of European banks.\par \par \uc2 The yen also resumed its recent rally as the dollar declined, helped by investors assuming that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen's rise ahead of the meeting of G7 finance ministers attending the International Monetary Fund conference in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell well below the level at which the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, with Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan repeating that the government will take decisive steps to stop volatile moves.\par \par \uc2 The pound also managed to post some gains against the dollar, even though a new survey from Halifax showed house prices falling by 3.6% last month, the largest ever monthly fall since records were started in 1983. This comes ahead of a Bank of England policy announcement Thursday. The bank is widely expected to leave rates unchanged but there is a chance it will decide to extend quantitative easing given the rapid pace of the recent economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Reports suggest that the U.K.'s coalition government plans to reschedule its fiscal cuts at a more even pace over the next five years. Some analysts suggest that this is the result of the sharp slowdown.\par \par \uc2 "Postponing fiscal cuts like welfare benefits could fuel concerns that the downside risks to the official near- and medium-term growth projections have grown of late. This in turn should be less supportive for sterling," said Valentin Marinov, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the euro was up at $1.3974 from $1.3929 late Wednesday in New York, according to EBS. But the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to trade shy of the emotive $1.40 level.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is down at Y82.50 from Y82.96 while the pound has risen to $1.5936 from $1.5885.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also down at CHF0.9580 from CHF0.9610 after hitting a new record low of CHF0.9555 while the euro has fallen to Y115.33 from Y115.55.\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493;\par \par \uc2 nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 07-10-10 0933GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101007e6a7000vm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
