\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Down, But Recoups A Bit Before Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 16:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)-- The dollar slumped broadly Thursday, hitting a 15-year low against the yen, a record low against the Swiss franc and its lowest level since 1983 against the Australian dollar, before regaining some poise as investors pared riskier positions ahead of Friday's all-important U.S. employment figures.\par \par \uc2 Still, investors continue to expect a fresh round of Federal Reserve stimulus just around the corner, leading the broad trend of dollar weakness to remain in place, said Steven Barrow, head of G10 strategy at Standard Bank in London.\par \par \uc2 Even a better-than-expected reading of U.S. weekly jobless claims was not enough to decidedly change the anti-dollar sentiment coursing through markets.\par \par \uc2 "At the end of the day, it's encouraging to see improvement in the data, but the pace of improvement is not enough to change the perceived policy coming," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Washington he said. "It's still about the Fed."\par \par \uc2 Thursday mid-morning, the euro was at $1.3940 from $1.3929 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose as high as $1.4030. The dollar was at Y82.29 from Y82.96, after falling as low as Y82.11, while the euro was at Y114.73 from Y115.55. The U.K. pound was at $1.5940 from $1.5885. The dollar was at CHF0.9641 from CHF0.9618.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 77.284 from 77.435, rebounding from a dip below 77, its lowest level since January.\par \par \uc2 While investors continue to speculate a Fed stimulus program is imminent, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's president, Jean-Claude Trichet, on the other hand articulated a steady-on approach to monetary policy at its Thursday press conference after having earlier left key rates unchanged. This contrast--the ECB on the path to eventual policy normalization, with the Fed possibly near to policy loosening--propelled the euro above $1.40.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of Friday's U.S. non-farm payroll report, considered a key gauge of the pace of the economy, investors have taken some risk off the table, helping the dollar recoup some its earlier losses against its rivals, including the euro. "Clients are hesitant to push too much," on the euro and other higher-yielding assets," said Amelia Bourdeau, senior G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Stamford, Conn.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar took advantage of broad greenback weakness and a much better-than-expected Australian jobs report, shooting to its highest level since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in 1983, gaining above the US$0.99 mark, leading some analysts to point to possible parity. The U.S. dollar took back some of the Aussie's gains as U.S. stocks dipped into the red. By mid-morning, the Australian dollar was at $0.9838.\par \par \uc2 Before posting a modest gain against the Swiss franc, the dollar earlier fell to a new all-time low against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dropping as low as CHF0.9555, while the greenback also declined against the yen, dropping to a fresh 15-year low, at Y82.11, and keeping investors on alert for signs of Japan in the market to prop the dollar and stem yen strength.\par \par \uc2 Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York, said there were no signs of Japan in the market to stem yen strength as of Thursday morning North American trading.\par \par \uc2 Investors still think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible, but they said the risk is decreasing for now because a meeting of financial ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrialized nations will be held this weekend. Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just before the meeting would raise difficult questions for Japanese authorities in trying to explain that they are not just pushing the yen lower to gain a trade advantage against other nations.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England also left its monetary policy unchanged Thursday, though speculation mounted whether the bank's Monetary Policy Committee would eventually enact another round of its own stimulus--asset purchases dubbed quantitative easing--which would likely weigh on the pound. Still, sterling gained more than 0.1% on the dollar, participating in the broadside against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Andrew J. Johnson in New York, Takashi Mochizuki in Tokyo contributed to this article.) [ 10-07-10 1042ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a7000ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
