\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Hits New 15-Yr Low; May Fall To 82-Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 08:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY hits fresh 15-year low of 82.62, tracking broad USD weakness vs majors, regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; pair last at 82.69. AUD strength vs greenback, in particular, weighing on USD/JPY; AUD/USD hits new post float high in wake of stronger-than-expected Australian jobs data, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Says large amount of stop-loss selling orders at 82.50, thus USD/JPY may extend losses to 82.00. EUR/USD rises to 8-month high, last at 1.3965; tips resistance at 1.4000. "All of today's moves are being driven by the strong Australian data," he says. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] Hang Seng Bank says Hong Kong faces major challenge from "elevated risk" of asset bubble due to expectation of prolonged low interest rates. Adds, global economy also showing widespread signs of slowing despite Hong Kong's exports and retail sales expanding with double-digit growth on-year in August. "We stay cautious about the outlook for the outwardly facing Hong Kong economy and see growth momentum (easing) significantly ahead," report says. Tips Hong Kong GDP to slow sharply to near +2.0% on-year for last two quarters of 2010 vs +8.0% on-year in 1Q and +6.5% in 2Q. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] The European peripheral bond yield spreads are stable in early trading Thursday ahead of a new French 10-year auction and a 3-year Spanish auction, with a slight tightening bias. A successful Spanish auction could underpin the tentative spread tightening trend seen over the past week, says Christoph Rieger, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist. The ECB also meet Thursday but nothing new is expected, although investors will be watching for any comments on the ECB's liquidity provision \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The 10-year Spanish/German yield spread trades 1 bp tighter at 176.5 bps, 10-year Italian/German is 2 bps tighter at 151.5 bps, 10-year Portuguese/German 1 bp tighter at 397.5 bps, 10-year Ireland/German 1 bp tighter at 422.5 bps and 10-year Greek/German 1.5 bps wider at 766 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD rises to new post-float high with almost nothing standing in its way to parity and beyond, say Sydney-based traders. "It's really just a matter of time at this point," says Brisbane-based trader. AUD/USD recently at 0.9870, pushing above key resistance level and post-float high of 0.9850 as Europe \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading opens in wake of strong Aussie jobs report earlier. Trader adds, 0.9870 could provide short-term resistance as some sell orders sit there, but is unlikely to be more than temporary hiccup. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are down 0.25 at 131.77, slipping below initial support at 131.84, and WestLB sees next support at 131.63, followed by 131.31. Resistance is at 132.20, 132.51, and 132.77. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad higher after weekly inflation for food articles shows decline while primary articles rose only marginally, showing signs price pressures may moderate further, says trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond rises to 99.13 post-data vs 99.07 before. Trader says, concerns still remain over possible rate action at Nov. 2 RBI monetary policy review, market to remain cautious. Adds, market may take some comfort from finance secretary's comment in New York Wednesday that "the acceptable level of inflation will have to be slightly higher." Trader tips benchmark 2020 bond yield in 7.91%-7.96% in session vs 7.93% last. Wholesale price index for primary articles rose 0.1% in week to Sept. 25, grew 18.53% on-year. Food articles index fell 0.1% in week, and was down marginally at 16.24% on-year. Fuel products index remained unchanged in week as well as on-year. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares likely to stay down for rest of session but losses not expected to widen given absence of newsflow. STI off 0.7% at 3166.28, with support tipped at 3150. "Technically, we note that the intraday MACD indicator is still heading lower at the moment but the rate of descent is showing signs of moderating. This suggests that the near-term downside momentum could be waning and we are unlikely to see any further significant retreat in the afternoon," says OCBC Investment Research analyst Philip Teo. Participation in broad market remains low, with volume just over 1 billion shares. Among STI components, notable decliners include recent key performers, such as \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG), off 2.3% at S$4.23, \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG), off 3.4% at S$4.57. Genting Singapore (G13.SG), off 2.9% at S$2.02. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall below 82.00 soon as "there are large amounts of" stop-loss sell orders between 82.75, 82.00, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. "I see some bids below the current level, but the amount of stop orders is just overwhelming," he says. USD/JPY last at 82.83. Adds, there's some easing of expectations among dealers that the Japan authorities would step into market to weaken JPY as they didn't act at 82.90-82.95 range earlier, where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds have opened lower Thursday ahead of the ECB's rate decision and supply lined up later in the session. The ECB is expected to hold rates Thursday but the press conference at 1230 GMT will be eyed given the recent rise in money market rates, in contrast with prospects of further policy easing by the Fed and the BOE. There is also a flurry of activity in the primary market, with France selling up to EUR9B of bonds, and EUR2.5B of 3-year bonds from Spain. A smooth passage for the Spanish supply could weigh on bunds, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . On the data front, German industrial production figures for August are out at 1000 GMT, following from a faster than expected rise in manufacturing orders on Wednesday. December bunds are down 0.18 at 131.84 and the 10-year bund is near flat at 100.25 to yield 2.221%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW slips on foreign equity fund inflows but finds firm support at 1,114.5 vs 1,118.0 yesterday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to 3-Seoul based traders. One trader estimates BOK may have bought around $500 million below 1,115.0, minutes before domestic session close. Still, pair remains heavily biased down due to growing expectations of further quantitative easing from Fed. Next support tipped at 1,110 but if pair falls below that level, "the pair will probably find some support at KRW1,108," as players see chances of another BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing, says foreign bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 24.4% at HK$2.11 billion midday, 4.7% of total market volume vs 4.5% at Wednesday's half-day, suggests view HSI's 0.2% pullback due to normal profit-taking. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) leads with HK$304.70 million, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$137.37 million, \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$98.76 million. Bank of China (3988.HK), Cnooc (0883.HK) round out top 5, with HK$69.62 million, HK$65.02 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.1%, S&P/ASX 200 +0.1%, Kospi down 0.2%, HSI off 0.2%, STI down 0.6%, KLCI 0.1% lower, SET down 0.3%, NZX-50 flat, Sensex down 0.2%, Taiex flat. Markets in China closed for holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD lower vs JPY on growing expectations of further quantitative easing from U.S. Fed. USD/JPY at 82.83 vs 82.96 in late NY trade; EUR/JPY at 115.40 vs 115.55, EUR/USD at 1.3928 vs 1.3929. AUD/USD hit fresh 26-month high of 0.9848 after data showed number of people in full-time work in Australia rose 55,800 to 7.98 million in September from August; pair last at 0.9845 vs 0.9714 late Wednesday. Robust labor market suggests RBA will likely hike rates again in order to contain surging economy. Australia's unemployment rate remained at as-expected seasonally adjusted 5.1% in September. Australian Industry Group Performance of Construction Index fell 2.4 points in September from August to 40.8. Growth in Korea's broadest measure of money supply, known as L money supply, rose provisional 9.4% on-year to KRW2,661.7 trillion; rise is slowest since August last year, when money supply expanded by 8.9%. Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves +$39.45 billion on-month in September at $1.110 trillion. Spot gold at $1,353.20/oz, up $4.10 cents vs NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures down 47 cents at $83.70/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0254ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a700049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
