ice index for primary articles rose 0.1% in week to Sept. 25, grew 18.53% on-year. Food articles index fell 0.1% in week, and was down marginally at 16.24% on-year. Fuel products index remained unchanged in week as well as on-year. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares likely to stay down for rest of session but losses not expected to widen given absence of newsflow. STI off 0.7% at 3166.28, with support tipped at 3150. "Technically, we note that the intraday MACD indicator is still heading lower at the moment but the rate of descent is showing signs of moderating. This suggests that the near-term downside momentum could be waning and we are unlikely to see any further significant retreat in the afternoon," says OCBC Investment Research analyst Philip Teo. Participation in broad market remains low, with volume just over 1 billion shares. Among STI components, notable decliners include recent key performers, such as \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG), off 2.3% at S$4.23, \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG), off 3.4% at S$4.57. Genting Singapore (G13.SG), off 2.9% at S$2.02. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall below 82.00 soon as "there are large amounts of" stop-loss sell orders between 82.75, 82.00, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. "I see some bids below the current level, but the amount of stop orders is just overwhelming," he says. USD/JPY last at 82.83. Adds, there's some easing of expectations among dealers that the Japan authorities would step into market to weaken JPY as they didn't act at 82.90-82.95 range earlier, where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds have opened lower Thursday ahead of the ECB's rate decision and supply lined up later in the session. The ECB is expected to hold rates Thursday but the press conference at 1230 GMT will be eyed given the recent rise in money market rates, in contrast with prospects of further policy easing by the Fed and the BOE. There is also a flurry of activity in the primary market, with France selling up to EUR9B of bonds, and EUR2.5B of 3-year bonds from Spain. A smooth passage for the Spanish supply could weigh on bunds, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . On the data front, German industrial production figures for August are out at 1000 GMT, following from a faster than expected rise in manufacturing orders on Wednesday. December bunds are down 0.18 at 131.84 and the 10-year bund is near flat at 100.25 to yield 2.221%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW slips on foreign equity fund inflows but finds firm support at 1,114.5 vs 1,118.0 yesterday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to 3-Seoul based traders. One trader estimates BOK may have bought around $500 million below 1,115.0, minutes before domestic session close. Still, pair remains heavily biased down due to growing expectations of further quantitative easing from Fed. Next support tipped at 1,110 but if pair falls below that level, "the pair will probably find some support at KRW1,108," as players see chances of another BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing, says foreign bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 24.4% at HK$2.11 billion midday, 4.7% of total market volume vs 4.5% at Wednesday's half-day, suggests view HSI's 0.2% pullback due to normal profit-taking. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) leads with HK$304.70 million, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$137.37 million, \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$98.76 million. Bank of China (3988.HK), Cnooc (0883.HK) round out top 5, with HK$69.62 million, HK$65.02 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.1%, S&P/ASX 200 +0.1%, Kospi down 0.2%, HSI off 0.2%, STI down 0.6%, KLCI 0.1% lower, SET down 0.3%, NZX-50 flat, Sensex down 0.2%, Taiex flat. Markets in China closed for holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD lower vs JPY on growing expectations of further quantitative easing from U.S. Fed. USD/JPY at 82.83 vs 82.96 in late NY trade; EUR/JPY at 115.40 vs 115.55, EUR/USD at 1.3928 vs 1.3929. AUD/USD hit fresh 26-month high of 0.9848 after data showed number of people in full-time work in Australia rose 55,800 to 7.98 million in September from August; pair last at 0.9845 vs 0.9714 late Wednesday. Robust labor market suggests RBA will likely hike rates again in order to contain surging economy. Australia's unemployment rate remained at as-expected seasonally adjusted 5.1% in September. Australian Industry Group Performance of Construction Index fell 2.4 points in September from August to 40.8. Growth in Korea's broadest measure of money supply, known as L money supply, rose provisional 9.4% on-year to KRW2,661.7 trillion; rise is slowest since August last year, when money supply expanded by 8.9%. Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves +$39.45 billion on-month in September at $1.110 trillion. Spot gold at $1,353.20/oz, up $4.10 cents vs NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures down 47 cents at $83.70/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Dubai Mercantile Exchange added its voice to criticism of U.S. efforts to overhaul financial market regulations, saying these hindering its plans to grow, adapt its business; exchange facing delays in launching new contracts and in relaxing its margin calculation rules, as it hasn't been able to receive approval for these changes from U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission, Chief Executive Thomas Leaver says. DME had planned to alter its margin calculation rules in late 2009 and to offer four new swaps, options contracts in early 2010, but progress slow as SEC and CFTC need time to lay out new rules for financial industry after Dodd-Frank act took effect. Earlier, \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2  Liffe's chief executive Thomas Callahan argued that possible new rules requiring some overseas exchanges to register with U.S. regulators could have "very severe unintended consequences." See: "=INTERVIEW: DME Faces Delays, Criticizes Regulatory Uncertainty" (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end flat at 8283.92 after touching fresh 8.5-month high of 8310.80 earlier; financial stocks higher amid rotational buying but property developers weak on profit-taking, says Taiwan International Securities assistant vice-president Andrew Teng. Many Taiwanese companies posted healthy September revenue, but market may have priced in good news. Tips Taiex in 8250-8350 band Friday. "Chances are high that the market will go through corrections in the near term," judging from weak momentum in market today, he says. Shining Building (5531.TW) down 1.5% at NT$33.00, Cathay (2882.TW) +0.5% at NT$49.25, TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.8% at NT$61.30. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely stand pat at semiannual monetary policy meet on Oct. 14 as any tightening could hurt export sector, invite speculative inflows. SGD already up around 7.0% vs USD so far this year, making it one of best performers in Asia. In April, MAS raised target band for SGD, targeted a "modest, gradual appreciation" for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on trade-weighted basis. Ravi Menon, permanent secretary in the Ministry of Trade and Industry said in September expects "sharp slowdown" in sequential growth for 2H 2010 as growth in key export markets falters. See =MONEY TALKS: MAS Set To Stand Pat As Singapore Dollar Soars (venkat.pr@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0246ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a700045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
