ones] USD/KRW slips on foreign equity fund inflows but finds firm support at 1,114.5 vs 1,118.0 yesterday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to 3-Seoul based traders. One trader estimates BOK may have bought around $500 million below 1,115.0, minutes before domestic session close. Still, pair remains heavily biased down due to growing expectations of further quantitative easing from Fed. Next support tipped at 1,110 but if pair falls below that level, "the pair will probably find some support at KRW1,108," as players see chances of another BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing, says foreign bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 24.4% at HK$2.11 billion midday, 4.7% of total market volume vs 4.5% at Wednesday's half-day, suggests view HSI's 0.2% pullback due to normal profit-taking. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) leads with HK$304.70 million, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$137.37 million, \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$98.76 million. Bank of China (3988.HK), Cnooc (0883.HK) round out top 5, with HK$69.62 million, HK$65.02 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.1%, S&P/ASX 200 +0.1%, Kospi down 0.2%, HSI off 0.2%, STI down 0.6%, KLCI 0.1% lower, SET down 0.3%, NZX-50 flat, Sensex down 0.2%, Taiex flat. Markets in China closed for holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD lower vs JPY on growing expectations of further quantitative easing from U.S. Fed. USD/JPY at 82.83 vs 82.96 in late NY trade; EUR/JPY at 115.40 vs 115.55, EUR/USD at 1.3928 vs 1.3929. AUD/USD hit fresh 26-month high of 0.9848 after data showed number of people in full-time work in Australia rose 55,800 to 7.98 million in September from August; pair last at 0.9845 vs 0.9714 late Wednesday. Robust labor market suggests RBA will likely hike rates again in order to contain surging economy. Australia's unemployment rate remained at as-expected seasonally adjusted 5.1% in September. Australian Industry Group Performance of Construction Index fell 2.4 points in September from August to 40.8. Growth in Korea's broadest measure of money supply, known as L money supply, rose provisional 9.4% on-year to KRW2,661.7 trillion; rise is slowest since August last year, when money supply expanded by 8.9%. Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves +$39.45 billion on-month in September at $1.110 trillion. Spot gold at $1,353.20/oz, up $4.10 cents vs NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures down 47 cents at $83.70/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Dubai Mercantile Exchange added its voice to criticism of U.S. efforts to overhaul financial market regulations, saying these hindering its plans to grow, adapt its business; exchange facing delays in launching new contracts and in relaxing its margin calculation rules, as it hasn't been able to receive approval for these changes from U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission, Chief Executive Thomas Leaver says. DME had planned to alter its margin calculation rules in late 2009 and to offer four new swaps, options contracts in early 2010, but progress slow as SEC and CFTC need time to lay out new rules for financial industry after Dodd-Frank act took effect. Earlier, \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2  Liffe's chief executive Thomas Callahan argued that possible new rules requiring some overseas exchanges to register with U.S. regulators could have "very severe unintended consequences." See: "=INTERVIEW: DME Faces Delays, Criticizes Regulatory Uncertainty" (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end flat at 8283.92 after touching fresh 8.5-month high of 8310.80 earlier; financial stocks higher amid rotational buying but property developers weak on profit-taking, says Taiwan International Securities assistant vice-president Andrew Teng. Many Taiwanese companies posted healthy September revenue, but market may have priced in good news. Tips Taiex in 8250-8350 band Friday. "Chances are high that the market will go through corrections in the near term," judging from weak momentum in market today, he says. Shining Building (5531.TW) down 1.5% at NT$33.00, Cathay (2882.TW) +0.5% at NT$49.25, TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.8% at NT$61.30. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely stand pat at semiannual monetary policy meet on Oct. 14 as any tightening could hurt export sector, invite speculative inflows. SGD already up around 7.0% vs USD so far this year, making it one of best performers in Asia. In April, MAS raised target band for SGD, targeted a "modest, gradual appreciation" for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on trade-weighted basis. Ravi Menon, permanent secretary in the Ministry of Trade and Industry said in September expects "sharp slowdown" in sequential growth for 2H 2010 as growth in key export markets falters. See =MONEY TALKS: MAS Set To Stand Pat As Singapore Dollar Soars (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall in afternoon trade due to bout of foreign-equity fund inflows, with strong AUD, EUR in Asian trade also buoying KRW, traders say. Pair now trading around 5-month lows at 1,115.4 vs 1,118.0 late yesterday in Seoul. "But I don't think pair will post any big losses today," given high chance of USD-buying, smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says foreign bank trader. Traders put initial support at 1,115, then at 1,110. Note, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb KRW gains for 4th straight session Wednesday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0544 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 0.3% at 975.77 at midday pause amid tepid mood in regional bourses. Benchmark wavering between positive, negative territories in active trade. While continued THB appreciation vs USD assures investors that fund inflows still ongoing, 10% year-to-date gain for THB raises worries that Thai authorities would impose measures to stem THB rise, says Finansia Syrus Securities' analyst Somchai Anektaweepon. Adds SET Index likely to continue in lackluster trade in afternoon session; tipped in 970-985 range. Among top actives, \uc2 PTT Exploration and Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) down 2.1% at THB166.50, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 0.8% at THB736, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) flat at THB2.30, PTT (PTT.TH) flat at THB302. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures down 0.2% at 9680, hovering near Wednesday close of 9700; sentiment sour as USD/JPY trading below 83 (now 82.81), market analysts say. Also say after rising sharply for past 2 days, it's not surprising to see investors take profits. While investors await outcome of U.S. jobs data Friday, they maintain cautious stance on U.S. economy, which will likely affect USD moves against JPY. Says index may stay in narrow range for remainder of day (range thus far: 9650-9730), with support pegged at 9600. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0515 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 1.3% at 3556.392 midday in moderate volume, on profit-taking in most blue chips after recent sharp gains, says trader; tips support at 3540. Main index still up 40% since start of 2010. "Selling is occurring across the board, with overall sentiment looking weak given falls in several Asian markets," says trader at Valbury Securities. Among decliners, heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 3.7% at IDR9,150, food producer \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) down 6.2% at IDR5,300, while coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) down 2.2% at IDR2,200. Bucking trend, \uc2 Indofood CBP\uc2  (ICBP.JK) +7.5% at IDR5,800 on trade debut. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0238ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a700040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
