\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Gold Hits Stops; Off High; Vietnam Move Helps\b0\par\par\uc2 1,694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,352.30/oz, up $3.20 just off new record high of $1,355.95. "Stops were hit at $1,350 and that's pushed us on. Silver is the leader, but all the precious metals are well-bid. To be honest, there just isn't a natural seller out there," says trader at European bank in Singapore. Adds, Vietnam move to allow some gold imports was an instigator for break of $1,350. "It's very much a USD-based economy, but there's just a rush to get more exposure to gold," he says. Gold used in Vietnam as collateral for cheap USD funding so many corporates seek gold holdings, he says. Surge in EUR/USD also factor; now at 1.3918, after touching highest level since late January. Does not expect gold to gap higher from here, but says dips well supported with major gold producer Anglogold (AU) energetically covering its remaining hedge book whenever price dips, he says. Spot silver at $23.30/oz, up 11 cents, but off 30-year high of $23.39 hit this morning. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 drifts down to 4680.3 after hitting 6-day high of 4697.3 on strong domestic jobs data. "The market rolled over a bit after testing 4700," says Patersons Senior Private Client Adviser Peter Morgan. "There's been more demand for resources than financials today but we're still very much dependent on what happens offshore." Adds that Australian dollar strength could be drawing profit taking from offshore investors, although any renewed quantitative easing by the U.S. Fed next month could help keep the current trends bullish in AUD/USD, Australian equities. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0453 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.1% at 1,478.02 midday in subdued volumes; trapped in tight range throughout morning session due to lack of any local or overseas cues. Broader market negative with decliners outnumbering gainers by 379 to 218, as investors look to book profits in stocks that ran up in yesterday's rally, dealer says. Adds, in short-term, market to take cues from U.S., Europe; tips rangebound trade for rest of session with index likely in 1,470-1,480 band, with 1,480 acting as strong barrier on upside. Decliners led by latex glove makers, down following disappointing 4Q results yesterday by Top Glove (7113.KU), which down 2.6% at MYR5.54, Supermax (7106.KU) down 2.6% at MYR4.15, \uc2 Latexx Partners\uc2  (7064.KU) down 2.5% at MYR2.71; other decliners include \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) down 1.0% at MYR3.10; gainers include Dialog Group (7277.KU) +1.8% at MYR1.15 after securing MYR61 million contract; SP Setia (8664.KU) +2.1% at MYR4.90. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD looks ever closer to its dance with parity, shaking off RBA's on hold decision and now boosted to fresh highs on robust jobs numbers. Next major level is 0.9850, but that looks to pose little resistance, given widening Australia-U.S. two year yield spread on government bonds. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  tips to look for 1.02 by year-end. RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Greg Gibbs notes RBA's tightening bias can only drive pair higher. "Australian interest rates have been normalized and are heading into restrictive territory. In contrast, policy in the G4 major economies is becoming more desperate." See: =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Aussie Dollar Ever Closer To Its Dance With Parity (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] As AUD/USD gets near new post-float high, little standing in way of parity, says Callum Henderson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for Standard Chartered. "Is the market nervous about taking on parity? It is psychologically important, but that is all it is. There may be some hesitation ahead of that, but when you have such a stark contrast in economic fundamentals and policy between the countries, further gains are easy to justify," says Henderson. "The question now is why should it stop after parity?" AUD/USD recently at 0.9836 after Aussie jobs report bested expectations earlier today, just off post-float high of 0.9850 set on July 15, 2008. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares remain underwater but pullback shallow, with investors awaiting more U.S. economic data, including Friday's September nonfarm payrolls, before taking new positions. STI off 0.6% at 3171.42 midday, expected to hold above 3150 for rest of session. "While we have the view that the STI is on a secular uptrend based on Asian growth, near-term macro risks from the U.S. are not insignificant," says Phillip Securities. Overall market volume paltry, with \uc2 Overseas Union Enterprise\uc2  (LJ3.SG) most heavily-traded stock, down 5.9% at S$2.87, following share placement by controlling shareholder. Sino Grandness Food (JS5.SG), which also carried out share placement recently, off 1.2% at S$0.41 in active trade. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9651.98 vs +0.1% midday close, index seesawing back and forth into positive territory as investors don't want to take large positions before closely eyed U.S. jobs data come out Friday, market analysts say. Also say although USD/JPY trading below 83 (now 82.90), heavy selling in exporters unlikely as there are hopes government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again; Nikkei may stay in range of 9550-9750 for rest of day. 22/33 Topix subindexes lower, with exporters generally weaker; Advantest (6857.TO) down 1.8% at Y1,734, Fanuc (6954.TO) off 1.7% at Y10,840. Retailers, real estate among top performing sectors; Aeon (8267.TO) up 5.8% at Y974 after stellar 1H FY earnings/outlook, Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 2.3% at Y1,510 on bullish central Tokyo office vacancy data. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares rise to new all-time high as fund inflows continue. Bellwether PSEi ends +1.2% at 4245.05 in heavy volume. "Investors have remained very bullish and we expect equity strength to persist throughout the year," says Jun Calaycay, Accord Capital Equities trader. Reckons \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep policy rate steady later today, keep market sentiment positive. Still, intermittent profit-taking expected following market's substantial gains in recent sessions; next resistance at 4270, support at 4200. Among gainers, PLDT (TEL.PH), +1.2% at PHP2,760; SM Investments (SM.PH), +1.1% at PHP535; Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH), +2.2% at PHP75.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.3% at 462.05 as foreigners buy blue chips on hopes banks will start lowering interest rates from mid-October to boost market liquidity. "Investors remain cautious, but they are coming back to the market after the state-run Vietnam Banking Association and government economists said the government will support local companies by lowering borrowing costs from October 15," says Hanoi-based fund management analyst. Volume thin with 37.3 million shares valued at VND983 billion traded. Among actives, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2 , Fertilizer (DPM.VH) ends +4.8% at VND32,900, Eximbank (EIB.VH) ends down 0.6% at VND17,800, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends flat at VND17,400. Tips more gains tomorrow; expects key index to move in 465-470 range Friday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT [Dow Jones] With participants agreeing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets now an important driver for commodities (including gold), it isn't surprising that reversal in EUR/USD direction, sparked rapid rise in spot gold to fresh record high of $1,355.95/oz at 0340 GMT; EUR/USD rose to $1.3923 from $1.3900 around 2:30GMT. But gold unable to sustain level, drifted lower as USD regained a little strength; gold last bid at $1,352.30/oz, +$3.20 from late NY levels. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0428 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says Bank of Japan's announced additional easing measures on October 5, including unveiling of potential asset purchase program targeting various assets, including JGBs, commercial paper, corporate bonds, ETFs, and J-REITs were "positive surprises". Adds, however, it remains to be seen how BOJ would react to further easing by U.S. Fed. "We think the announcement could help break the cycle of excessive yen appreciation versus the dollar, possibly triggering a re-entry into external demand-driven stocks," writes analyst in report. Over medium term, says BOJ's efforts to combat deflation, if successful, could help lift broader market, including domestic-focused sectors such as financials, construction, and real estate. Banks and real estate shares have been 2 of biggest sector gainers post-BOJ easing, rising 6.6% and 8.0%, respectively. USD/JPY has continued to fall, however, hitting 15-year low overnight; pair still looks weak under 83 (now 82.87), dragging on exporter shares. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options rise to 11.25%/11.95% from 11.10%/11.80% Wednesday in New York. Spot falls below 82.87 overnight, level hit just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15; may have prompted investors to buy hedges against pair's expected sharp rise on possible additional round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo in near term. Options dealer at major brokerage in Tokyo says multiple players buy 1-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 84.50 with varying volatilities (14.25%, 14.00%, 13.85%, 13.73%). Says those deals amount to $120 million altogether. Says vols may rise to 11.85 if spot extends falls to 82.50.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0057ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a700036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
