\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: AUD/USD Closer To Its Dance With Parity -  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 06:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD looks ever closer to its dance with parity, shaking off RBA's on hold decision and now boosted to fresh highs on robust jobs numbers. Next major level is 0.9850, but that looks to pose little resistance, given widening Australia-U.S. two year yield spread on government bonds. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  tips to look for 1.02 by year-end. RBS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Greg Gibbs notes RBA's tightening bias can only drive pair higher. "Australian interest rates have been normalized and are heading into restrictive territory. In contrast, policy in the G4 major economies is becoming more desperate." See: =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: Aussie Dollar Ever Closer To Its Dance With Parity (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] As AUD/USD gets near new post-float high, little standing in way of parity, says Callum Henderson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for Standard Chartered. "Is the market nervous about taking on parity? It is psychologically important, but that is all it is. There may be some hesitation ahead of that, but when you have such a stark contrast in economic fundamentals and policy between the countries, further gains are easy to justify," says Henderson. "The question now is why should it stop after parity?" AUD/USD recently at 0.9836 after Aussie jobs report bested expectations earlier today, just off post-float high of 0.9850 set on July 15, 2008. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares remain underwater but pullback shallow, with investors awaiting more U.S. economic data, including Friday's September nonfarm payrolls, before taking new positions. STI off 0.6% at 3171.42 midday, expected to hold above 3150 for rest of session. "While we have the view that the STI is on a secular uptrend based on Asian growth, near-term macro risks from the U.S. are not insignificant," says Phillip Securities. Overall market volume paltry, with \uc2 Overseas Union Enterprise\uc2  (LJ3.SG) most heavily-traded stock, down 5.9% at S$2.87, following share placement by controlling shareholder. Sino Grandness Food (JS5.SG), which also carried out share placement recently, off 1.2% at S$0.41 in active trade. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9651.98 vs +0.1% midday close, index seesawing back and forth into positive territory as investors don't want to take large positions before closely eyed U.S. jobs data come out Friday, market analysts say. Also say although USD/JPY trading below 83 (now 82.90), heavy selling in exporters unlikely as there are hopes government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again; Nikkei may stay in range of 9550-9750 for rest of day. 22/33 Topix subindexes lower, with exporters generally weaker; Advantest (6857.TO) down 1.8% at Y1,734, Fanuc (6954.TO) off 1.7% at Y10,840. Retailers, real estate among top performing sectors; Aeon (8267.TO) up 5.8% at Y974 after stellar 1H FY earnings/outlook, Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 2.3% at Y1,510 on bullish central Tokyo office vacancy data. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares rise to new all-time high as fund inflows continue. Bellwether PSEi ends +1.2% at 4245.05 in heavy volume. "Investors have remained very bullish and we expect equity strength to pers