\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Little Standing In Way Of AUD/USD Parity-StanChart\b0\par\par\uc2 1,595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 06:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] As AUD/USD gets near new post-float high, little standing in way of parity, says Callum Henderson, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy for Standard Chartered. "Is the market nervous about taking on parity? It is psychologically important, but that is all it is. There may be some hesitation ahead of that, but when you have such a stark contrast in economic fundamentals and policy between the countries, further gains are easy to justify," says Henderson. "The question now is why should it stop after parity?" AUD/USD recently at 0.9836 after Aussie jobs report bested expectations earlier today, just off post-float high of 0.9850 set on July 15, 2008. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares remain underwater but pullback shallow, with investors awaiting more U.S. economic data, including Friday's September nonfarm payrolls, before taking new positions. STI off 0.6% at 3171.42 midday, expected to hold above 3150 for rest of session. "While we have the view that the STI is on a secular uptrend based on Asian growth, near-term macro risks from the U.S. are not insignificant," says Phillip Securities. Overall market volume paltry, with \uc2 Overseas Union Enterprise\uc2  (LJ3.SG) most heavily-traded stock, down 5.9% at S$2.87, following share placement by controlling shareholder. Sino Grandness Food (JS5.SG), which also carried out share placement recently, off 1.2% at S$0.41 in active trade. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9651.98 vs +0.1% midday close, index seesawing back and forth into positive territory as investors don't want to take large positions before closely eyed U.S. jobs data come out Friday, market analysts say. Also say although USD/JPY trading below 83 (now 82.90), heavy selling in exporters unlikely as there are hopes government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again; Nikkei may stay in range of 9550-9750 for rest of day. 22/33 Topix subindexes lower, with exporters generally weaker; Advantest (6857.TO) down 1.8% at Y1,734, Fanuc (6954.TO) off 1.7% at Y10,840. Retailers, real estate among top performing sectors; Aeon (8267.TO) up 5.8% at Y974 after stellar 1H FY earnings/outlook, Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 2.3% at Y1,510 on bullish central Tokyo office vacancy data. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares rise to new all-time high as fund inflows continue. Bellwether PSEi ends +1.2% at 4245.05 in heavy volume. "Investors have remained very bullish and we expect equity strength to persist throughout the year," says Jun Calaycay, Accord Capital Equities trader. Reckons \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep policy rate steady later today, keep market sentiment positive. Still, intermittent profit-taking expected following market's substantial gains in recent sessions; next resistance at 4270, support at 4200. Among gainers, PLDT (TEL.PH), +1.2% at PHP2,760; SM Investments (SM.PH), +1.1% at PHP535; Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH), +2.2% at PHP75.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.3% at 462.05 as foreigners buy blue chips on hopes banks will start lowering interest rates from mid-October to boost market liquidity. "Investors remain cautious, but they are coming back to the market after the state-run Vietnam Banking Association and government economists sai