\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI Off 0.6%; Watch US Macro Risks -Phillip Sec\b0\par\par\uc2 1,578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 06:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares remain underwater but pullback shallow, with investors awaiting more U.S. economic data, including Friday's September nonfarm payrolls, before taking new positions. STI off 0.6% at 3171.42 midday, expected to hold above 3150 for rest of session. "While we have the view that the STI is on a secular uptrend based on Asian growth, near-term macro risks from the U.S. are not insignificant," says Phillip Securities. Overall market volume paltry, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Overseas Union Enterprise\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LJ3.SG) most heavily-traded stock, down 5.9% at S$2.87, following share placement by controlling shareholder. Sino Grandness Food (JS5.SG), which also carried out share placement recently, off 1.2% at S$0.41 in active trade. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9651.98 vs +0.1% midday close, index seesawing back and forth into positive territory as investors don't want to take large positions before closely eyed U.S. jobs data come out Friday, market analysts say. Also say although USD/JPY trading below 83 (now 82.90), heavy selling in exporters unlikely as there are hopes government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again; Nikkei may stay in range of 9550-9750 for rest of day. 22/33 Topix subindexes lower, with exporters generally weaker; Advantest (6857.TO) down 1.8% at Y1,734, Fanuc (6954.TO) off 1.7% at Y10,840. Retailers, real estate among top performing sectors; Aeon (8267.TO) up 5.8% at Y974 after stellar 1H FY earnings/outlook, Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 2.3% at Y1,510 on bullish central Tokyo office vacancy data. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares rise to new all-time high as fund inflows continue. Bellwether PSEi ends +1.2% at 4245.05 in heavy volume. "Investors have remained very bullish and we expect equity strength to persist throughout the year," says Jun Calaycay, Accord Capital Equities trader. Reckons \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep policy rate steady later today, keep market sentiment positive. Still, intermittent profit-taking expected following market's substantial gains in recent sessions; next resistance at 4270, support at 4200. Among gainers, PLDT (TEL.PH), +1.2% at PHP2,760; SM Investments (SM.PH), +1.1% at PHP535; Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH), +2.2% at PHP75.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.3% at 462.05 as foreigners buy blue chips on hopes banks will start lowering interest rates from mid-October to boost market liquidity. "Investors remain cautious, but they are coming back to the market after the state-run Vietnam Banking Association and government economists said the government will support local companies by lowering borrowing costs from October 15," says Hanoi-based fund management analyst. Volume thin with 37.3 million shares valued at VND983 billion traded. Among actives, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2 , Fertilizer (DPM.VH) ends +4.8% at VND32,900, Eximbank (EIB.VH) ends down 0.6% at VND17,800, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends flat at VND17,400. Tips more gains tomorrow; expects key index to move in 465-470 range Friday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT [Dow Jones] With participants agreeing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets now an important driver for commodities (including gold), it isn't surprising that reversal in EUR/USD direction, sparked rapid rise in spot gold to fresh record high of $1,355.95/oz at 0340 GMT; EUR/USD rose to $1.3923 from $1.3900 around 2:30GMT. But gold unable to sustain level, drifted lower as USD regained a little strength; gold last bid at $1,352.30/oz, +$3.20 from late NY levels. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0428 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says Bank of Japan's announced additional easing measures on October 5, including unveiling of potential asset purchase program targeting various assets, including JGBs, commercial paper, corporate bonds, ETFs, and J-REITs were "positive surprises". Adds, however, it remains to be seen how BOJ would react to further easing by U.S. Fed. "We think the announcement could help break the cycle of excessive yen appreciation versus the dollar, possibly triggering a re-entry into external demand-driven stocks," writes analyst in report. Over medium term, says BOJ's efforts to combat deflation, if successful, could help lift broader market, including domestic-focused sectors such as financials, construction, and real estate. Banks and real estate shares have been 2 of biggest sector gainers post-BOJ easing, rising 6.6% and 8.0%, respectively. USD/JPY has continued to fall, however, hitting 15-year low overnight; pair still looks weak under 83 (now 82.87), dragging on exporter shares. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options rise to 11.25%/11.95% from 11.10%/11.80% Wednesday in New York. Spot falls below 82.87 overnight, level hit just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15; may have prompted investors to buy hedges against pair's expected sharp rise on possible additional round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo in near term. Options dealer at major brokerage in Tokyo says multiple players buy 1-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 84.50 with varying volatilities (14.25%, 14.00%, 13.85%, 13.73%). Says those deals amount to $120 million altogether. Says vols may rise to 11.85 if spot extends falls to 82.50.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF sells Y2.0073 trillion of 0.8% 10-year bonds with lowest price of 99.60 yielding 0.843%, in-line with market expectations; bid-to-cover ratio at 2.85 vs 3.16 at previous auction in September, while tail shorter at 0.04 vs 0.10 at last tender. Auction results were fair, supported by dealers' short-covering, but investor demand not too strong due to extremely low yields (new issue carries lowest coupon since June 2003), caution ahead of key U.S. nonfarm payroll data due Friday, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita. Lead December JGB futures last down 0.19 at 143.96.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] Even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to conduct another round of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , USD/JPY unlikely to rise as much as it did in September 15, says Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Reckons many short-term hedge funds have set up their portfolio to make quick gains on any USD/JPY rises. Expects hedge funds to sell into strength (if MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  again) to cash out; "they have made \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  options portfolio in a way that could help them to make money on the dollar's rise against the yen. So they are actually eagerly waiting for Japan's action." Pair last at 82.91.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may slowly fall to 82.50 vs last 82.92 as investors taking cue from falling U.S. Treasury yields, which aren't likely to pick up anytime soon due to intensifying speculation Fed to ease further policy, says Shinkin Ass