\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Rise;Spot Fell Below 82.87 Overnight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 06:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options rise to 11.25%/11.95% from 11.10%/11.80% Wednesday in New York. Spot falls below 82.87 overnight, level hit just before \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Sept. 15; may have prompted investors to buy hedges against pair's expected sharp rise on possible additional round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Tokyo in near term. Options dealer at major brokerage in Tokyo says multiple players buy 1-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 84.50 with varying volatilities (14.25%, 14.00%, 13.85%, 13.73%). Says those deals amount to $120 million altogether. Says vols may rise to 11.85 if spot extends falls to 82.50.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF sells Y2.0073 trillion of 0.8% 10-year bonds with lowest price of 99.60 yielding 0.843%, in-line with market expectations; bid-to-cover ratio at 2.85 vs 3.16 at previous auction in September, while tail shorter at 0.04 vs 0.10 at last tender. Auction results were fair, supported by dealers' short-covering, but investor demand not too strong due to extremely low yields (new issue carries lowest coupon since June 2003), caution ahead of key U.S. nonfarm payroll data due Friday, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita. Lead December JGB futures last down 0.19 at 143.96.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] Even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to conduct another round of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , USD/JPY unlikely to rise as much as it did in September 15, says Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Reckons many short-term hedge funds have set up their portfolio to make quick gains on any USD/JPY rises. Expects hedge funds to sell into strength (if MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  again) to cash out; "they have made \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  options portfolio in a way that could help them to make money on the dollar's rise against the yen. So they are actually eagerly waiting for Japan's action." Pair last at 82.91.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may slowly fall to 82.50 vs last 82.92 as investors taking cue from falling U.S. Treasury yields, which aren't likely to pick up anytime soon due to intensifying speculation Fed to ease further policy, says Shinkin Asset Management senior dealer Jun Kato. Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step into market, but any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "cannot change the current dollar-weakness trend," he says. Speculation increasing that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  anymore due to mounting pressure from overseas; Reuters reports former top MOF official Naoyuki Shinohara said little point in Japan trying to guide USD/JPY higher as U.S., European policy to remain loose. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex flat, last off 0.1% at 20,522.79 after opening 0.2% higher, in line with subdued regional cues, caution after recent sharp run-up. "Trading is choppy and slow. Lack of clear direction from global markets, mainly the U.S., and high valuations after over 14% rally (since Sept. 1) are keeping investors cautious on committing fresh money," says dealer at local brokerage. Adds, expects trading to be rangebound, but with a downward bias. Tips Sensex in 20,380-20,600 range. Among heavyweights, \uc2 Reliance Communications (532712.BY) \uc2 +1.7% at INR179.90, \uc2 Hindustan Unilever\uc2  (500696.BY) +0.9% at INR304. Wipro (507685.BY) down 1.3% at INR457.70, \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 off 0.8% at INR3,076.80. \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY) +1.3% at INR356.65 while HDFC (500010.BY) down 1.1% at INR737. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0357 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad higher as upbeat U.S. Treasurys support, but some caution in market ahead of weekly food price data due mid-session, says trader with state-run bank; 7.80% government bond at 99.16 vs 99.15 last close. "Another uptick in food prices would definitely be bad news for bonds, and could spark a selloff," says trader. Notes, much may be read into food price data this week ahead of monthly inflation data due next week, already rife speculation on RBI policy rate hike at November 2 policy meeting. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 near flat at 4684.7 after the rally on above-consensus jobs data faded near the major technical resistance level of 4700 (61.8% retracement of April-May fall). With AUD/USD also testing major technical resistance, there could be some profit taking from offshore investors. In U.S. dollar terms, the S&P/ASX 200 is up a massive 34% since June, so profit taking must be tempting for any offshore investors who bought Australian equities in June. That said, there's no immediate catalyst for a sell-off and any renewed quantitative easing by the U.S. Fed could provide further motivation to buy equities and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . AUD/USD shook off the steady rate decision this week and further Australian rate hikes are expected, while the mining boom is widely expected to continue unabated for the next couple of years. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals all higher in Asia, but many market participants unimpressed. "It's the same for all the metals. It's speculators and funds driving this, but the physical market is much weaker than prices suggest," says executive from trading arm of large Japanese industrial name. "The central banks keep pouring in money and it has nowhere to go, so some comes toward commodities," he says. Executive at metals fabricator in Singapore agrees. "Some of our customers buy lots of silver for plating. How do you think they will respond to a 30% price rise?" LME 3-month copper at $8,306/ton, up $47 since yesterday's kerb with 723 lots done, nearing 26-month high of $8,326 hit yesterday. Spot silver at $23.35/oz, up 16 cents since NY close and at a new 30-year high. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 0.1% at 977.84 in choppy trade, with big cap energy, petrochemical plays dominating market volume. "Profit-taking pressure is increasing and investors are more cautious after the stock market's recent rally. Also, the dollar slipped through the THB30.00-level," raising concerns foreign investors may start considering cashing out, says analyst with BFIT Securities Anupon Sriard. Support tipped at 975, resistance at 986. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.7% at THB304, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) off 1.5% at THB167.50, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 1.1% at THB734, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) +1.7% at THB2.34, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) flat at THB135.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR slips as upbeat EUR weighs, but weak local stocks likely to support pair, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.33 vs 44.50 late Wednesday. "How well the EUR holds up at these levels will be the key. If the EUR shows signs of slackening, the INR could give up some gains too," says trader. But rangebound EUR and mildly weaker stocks should keep pair in 44.30-44.40 band in early trade, with 44.25-44.55 band likely for rest of session.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD consolidating overnight range of between 0.7500 and 0.7550, likely to remain stronger on weak USD, says \uc2 OzForex\uc2  manager of corporate trading Jim Vrondas; "U.S. dollar weakness is likely to continue up until we get the Fed meeting in early November as the market is expecting them to expand quantitative easing." Pair last 0.7530 vs 0.7523 early this morning. Adds, market will be looking at U.S. weekly jobless claims out tonight, more focus on U.S. nonfarm payrolls data Friday for cue on U.S. economic strength. Tips initial support at 0.7500 with further support at 0.7450, resistance at October 2009 high of 0.7635. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tad higher, tracking slight retreat of EUR in early Asian trade; pair last at 43.525 in moderate volume after settling at 43.505 late Wednesday and dipping to fresh 28-month low of 43.40 in early trade; support 43.30, resistance 43.75. Traders say market also awaiting what rule changes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might introduce to temper PHP's strength. "They (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) might lower the oversold limit of banks and that could spur some dollar buying," says one trader. Currently, banks are allowed overbought and oversold dollar positions of $50 million, or the equivalent 2.5% of unimpaired capital, whichever is lower; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set to announce policy action around 0800 GMT, after local spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closes. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0026ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a70002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pi