uptick in food prices would definitely be bad news for bonds, and could spark a selloff," says trader. Notes, much may be read into food price data this week ahead of monthly inflation data due next week, already rife speculation on RBI policy rate hike at November 2 policy meeting. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 near flat at 4684.7 after the rally on above-consensus jobs data faded near the major technical resistance level of 4700 (61.8% retracement of April-May fall). With AUD/USD also testing major technical resistance, there could be some profit taking from offshore investors. In U.S. dollar terms, the S&P/ASX 200 is up a massive 34% since June, so profit taking must be tempting for any offshore investors who bought Australian equities in June. That said, there's no immediate catalyst for a sell-off and any renewed quantitative easing by the U.S. Fed could provide further motivation to buy equities and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . AUD/USD shook off the steady rate decision this week and further Australian rate hikes are expected, while the mining boom is widely expected to continue unabated for the next couple of years. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals all higher in Asia, but many market participants unimpressed. "It's the same for all the metals. It's speculators and funds driving this, but the physical market is much weaker than prices suggest," says executive from trading arm of large Japanese industrial name. "The central banks keep pouring in money and it has nowhere to go, so some comes toward commodities," he says. Executive at metals fabricator in Singapore agrees. "Some of our customers buy lots of silver for plating. How do you think they will respond to a 30% price rise?" LME 3-month copper at $8,306/ton, up $47 since yesterday's kerb with 723 lots done, nearing 26-month high of $8,326 hit yesterday. Spot silver at $23.35/oz, up 16 cents since NY close and at a new 30-year high. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 0.1% at 977.84 in choppy trade, with big cap energy, petrochemical plays dominating market volume. "Profit-taking pressure is increasing and investors are more cautious after the stock market's recent rally. Also, the dollar slipped through the THB30.00-level," raising concerns foreign investors may start considering cashing out, says analyst with BFIT Securities Anupon Sriard. Support tipped at 975, resistance at 986. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.7% at THB304, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) off 1.5% at THB167.50, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 1.1% at THB734, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) +1.7% at THB2.34, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) flat at THB135.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR slips as upbeat EUR weighs, but weak local stocks likely to support pair, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.33 vs 44.50 late Wednesday. "How well the EUR holds up at these levels will be the key. If the EUR shows signs of slackening, the INR could give up some gains too," says trader. But rangebound EUR and mildly weaker stocks should keep pair in 44.30-44.40 band in early trade, with 44.25-44.55 band likely for rest of session.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD consolidating overnight range of between 0.7500 and 0.7550, likely to remain stronger on weak USD, says \uc2 OzForex\uc2  manager of corporate trading Jim Vrondas; "U.S. dollar weakness is likely to continue up until we get the Fed meeting in early November as the market is expecting them to expand quantitative easing." Pair last 0.7530 vs 0.7523 early this morning. Adds, market will be looking at U.S. weekly jobless claims out tonight, more focus on U.S. nonfarm payrolls data Friday for cue on U.S. economic strength. Tips initial support at 0.7500 with further support at 0.7450, resistance at October 2009 high of 0.7635. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tad higher, tracking slight retreat of EUR in early Asian trade; pair last at 43.525 in moderate volume after settling at 43.505 late Wednesday and dipping to fresh 28-month low of 43.40 in early trade; support 43.30, resistance 43.75. Traders say market also awaiting what rule changes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might introduce to temper PHP's strength. "They (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) might lower the oversold limit of banks and that could spur some dollar buying," says one trader. Currently, banks are allowed overbought and oversold dollar positions of $50 million, or the equivalent 2.5% of unimpaired capital, whichever is lower; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set to announce policy action around 0800 GMT, after local spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closes. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Lowest accepted price for 10-year JGBs for 0345 GMT auction result likely around 99.60, traders say. Latest issue carries coupon of 0.8%, lowest coupon since June 2003; while some analysts concerned about extremely low coupon, others expect firm demand as many investors still eager to buy long-term JGBs on back of BOJ's aggressive monetary easing. Lead December JGB futures down 0.08 at 144.07 at morning close. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0016ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a70002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
