\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW Tad Down But Off Early Lows;1,112 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 02:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW tad down at 1,117.3 vs 1,118.0 Wednesday, quickly coming off earlier low of 1,113.5 as Kospi eased 0.3%, while players increasingly wary about possibility of USD buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Traders note \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb KRW gains for 4th-straight session yesterday. "Still, I reckon, the downward pressure on pair will remain. Nothing has changed much," with broadly weaker USD, on expectations for more quantitative easing from Fed, pressuring USD/KRW lower, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,112-1,122 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.2% at 9668.60, as exporters suffer with USD/JPY trading below 83 (now 92.96), but Topix up 0.2% as real estate shares, trading houses rise; Nikkei may trade between 9550-9750 today, say market analysts. "The market may take a break today," says Yoshinori Nagano, senior strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Asset Management\uc2 , adding profit-taking kicking in as Nikkei was strong over prior 2 sessions (adding 3.3%). Advantest (6857.TO) down 1.4% at Y1,741, \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) down 1.3% at Y4,595. Real estate stocks extending gains on BOJ easing steps, with \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 1.9% at Y1,585, Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 2.0% at Y1,505. \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2  (8058.TO) up 0.7% at Y2,036 on rising gold prices. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures lower, may extend drops to 144.00 as investors likely to continue selling long-term bonds ahead of 10-year JGB tender later in day, says trader at Tokyo securities house. Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities, says many players think 0.8% or 0.9% coupon not so attractive, thus unlikely to draw strong buying interest. If tender results due 0345 GMT turn out to be worse than expected, long-term cash bonds could be sold in secondary market, pushing yields higher. JGB futures last at 144.10, down 0.05; key 10-year JGB yield flat at 0.835%.(tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0015 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR tad lower at 3.0880 vs 3.0910 late yesterday on broad USD weakness vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; pair, which largely trapped in 3.0800-3.1000 range for last 2 weeks, not expected to break out of the band due to dearth of fresh domestic cues. Dealers suspect USD buying by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in previous sessions, although in small doses; that suggests limited MYR's gains near-term; "The 3.0800 (USD/MYR) support won't be so easily broken," dealer says. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold bid $1,345.20/oz, down $3.90 from late NY, softens after posting fresh all-time high overnight at $1,349.80, after USD strengthens tad this morning against EUR, suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains primary driver at this point. ScotiaMocatta, in daily technical analysis, posits various targets for gold, says break above $1,320 opened the measured target of $1,359 from width of its 2-month bull channel. "Only a close back below $1,320 would shake our bullish enthusiasm," with long term target remaining at $1,369 from a 2-year up move off $683, post-Lehman collapse, it says; adds, primary support now at $1,341, resistance $1,351. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Mixed session on Wall Street may prompt investors to take some profits off Singapore shares in early trade. Ahead of Singapore's earnings season starting next week, investors expected to take cues from economic data, with surprise fall in U.S. September private-sector employment potentially keeping sentiment guarded. STI, +0.9% at 3190.07 yesterday, expected to hold above 3150, with resistance tipped at 3214 (June 2, 2008 high). "The U.S. private-sector jobs report could mean last month's nonfarm payrolls data (due Friday) will disappoint. The market could be in a wait-and-see mode today," says local house trader. \uc2 Overseas Union Enterprise\uc2  (LJ3.SG) may gain as property group's controlling shareholder seeks to sell 120.5 million shares at S$2.80 each, lowering stake to 67.07% from 79.35% in move which could improve trading liquidity; stock +2.0% at S$3.05 yesterday. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0004 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  reckons that regulatory risk will increase for Australia's big banks if they try to protect margins and ROEs in their retail banking arms with 'out-of-cycle' rises in home loan rates during the 2011 fiscal year. "This means that retail bank profitability could be under threat even if home loan re-pricing occurs this year," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. "Sources of downside regulatory risk include lower mortgage exit fees, lower transaction account fees, home loan pricing constraints, capped ATM fees and lower credit card fees." (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely rangebound but biased up after overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys. Dealer says while participants await announcement on reopening of 7-year benchmark September 2017 MGS, bonds may take cue from external factors in near term; adds, following moderate declines in bond prices in recent days, some buying interest likely to emerge amid expectations of further MYR gains. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGSs ended mostly higher, with May 2013 +2 bps at 3.15%, August 2015 +1 bp at 3.26%, September 2017 down 1 bp at 3.56%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] Premature to suggest top to sharp rally by oil prices, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Nymex crude for November delivery settled 41 cents higher, or 0.5%, overnight at $83.23/bbl; contract hit $84.09 intraday, highest front-month price since May. Thursday trade likely to see market consolidate before "volatility spike" on Friday when the U.S. releases non-farm payrolls data, providing latest snapshot of health of U.S. economy. "While nearby crude futures achieved and exceeded our previously mentioned upside possibility at the $84 area, a bullish momentum play remains very much alive across the complex," Ritterbusch says. Nymex crude for November delivery now up 12 cents at $83.35/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2354 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.2% at 4677.4, earlier hit 4668.3, primarily due to a fall in financials after U.S. banks lost ground overnight. Major banks down 0.5%-0.9%, while \uc2 Macquarie Group\uc2  (MQG.AU) down 0.9% and most other sectors under slight downward pressure. However, resources are up amid commodity price strength, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) up 0.8%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 0.9%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 0.6%, \uc2 OZ Minerals\uc2  (OXL.AU) up 3.5%. Expectations of renewed U.S. quantitative easing expected to limit downside potential after disappointing private sector jobs data. But some traders now expect consolidation to prevail before Friday's U.S. jobs data. "Global equities markets are drifting as they look for a lead from the U.S. Federal Reserve," says Macquarie Private Wealth associate director Martin Lakos. "I think markets will just mark time before we see the U.S. nonfarm payrolls data on Friday. We remain positive on equity markets, although they ran hard last month." Favors resources because of strength in China, and retailers as employment remains strong. Focus now turns to domestic jobs data at 0030 GMT. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2346 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,118.0 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking broadly weaker USD after weak U.S. private jobs data further stoked expectations Fed will act soon to stimulate slowing U.S. economy. "Amid anticipation for extra quantitative easing from the Fed, financial markets are in an untiring rally," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. Tips 1,108-1,120 range for day, with eyes on possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities and whether 1,110 support will hold. Adds "the next (key) support will be at 1,100, a level which hasn't been breached since the global financial crisis started." USD/KRW last below 1,100 on September 12, 2008. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to fresh 15-year low at 82.30, based on chart analysis, in global day, below overnight low of 82.75, as view strengthening Fed may take additional quantitative easing measures as ADP report overnight shows U.S. private jobs data weaker-than-expected, says Satoshi Tate, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 ; data fuel concerns that non-farm payrolls for September due Friday may also turn out weak. Tate says if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market pair may rise to 85.50, otherwise upside limited around 83.50 in global day. Says market focused on U.S. jobless claims for week ending Oct. 2 due 1230 GMT. USD/JPY last 83.00. Tate says EUR/USD biased up due to broad USD weakness, may trade 1.3830-1.4020 range vs last 1.3913. EUR/JPY may trade in 114.70-116.20 vs last 115.48.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 10-06-10 2026ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a70000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
