\par\par \b \uc2 POINT OF VIEW: Noise Levels Up In  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  'War'; Will China Budge?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal Lipschutz  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 21:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 It's war!\par \par \uc2 War is a pretty strong word and one not often used in financial reporting. But there it is, over and over. The word war is used in direct quotes and out of them. It's the operative term to describe what is going on in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 We might be short of war, but clearly things have changed. Just today, the rhetorical level aimed at China's mostly unchanging yuan is higher than before. And it's been pretty high.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury secretary, Timothy Geithner, still employs non-threatening words and won't use China's name. But, still.... "When large economies with undervalued exchange rates act to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating, that encourages other countries to do the same, and this sets off a dangerous dynamic," Geithner said.\par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires headline Wednesday afternoon reads, "EU President: Europe Demands China Budge On \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," implying the comments earlier in the day of Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao in Brussels--in which he implored Europe's leaders not to "join the chorus pressing to revalue the yuan"--fell on deaf ears.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will launch more quantitative easing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still strong against the dollar. Chile has publicly worried about its too-high \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as has Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the U.S., markets are reacting as if it is a signed-and-delivered deal that the U.S. Federal Reserve will embark on more quantitative easing of its own, knocking more starch out of the weakened dollar. The final catalyst might be if Friday's September jobs report is distressing.\par \par \uc2 The world's major nations have done a solid job since the start of the credit crisis--and the subsequent broad and deep recession--of avoiding significant protectionist trade measures. It would be disappointing indeed to mitigate that necessary success with a no-holds-barred fight to gain advantage through weaker exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 It might not be up to China to stop all this, but some real, rather than apparent, relaxation of controls on the yuan by the Chinese government surely would help calm things down.\par \par \uc2 (Neal Lipschutz is senior vice president and managing editor of Dow Jones Newswires. He can be reached at 212-416-2285 or by e-mail at neal.lipschutz@dowjones.com. Neal and other senior Dow Jones Newswires editors blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://randomnotes.newswires-americas.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://randomnotes.newswires-americas.com }}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 10-06-10 1543ET ]\par \par \uc2 70701  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101006e6a6000jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
