\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-Will Bank of Japan's "comprehensive easing" work?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rie Ishiguro and Stanley White    \par \uc2 1,080 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 14:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 6 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan pledged to pump more funds into the struggling economy and keep interest rates virtually at zero, surprising markets and stealing a march on the Federal Reserve in providing a fresh dose of economic stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Following are key questions and answers about the array of steps announced by the BOJ on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 WILL IT WORK?\par \par \uc2 Analysts agree the BOJ was more creative and bolder than had been expected with what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor, Masaaki Shirakawa, called a "comprehensive monetary easing".\par \par \uc2 That explained the yen's initial fall against the dollar. However, investors are also looking at what wasn't included, which explains the subsequent rise in the yen.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will buy Japanese government bonds using cash from a 5 trillion yen ($60 billion) fund. But it is keeping its ceiling on purchases of long-term JGBs unchanged, while making the new fund an exception to this rule. The ceiling will continue to match the value of money in circulation.\par \par \uc2 A 30 trillion yen loan scheme is also unchanged.\par \par \uc2 The 5 trillion-yen pool of funds is designed to buy up various assets, including government debt, exchange-traded funds and real estate investment trusts. The BOJ hopes that will push down long term rates.\par \par \uc2 But the amount is just a fraction when measured against total government bonds outstanding of 904 trillion yen, suggesting its impact may be limited. That may explain why 30-year bond yields rose after the BOJ announcement.\par \par \uc2 The decision to cut its policy rate to a range of zero to 0.1 percent from 0.1 percent seems to have limited scope since the BOJ will still set a floor for money markets by offering interest on excess reserves of 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 A slightly lower policy rate is unlikely to boost loans demand anyhow. The balance of outstanding loans in Japan has fallen from a year earlier for nine straight months.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ special loans scheme has seen reasonable demand but has had little impact on market rates.\par \par \uc2 So the cut in the policy rate was more a symbol of the BOJ's intent as it suggested by saying it will maintain virtually zero rates until price stability is in sight. In other words, rates will stay at these levels unless deflation is licked.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ may have surprised with its policy easing, but any benefits may quickly be undone because the Fed is expected to follow suit shortly with fresh measures to stimulate the U.S. economy, weighing on the dollar and lifting the yen.\par \par \uc2 That may explain why the BOJ was not as aggressive as some had hoped. It still has tools in its monetary policy kit just in case the fall in industrial production and slide in exports growth gathers momentum and pushes the economy back towards another recession.\par \par \uc2 WHAT POLICY OPTIONS ARE LEFT?\par \par \uc2 The BOJ could return to full-blown quantitative easing (QE) first adopted nearly a decade ago, targeting the amount of banks' current account deposits at the BOJ instead of interest rates.\par \par \uc2 However, the BOJ is not convinced the policy was that effective so it will be reluctant to adopt QE again. The abundant supply of funds under QE did little to encourage an increase in capital spending, or therefore bank lending, because of pessimism about the economy and structural problems of the banking system.\par \par \uc2 Other options; it could expand the amount and range of assets it purchases via the new fund, such as including asset-backed securities backed by loans to small businesses. That could be more effective because small businesses employ 70 percent of Japanese workers.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa said on Tuesday that the BOJ could consider expanding the fund if necessary.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ could expand its fixed-rate funding tool again, although such a decision could be met with scepticism given the limited effect of the facility so far.\par \par \uc2 Another option is to stop paying 0.1 percent interest on excess reserves. This would force banks to think twice about what to do with the cash by eliminating their incentive to park it at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHAT HAS CHANGED SINCE THE BOJ LAST ADOPTED QE?\par \par \uc2 When the BOJ introduced quantitative easing in 2001, Japan was in a financial crunch. The BOJ force-fed money into the banking system, but widespread pessimism about the economy prevented any significant pick up in loan demand.\par \par \uc2 Banks were reluctant to boost lending anyhow because they were saddled with huge non-performing loans. The economy eventually recovered thanks to a rebound in the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Still, QE helped stabilise the financial system because the extra money acted as a buffer for emergency funding against events such as a run on bank deposits.\par \par \uc2 It also effectively accommodated \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that lasted for 15 months until March 2004 and amounted to 35 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 Fast forward to 2010 and Japan's financial system is relatively healthy and in little need of a safety net.\par \par \uc2 But the global economic situation is very different. Developed economies are suffering and vying to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition, the Federal Reserve and the Bank of England are conducting quantitative easing, limiting the yen-weakening effects of Japan's monetary policy. So the BOJ may yet be forced down the road of full QE.\par \par \uc2 WHAT IS THE GOVERNMENT DOING TO HELP THE ECONOMY?\par \par \uc2 The cabinet is considering an extra budget worth around 4.8 trillion yen ($57.7 billion) to stimulate the economy, a level roughly matched by the BOJ's 5 trillion yen pool of funds.\par \par \uc2 It is uncertain how quickly the budget can become law because the government would need opposition support and so far the opposition has been lukewarm to the idea.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, the economic impact may not be immediate because much of the spending is aimed at supporting the social safety net.\par \par \uc2 The government aims to raise the funds without tapping bond markets, arguing it can squeeze money out of reserves created by lower-than-expected interest payments on debt and a tax revenue windfall. Still, the government will face pressure to increase stimulus and issue new bonds from some members of the ruling party who favour more spending. ($1=83.24 Yen) (Editing by Edmund Klamann and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (Q+A)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101006e6a6000tt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
