\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Fed Fears Pressure Dollar; Euro Up A Little\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 11:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The dollar was under pressure again Wednesday after Chicago Federal Reserve President Charles Evans suggested that the Federal Reserve could be more accommodative.\par \par \uc2 This once again raised expectations that the Fed will expand its quantitative easing program at the next policy meeting early in November.\par \par \uc2 However, the dollar's losses against most other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were limited.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the euro, investors were reluctant to push the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much closer to $1.40 ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting Thursday as well as the meeting of finance ministers from the \uc2 Group\uc2  of Seven leading industrial nations in Washington this weekend.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro has bounced around just under $1.40:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4689}}}\par \par \uc2 Although the ECB is expected to remain hawkish, as members of its governing council debate exit strategies from the unconventional monetary easing introduced the year before last, some at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may express concern about the risk that euro strength could pose to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc's recovery.\par \par \uc2 There is also some concern about what G-7 ministers might say about the growing trend for competitive devaluations as most major economies attempt to export themselves out of recession. The International Monetary Fund issued its own warning about a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" if countries continue to use exchange rates to solve domestic problems.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, investors are also probably wary of buying the euro too much ahead of the latest bond offering from Portugal, a EUR1 billion auction of three-month and 12-month paper later Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also slipped a little against the yen but this time losses were limited by the continued threat of more Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There had been talk in New York late Tuesday that Japanese agencies close to the government were active buyers of the dollar against the yen.\par \par \uc2 However, there is still no indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has launched a new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The prospect of new unemployment data from the U.S. Friday is also hanging over the dollar, especially as a disappointing number will only increase speculation about the possibility of more quantitative easing next month.\par \par \uc2 Later Wednesday, attention will center on the latest private-sector employment figures from ADP, which are expected to show a 20,000 increase last month after a 10,000 fall in August.\par \par \uc2 A rally in the pound, meanwhile, ran out of steam after hitting resistance at $1.5938. Analysts said that pound supporters were also getting nervous ahead of the Bank of England's latest policy meeting, also Thursday, in which dovish members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee could raise expectations of further quantitative easing in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the euro has risen to $1.3858 from $1.3835 late Tuesday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also managed to rise to Y115.13 from Y115.11 while the dollar slipped to Y83.07 from Y83.18.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell back a little to CHF0.9664 from CHF0.9671, while the pound reversed much of its earlier gains but was still up at $1.5912 from $1.5896.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 06-10-10 0925GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101006e6a6000t2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
