\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stock Markets Seen Resuming Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 3,216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen up, government debt little changed; \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in ranges; spot gold up, oil futures lower\par \par \uc2 -US Treasury: Total Cost Of TARP Will Be $50 Billion\par \par \uc2 -RWE Mulls Selling High Voltage Power Grid - Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Hochtief\uc2  Wants To Force ACS To Also Bid For Its Leighton Unit\par \par \uc2 -VTB Of Russia Confirms Talks On Stake Sale - FT\par \par \uc2 -Mexico Sells $1 Billion 100-Year Bond To Yield 6.1%\par \par \uc2 -IMF Chief Warns Against Exchange Rate Battles - FT\par \par \uc2 -Rio Denies Making Final Decision To Abandon BHP Joint Venture\par \par \uc2 -IMF Official: Yen Level Correct In Medium Term - Nikkei\par \par \uc2 -WSJ: IMF Talks Focus On \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Restraints\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0830 GMT UK Q2 Profitability of UK companies; 0900 GMT EU Q2 GDP - 2nd release; 1000 GMT GER Aug Manufacturing orders; 1100 GMT US Oct 1 MBA Weekly Mortgage Applications Survey; 1130 GMT US Sep Challenger Job-Cut Report; 1215 GMT US Sep ADP National Employment Report\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Rallying metals are helping Asian bourses higher on Wednesday, as investors seek alternatives to dollar investments. European bourses are likely to start higher, with government debt little changed. The euro is barely changed, while oil futures are lower and spot gold higher.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Investors are likely to bid European stock markets higher on Wednesday, with risk aversion receding though a healthy dose of caution lingers in the background.\par \par \uc2 For Wednesday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE up 25 at 5660, the DAX up 34 at 6249, and the CAC up 12 at 3743.\par \par \uc2 "For now, the markets remain in buoyant mood," said Kathleen Brooks, research director at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com. "The market is currently positioned for a weak (U.S. September) payrolls number. It will be interesting to see if this stance remains as we get closer to Friday. If investors start to get nervous we could see some upward pressure on the greenback."\par \par \uc2 David Jones at \uc2 IG Index\uc2  added, "a degree of caution should probably be attached to these gains. The start of the U.S. earnings season and nonfarm payrolls later this week both have the potential to disappoint."\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are higher on Wednesday, after markets rallied to a five-month high Tuesday, boosted by encouraging services-sector data and hopes that global central banks will follow Japan's lead in stimulating economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The materials, industrials and financial sectors led.\par \par \uc2 Investor optimism comes ahead of some key data later this week. Dow-component \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  unofficially kicks off earnings season after the close Thursday. The closely watched nonfarm payrolls report follows Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 Bruce Bittles, chief investment strategist at Robert W. Baird, said he thinks Friday's jobs report will be a "win-win" for the market. Regardless of what it says about the economic recovery, a weak report will give the Fed more firepower to engage in quantitative easing, while a strong report will signal an improving economy, he said.\par \par \uc2 "As long as the Fed is willing to print money, all these economic reports are going to be considered favorable for the markets," Bittles said. "This will last for as long as the Fed is willing to backstop the stock market."\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets posted solid gains Wednesday, riding Wall Street's coattails, with gold miners around the region surging as the yellow metal hit a record high.\par \par \uc2 Possible "quantitative easing in the U.S. will be the big event for equities globally," said City Index Head of Dealing Michael McCarthy. "I think asset prices around the globe are on the way up. If Friday's U.S. jobs data are weak, I think the market will be relying on the (Federal Reserve chief Ben) Bernanke put option (i.e. further quantitative easing) and, if it shows strength, investors could also buy the market, so it's a win-win scenario."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors are trading in tight ranges after the dollar's losses Tuesday. Yen traders remained on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities as the dollar edged closer to Y82.87, the level at which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen in September.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell sharply Tuesday against the euro and dropped to its lowest point versus the yen since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors' attention turned to global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions targeted at propping up slowing economies.\par \par \uc2 Investors have fled the dollar as they gauge the Federal Reserve to be increasingly likely to engage in another round of economy-stimulating asset purchases, known as quantitative easing. Heightened chances for a second round of asset purchases by the Fed have weighed on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Clearly this day has revolved around quantitative easing," said Andrew Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The euro Tuesday rose 1.2% to its highest mark since February, extending its gains as far as $1.3860. The dollar fell below the Y83 mark for the first time since the Japanese government's Y2-trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15 to hinder the yen's appreciation. The greenback came within striking distance of its pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  15-year low at Y82.87, though the scale of its move lower was modest, dipping less than 0.2% on the day.\par \par \uc2 The euro remains the leader among major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because "the (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) looks the most likely not to restart quantitative easing," said Aroop Chatterjee, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  quantitative strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc gained strongly against the dollar because investors are banking on a weaker greenback after Fed easing and are heading for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that promise stability, said Lena Komileva, head of G-7 market economics at Tullett Prebon in London.\par \par \uc2 "The Swiss franc is the gold equivalency of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said.\par \par \uc2 Sterling, now higher against the dollar, looks to remain firm, underpinned by negative dollar sentiment, higher investor risk appetite, and the surprise rise in the U.K. services PMI. Against the euro, sterling is likely to consolidate around current levels.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds may open little changed ahead of more supply and data, while investors keep an eye on the sovereign debt issue, especially involving Greece.\par \par \uc2 Greek bond yields have fallen substantially over the last month suggesting a new trend may be establishing itself, but they still have a long way to go to make borrowing viable for the cash-strapped country.\par \par \uc2 On the supply front, Wednesday Germany conducts the final reopening of the Schatz 0.75% September 2012 for EUR5 billion. "This is a cheap bond and we expect strong demand," said \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the data calendar, German manufacturing orders and output for August are forecast to show a loss of momentum in this key sector.\par \par \uc2 "Orders and output data for the manufacturing sector were exceptionally buoyant in the second quarter," said \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  economists. "Leading indicators, however, signal a marked slowdown in activity in coming months."\par \par \uc2 Gilts may start little changed and seem capped as hopes of additional Bank of England asset purchases this year dimmed somewhat as Tuesday's data showed an improvement in the U.K. services sector. However, lingering expectations of additional Fed easing are keeping the downside limited. The U.K. economic calendar for Wednesday is very light.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury prices are higher Wednesday, after markets clung to slight gains Tuesday, pushing yields on most maturities down, as actions taken in Japan and Europe heightened expectations among bond investors that the Federal Reserve may be inclined to similarly ease monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Chicago Fed President Charles Evans said he favors "much more (monetary) accommodation" in an interview published by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "The issue with everyone doing QE is that the country that has the bigger program is the one that will win," said bond strategists at Nomura Securities. "We believe the U.S. Fed will set the bar high for others and the dollar will continue to stay weak. These \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows will result in short-term U.S. Treasury rates staying low," while the gap with long-term rates may increase, steepening the so-called yield curve.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo on Wednesday, the lead Japanese government bond futures crossed 144.00 for the first time in more than seven years, supported by the Bank of Japan's ambitious monetary easing measures. The December contract was recently up 0.50 at 144.1 points while the yield on the cash 10-year JGB was down seven basis points at 0.840%.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Oil prices are down Wednesday as forecasts of a massive increase in U.S. crude stockpiles tempered market sentiment following recent rallies, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in November, fell 15 cents to $82.67 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Brent North Sea crude for November delivery shed seven cents to %84.77.\par \par \uc2 Data released by the American Petroleum Institute Tuesday showing a spike in U.S. crude reserves was checking oil prices, said Victor Shum, senior principal of Purvin and Gertz international energy consultants in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "What's causing this adjustment this morning...is the inventory report from the API," he said.\par \par \uc2 "The API report indicated crude stockpiles climbing by 4.4 million barrels in the last week. That's a huge gain," added Shum.\par \par \uc2 The crude slump comes on the heels of recent rallies in the oil market, but Shum said prices would continue to be supported by a disruption in oil supply in the U.S. Gulf Coast and a rolling strike in a key French oil terminal.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Metals are all rallying Wednesday in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves to inject ever increasing amounts of liquidity into global markets.\par \par \uc2 Gold hit a record high of $1,344.20 a troy ounce, while copper was at its highest level since July 2008 at $8,250 a metric ton as metals traders reacted to a weak dollar and the likelihood of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, rather than fundamentals. It was last at $1,343.90 per troy ounce, up $3.30 from its New York close on Tuesday. [ 06-10-10 0500GMT ]\par \par \uc2 "It's a money war. Everything is moving up in tandem. Gold, copper, equities. It seems every major country wants a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and metals are getting bid higher as a result," said a precious metals trader at a Japanese house in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 06-10-10 0500GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10944.72  +193.45  +1.80% \par Nasdaq     2399.83   +55.31  +2.36% \par S&P 500    1160.75   +23.72  +2.09% \par FTSE 100   5635.76   +79.79  +1.44% \par Xetra DAX  6215.83   +81.62  +1.33% \par CAC40      3731.93   +82.12  +2.25% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9661.59  142.83  1.50% \par Hang Seng    22914.64 275.50  1.22% \par S&P/ASX 200   4688.40  81.51  1.77% \par Taiwan Index  8287.63  87.20  1.06% \par S.Korea Kospi 1899.30  20.36  1.08% \par Shanghai Comp Closed \par Bombay SenSex 20584.68 176.97 0.87% \par Dow Future    10877.00 +13.00  +0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 2020.50  +2.00   +0.1% \par S&P Future    1156.00  +1.30   +0.1% \par Above are as of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      83.13-15      -0.07% \par Range        83.26   83.08 \par EUR/USD      1.3844-49    +0.07% \par Range        1.3858  1.3823 \par AUD/USD      0.9714-15    +0.02% \par Range        0.9722  0.9694 \par GBP/USD      1.5917-18    +0.22% \par Range        1.5917  1.5885 \par USD/CHF      0.9651-59    -0.16% \par Range        0.9673  0.9649 \par EUR/GBP      0.8696-00    -0.13% \par Range        0.8712  0.8689 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.3363-71    -0.06% \par Range        1.3384  1.3355 \par Above are as of 0350 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 10.87%/11.55% \par Day earlier: 10.95%/11.63% \par EUR/USD Vol Option Contract 12.39%/13.01% \par Day earlier: 12.14%/12.76% \par AUD/USD Vol Option Contract 13.90%/14.45% \par Day earlier: 14.05%/14.60% \par GBP/USD Vol Option Contract 1