\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Oil Price Rally Still Has More Upside-Ritterbusch\b0\par\par\uc2 1,801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 01:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] Premature to suggest top to sharp rally by oil prices, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Nymex crude for November delivery settled 41 cents higher, or 0.5%, overnight at $83.23/bbl; contract hit $84.09 intraday, highest front-month price since May. Thursday trade likely to see market consolidate before "volatility spike" on Friday when the U.S. releases non-farm payrolls data, providing latest snapshot of health of U.S. economy. "While nearby crude futures achieved and exceeded our previously mentioned upside possibility at the $84 area, a bullish momentum play remains very much alive across the complex," Ritterbusch says. Nymex crude for November delivery now up 12 cents at $83.35/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2354 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.2% at 4677.4, earlier hit 4668.3, primarily due to a fall in financials after U.S. banks lost ground overnight. Major banks down 0.5%-0.9%, while \uc2 Macquarie Group\uc2  (MQG.AU) down 0.9% and most other sectors under slight downward pressure. However, resources are up amid commodity price strength, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) up 0.8%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 0.9%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 0.6%, \uc2 OZ Minerals\uc2  (OXL.AU) up 3.5%. Expectations of renewed U.S. quantitative easing expected to limit downside potential after disappointing private sector jobs data. But some traders now expect consolidation to prevail before Friday's U.S. jobs data. "Global equities markets are drifting as they look for a lead from the U.S. Federal Reserve," says Macquarie Private Wealth associate director Martin Lakos. "I think markets will just mark time before we see the U.S. nonfarm payrolls data on Friday. We remain positive on equity markets, although they ran hard last month." Favors resources because of strength in China, and retailers as employment remains strong. Focus now turns to domestic jobs data at 0030 GMT. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2346 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,118.0 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking broadly weaker USD after weak U.S. private jobs data further stoked expectations Fed will act soon to stimulate slowing U.S. economy. "Amid anticipation for extra quantitative easing from the Fed, financial markets are in an untiring rally," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. Tips 1,108-1,120 range for day, with eyes on possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities and whether 1,110 support will hold. Adds "the next (key) support will be at 1,100, a level which hasn't been breached since the global financial crisis started." USD/KRW last below 1,100 on September 12, 2008. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to fresh 15-year low at 82.30, based on chart analysis, in global day, below overnight low of 82.75, as view strengthening Fed may take additional quantitative easing measures as ADP report overnight shows U.S. private jobs data weaker-than-expected, says Satoshi Tate, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 ; data fuel concerns that non-farm payrolls for September due Friday may also turn out weak. Tate says if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market pair may rise to 85.50, otherwise upside limited around 83.50 in global day. Says market focused on U.S. jobless claims for week ending Oct. 2 due 1230 GMT. USD/JPY last 83.00. Tate says EUR/USD biased up due to broad USD weakness, may trade 1.3830-1.4020 range vs last 1.3913. EUR/JPY may trade in 114.70-116.20 vs last 115.48.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  now expects NZD/USD at 0.75, 0.76, 0.77 on a three, six and 12-month basis vs previous view of 0.74, 0.74, 0.70. Pair last 0.7517. NZ GS Strategist Bernard Doyle says main theme underpinning change is USD weakness and is "part of a broader set of changes to our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts that recognise the weaker growth signals currently emanating from the U.S. and the likelihood that the U.S. FOMC will move into a second phase of quantitative easing (QE2) when it meets on November 2." On NZD/AUD, says "ongoing divergence in growth momentum between NZ and Australia, which is flowing through to a widening interest rate differential between the two economies," and points to possibility of pair moving lower than previously expected. Now expects 0.75, 0.74, 0.73 on three, six, 12-month horizon vs previous view of 0.84, 0.84, 0.81. Pair last 0.7696. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +38.7% at HK$5.42 billion yesterday, though percentage of total market volume remained benign at 5.1% vs Tuesday's 4.9%, suggesting bears remained deterred by market strength, with HSI rising for 20th session out of 24. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) led with HK$553.55 million as stock succumbed to profit-taking, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$456.07 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$238.26 million. Cnooc (0883.HK), HKEx (0388.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$207.58 million, HK$166.88 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after setting fresh 5-month high $84.09/bbl yesterday, provided support at yesterday's $82.29 low doesn't break, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as 5-day & 15-day moving averages still rising; meanwhile, MACD & stochastics bullish, but with latter overbought. Rise above $84.09 resistance would open way up to psychological round-numbered levels of $85.00, $86.00, $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). But fall below $82.29 support would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to $81.15 (Tuesday's low), then $80.77 (Monday's low), $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.04 (previous cap set Sept. 13) and $77.84 (200-day moving average). November crude last up 8 cents at $83.31/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may rise to 0.875% vs Wednesday's 0.840% as investors could start to sell cash bonds to lighten positions ahead of 10-year JGB tender later in day, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Despite upturn in U.S. Treasurys, thinks 10-year JGB tender may not go smoothly as expected 0.8% or 0.9% coupon may not lure investors into notes, in turn pushing 10-year yield higher in secondary market. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  slated to sell Y2.2 trillion worth of 10-year bonds; auction results due 0345 GMT. Lead December JGB future may hover in 143.85-144.20 range vs Wednesday's 144.15. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 6.8 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 2nd consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 23.6 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 16.8 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2314 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may move in range of 9550-9750 after ending up 1.8% at 9691.43 on Wednesday, while exporters with exposure with USD/JPY may weaken as pair trades below 83 (now 82.98, after hitting 15-year low overnight). Commodity stocks like trading houses may outperform on rising gold futures, but investors may not want to take large positions ahead of U.S. jobs data due out Friday, analysts say. "Volume was impressive yesterday (2.88 billion shares on TSE 1st section, highest since May 7), but investors may stay on the sidelines today," says Yumi Nishimura, senior market analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Exporters such as Canon (7751.TO), \uc2 Mazda\uc2  (7261.TO) may outperform on their exposure to Europe with EUR/JPY higher (now 115.50). Nikkei 225 December futures ended yesterday's Chicago at 9670 vs Osaka close of 9700. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 treading water, down 0.1% at 3231.84, as markets globally wait for U.S. non farms payrolls data due later in week, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Belinda Stanley; "There is not a lot of movement in stocks, the market is just taking a bit of a breather." Adds market focused on strength of NZD/USD; "The high New Zealand dollar is having an impact on export stocks." Fisher & Paykel Healthcare down 1.0% at NZ$3.09, Cavalier down 0.7% at NZ$2.93. Michael Hill International +1.5% at NZ$0.69 as company announced it experienced a good 1Q of trade in all markets against last year's results. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals will again today be under major influence of USD, with an ICE Dollar Index, which tracks USD against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , overnight trading at its lowest level since January. Triland Metals says copper supported by weak USD and a perception that LME week, which starts Monday, will show a generally bullish sentiment; says Friday's reopening in China will highlight sentiment as last Shanghai Futures Exchange $280 under LME. As for aluminum, Triland sees "good room for further progression but participants may want to turn more cautious ahead of Friday's U.S. employment report," adds that situation where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets support for metal prices isn't likely to change before Friday. As for zinc, some selling seen in belief prices getting overdone on upside, though price still mostly driven higher by USD weakness, it says. Major market participant that doesn't want to be identified notes expectations that demand will continue to gain traction for base metals. LME 3-month copper rose $83 on day yesterday to $8,259/metric ton, tin settled $350 higher at $26,250, nickel added $79 to $24,800, lead edged $5 higher at $2,316, zinc rose $22 at $2,334 but aluminum lost $14.50 at $2,365.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 06, 2010 19:59 ET (23:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101006e6a6005uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
