\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: HSI +1.1%; 23,099 Eyed As Strong Cap N/T - SHKF\b0\par\par\uc2 1,720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 06:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.1% at 22,884.65 midday, off early high of 23,023.13, marking highest level since 23,099 touched Nov. 18, 2009, supported by gains in U.S. markets. "Trading sentiment remains strong as funds continue to favor equity markets on expectation of possible further quantitative easing in the U.S.," says Daniel So at Sun Hung Kai Financial. Tips 23,099 as strong cap near-term and suggests investors reduce holdings with index approaching that level. HK property stocks strong supported by strong home sales over long weekend, says So, with Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +1.4% at HK$116.90, New World Development (0017.HK) +1.9% at HK$16.22. Upstream oil play Cnooc (0883.HK) +2.3% at HK$16.26. after crude futures settled at 5-month high overnight. Volume strong at HK$62.27 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks remain in demand, underpinned by gains across Asian markets after Wall Street's overnight rally. STI +0.7% at 3183.32 midday, faces immediate resistance at 3200. Gainers outnumber decliners by more than 2 to 1 in broad market, with overall volume moderate. "The market is holding up well. The underlying trend is definitely positive in the near term. If the third-quarter earnings come in above expectations and guidance is bullish, we could be looking at another strong month," says dealer at local brokerage. Key gainers among blue chips include \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG), +4.1% at S$4.29, NOL (N03.SG), +3.5% at S$2.10, \uc2 Hongkong Land\uc2  (H78.SG), +2.3% at US$6.57. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +1.5% at 9664.88, off intraday high of 9685.47, on continuing heavy volume (over 2.0 billion shares traded thus far on TSE 1st section). "The global competition for monetary easing will likely trigger a mini-bubble from excess liquidity in the markets," says Takashi Ushio, general manager at \uc2 Marusan Securities\uc2 . "The BOJ now seems to have an increasing number of new cards at hand by rolling out the fresh measures," he adds, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has leeway to expand in future its recently announced Y5 trillion fund to buy assets. Pegs near-term Nikkei resistance at 9750, noting concerns about firmer yen ahead of U.S. jobs data due Friday and upcoming string of U.S. corporate earnings. 29/33 Topix subindexes higher; brokerages outperform on BOJ easing; \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO) up 5.9% at Y431. \uc2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \uc2 up 6.0% at Y123 on short-covering following FSA official's denial of Bloomberg report of possible tighter capital regulations for Japanese banks. Real estate shares maintain sectoral gains, \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  (7201.TO) off 2.1% at Y733 on strong yen. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0430 GMT misstated the name of the brokerage in the headline. The correct version follows:) Standard Chartered expects USD/HKD to remain low near 7.7600 in coming quarters as CNY developments look set to be persistently constructive factors for HKD. Says freely convertible HKD often traded as proxy for CNY, while pipeline of new IPOs likely to draw capital inflows into HK. Adds, 3-month Hibor and USD Libor fixes roughly equal and Fed unlikely to tighten "anytime soon", interest rate influences unlikely to hurt HKD in near term. "The Hong Kong (and China) growth story is likely to remain compelling to global investors, offering fundamental support to capital inflows if one looks beyond short-term gyrations in the equity and debt markets," house says. USD/HKD last at 7.7567 vs 7.7583 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares climb to fresh closing high, supported by overnight advance of U.S. equities, favorable inflation number last month. Main gauge PSEi ends up 0.6% at 4196.73 on heavy trade. "Benign inflation number increases the likelihood that interest rates will remain low, which is favorable for the stock market but the main reason is the U.S. (rally)," says April Tan, \uc2 CitisecOnline.com\uc2  research head. Tips some investors may take profit following recent advances, next support 4074; resistance 4250. Leading market advance are Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH) +4.8% at PHP4.11, Energy Development (EDC.PH) +1.1% at PHP6.24, Megaworld (MEG.PH) +2.5% at PHP2.42. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options higher at 10.85%/11.55% vs 10.80%/11.50% Tuesday in NY, as spot falls toward 82.87, level hit just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September 15, increasing demand for downside hedges. Options dealer at major brokerage in Tokyo says vols unlikely to rise sharply as long as spot stays above 83.00. Still, if U.S. ADP employment report for September due 1215 GMT turns out to be worse than expected and spot falls below 15-year low of 82.50, vols may rise to 11.50%. Median forecast of economists polled by Dow Jones is for ADP report to show 20,000 private jobs added vs 10,000 shed in August. USD/JPY last at 83.19. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered expects USD/HKD to remain low near 7.7600 in coming quarters as CNY developments look set to be persistently constructive factors for HKD. Says freely convertible HKD often traded as proxy for CNY, while pipeline of new IPOS likely to draw capital inflows into HK. Adds, 3-month Hibor and USD Libor fixes roughly equal and Fed unlikely to tighten "anytime soon", interest rate influences unlikely to hurt HKD in near term. "The Hong Kong (and China) growth story is likely to remain compelling to global investors, offering fundamental support to capital inflows if one looks beyond short-term gyrations in the equity and debt markets," house says. USD/HKD last at 7.7567 vs 7.7583 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds rise further as offshore capital continues to flow in, pulling USD/THB under 30.00 mark for first time since July 1997. Foreign players buying selectively in benchmark 5-year tenors and those in far-end of curve. "After the recent steep fall in yields of medium-term notes, investors are after longer-term bonds, which are likely to offer better return," says trader. MOF sold THB8 billion June 2023 bonds at weighted average accepted yield of 3.1811% with bid coverage of 1.79X. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.46%/2.43% vs 2.47%/2.43% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.62%/2.58% vs 2.61%/2.58%, June 2019 at 2.78%/2.75% vs 2.77%/2.75%, June 2023 at 3.15%/3.12% vs 3.20% bid/no offer. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan exports likely continued to expand strongly in September, driven by holiday demand in China, U.S.; exports tipped +26.5% on year, according to median forecast in Dow Jones Poll of 12 economists, vs +26.6% in August. Despite September's strong growth, "a weaker new export orders reading in both August's and September's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Taiwan PMI" gives reason to be "less upbeat" about the numbers, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Donnah Kwok. Though global demand for electronic goods may slow in coming months, rise in China wages, steady U.S. recovery should drive exports up next year, says Nomura's Tomo Kinoshita. Data due Thursday 0800 GMT. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +2.2% at 460.72, as investors, led by foreigners, continue buying on hopes banks will soon lower lending costs after Vietnam Banking Association's call for lower interest rates from mid-October. "Optimism is coming back as more players are buying on faith that the government will have to support the economy, and one high priority is to cut down \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs," says fund management analyst. Volume thin with 38.3 million shares valued at VND1.05 trillion traded. Among actives, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends +0.6% at VND16,900, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends +4.8% at VND17,400, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends +3.9% at VND32,000. Expects more gains tomorrow with key index in 462-467 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD extends sharp drop to 30.829 midday vs 31.252 Tuesday in heavy volume, touching fresh 2-year low 30.801 earlier, weighed by heavy foreign fund inflows amid gains in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Taiex's 1.0% gain, says foreign bank trader. Tips first support at 30.800, second at 30.600. Expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow pair's drop in late session as it's usually suspected of doing, but says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not try to reverse downward trend for now, as "the rise of New Taiwan dollar has lagged its regional peers," such as KRW. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad higher as demand for bonds holds steady as market backpedaled from knee-jerk negative reaction to RBI deputy governor Gokarn's comments mid-session yesterday; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.15 vs 99.06 last close. "On closer reading, the comments don't seem quite as hawkish as people thought initially, which means there's probably more bad news priced into bonds than there should be," notes trader. Expects bonds to trade firm in 99.00-99.30 band in session. But speculation surrounding Nov. 2 policy meeting could stall any early rally. Gokarn said Tuesday that inflation remained matter of concern, rate of inflation remained above RBI comfort zone. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 06, 2010 00:47 ET (04:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101006e6a6000ik\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
