ter.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds rise further as offshore capital continues to flow in, pulling USD/THB under 30.00 mark for first time since July 1997. Foreign players buying selectively in benchmark 5-year tenors and those in far-end of curve. "After the recent steep fall in yields of medium-term notes, investors are after longer-term bonds, which are likely to offer better return," says trader. MOF sold THB8 billion June 2023 bonds at weighted average accepted yield of 3.1811% with bid coverage of 1.79X. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.46%/2.43% vs 2.47%/2.43% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.62%/2.58% vs 2.61%/2.58%, June 2019 at 2.78%/2.75% vs 2.77%/2.75%, June 2023 at 3.15%/3.12% vs 3.20% bid/no offer. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan exports likely continued to expand strongly in September, driven by holiday demand in China, U.S.; exports tipped +26.5% on year, according to median forecast in Dow Jones Poll of 12 economists, vs +26.6% in August. Despite September's strong growth, "a weaker new export orders reading in both August's and September's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Taiwan PMI" gives reason to be "less upbeat" about the numbers, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Donnah Kwok. Though global demand for electronic goods may slow in coming months, rise in China wages, steady U.S. recovery should drive exports up next year, says Nomura's Tomo Kinoshita. Data due Thursday 0800 GMT. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +2.2% at 460.72, as investors, led by foreigners, continue buying on hopes banks will soon lower lending costs after Vietnam Banking Association's call for lower interest rates from mid-October. "Optimism is coming back as more players are buying on faith that the government will have to support the economy, and one high priority is to cut down \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight