\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/HKD To Stay Low Near 7.76 In Coming Qtrs-\uc2 HSBC\b0\par\par\uc2 3,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 06:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered expects USD/HKD to remain low near 7.7600 in coming quarters as CNY developments look set to be persistently constructive factors for HKD. Says freely convertible HKD often traded as proxy for CNY, while pipeline of new IPOs likely to draw capital inflows into HK. Adds, 3-month Hibor and USD Libor fixes roughly equal and Fed unlikely to tighten "anytime soon", interest rate influences unlikely to hurt HKD in near term. "The Hong Kong (and China) growth story is likely to remain compelling to global investors, offering fundamental support to capital inflows if one looks beyond short-term gyrations in the equity and debt markets," house says. USD/HKD last at 7.7567 vs 7.7583 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Corrected October 6, 2010 00:40 ET (04:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds rise further as offshore capital continues to flow in, pulling USD/THB under 30.00 mark for first time since July 1997. Foreign players buying selectively in benchmark 5-year tenors and those in far-end of curve. "After the recent steep fall in yields of medium-term notes, investors are after longer-term bonds, which are likely to offer better return," says trader. MOF sold THB8 billion June 2023 bonds at weighted average accepted yield of 3.1811% with bid coverage of 1.79X. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.46%/2.43% vs 2.47%/2.43% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.62%/2.58% vs 2.61%/2.58%, June 2019 at 2.78%/2.75% vs 2.77%/2.75%, June 2023 at 3.15%/3.12% vs 3.20% bid/no offer. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan exports likely continued to expand strongly in September, driven by holiday demand in China, U.S.; exports tipped +26.5% on year, according to median forecast in Dow Jones Poll of 12 economists, vs +26.6% in August. Despite September's strong growth, "a weaker new export orders reading in both August's and September's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Taiwan PMI" gives reason to be "less upbeat" about the numbers, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Donnah Kwok. Though global demand for electronic goods may slow in coming months, rise in China wages, steady U.S. recovery should drive exports up next year, says Nomura's Tomo Kinoshita. Data due Thursday 0800 GMT. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +2.2% at 460.72, as investors, led by foreigners, continue buying on hopes banks will soon lower lending costs after Vietnam Banking Association's call for lower interest rates from mid-October. "Optimism is coming back as more players are buying on faith that the government will have to support the economy, and one high priority is to cut down \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs," says fund management analyst. Volume thin with 38.3 million shares valued at VND1.05 trillion traded. Among actives, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends +0.6% at VND16,900, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends +4.8% at VND17,400, Ocean \uc2 Group\uc2  (OGC.VH) ends +3.9% at VND32,000. Expects more gains tomorrow with key index in 462-467 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD extends sharp drop to 30.829 midday vs 31.252 Tuesday in heavy volume, touching fresh 2-year low 30.801 earlier, weighed by heavy foreign fund inflows amid gains in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Taiex's 1.0% gain, says foreign bank trader. Tips first support at 30.800, second at 30.600. Expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow pair's drop in late session as it's usually suspected of doing, but says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not try to reverse downward trend for now, as "the rise of New Taiwan dollar has lagged its regional peers," such as KRW. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad higher as demand for bonds holds steady as market backpedaled from knee-jerk negative reaction to RBI deputy governor Gokarn's comments mid-session yesterday; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.15 vs 99.06 last close. "On closer reading, the comments don't seem quite as hawkish as people thought initially, which means there's probably more bad news priced into bonds than there should be," notes trader. Expects bonds to trade firm in 99.00-99.30 band in session. But speculation surrounding Nov. 2 policy meeting could stall any early rally. Gokarn said Tuesday that inflation remained matter of concern, rate of inflation remained above RBI comfort zone. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB falls below 30.00 for first time since July 1997 as exporters, gold traders sell, while portfolio inflows continue to support local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair last at 29.97 vs 30.07-30.09 earlier, 30.14 late yesterday. BOT was suspected of buying from 30.08 this morning, say dealers; think \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have bought at least a few hundred million dollars. "Since the THB30.00 support is broken, exporters are expected to sell before the pair falls further," says one dealer. Next support tipped at 29.90. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.9% at 20,630.92 in early trade, supported by gains in regional markets. Market breadth positive with all 30 index issues up; Hindalco (500440.BY) leading gains, last +2.1% at INR207.30. "The overnight rally in the U.S. has helped markets to open gap up. Metals and technology shares are witnessing fair amount of buying interest," says local dealer. Still, advises investors to partly book profits as markets look expensive at current levels. Tips 20,670 as resistance. Sterlite (500900.BY) +1.8% at INR173.85. HDFC (500010.BY) +1.1% at INR749, Larsen (500510.BY) +1.0% at INR2,074.40. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) +1.1% at INR1,034.20. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says Thailand's GDP likely to grow 7.9% in 2010, 5.3% in 2011. For 2H, external demand likely to be less robust than before though shipments to Asian counterparts may provide some buffer against slowdown in exports to Europe, U.S. "The shifts in the main growth drivers from external to internal and from government to private hands mean that a strong economic performance should still be possible," says house. Sets 3Q, 4Q GDP growth target at 7.1%, 3.8%, respectively. Such robust growth, however, implies still-low inflation on uptick, although stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps limit imported inflation to some extent. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  maintains view BOT to raise policy rate one more time this year by 25 bps to 2.0% at Oct. 20 meeting. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall below 80.00 vs last 83.13 if investors confirm \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 82.90, level at which Finance Ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, as investors believe upcoming U.S. economic indicators may turn out to be weak and expected Fed to ease policy very soon, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  senior dealer Tsutomu Soma. Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  difficult ahead of G-7 meeting this weekend because pace of current USD/JPY decline moderate, making it difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to fix excessive moves. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR opens lower as upbeat local stocks, weak global USD help boost INR against USD, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.40 vs 44.67 late Tuesday. "The bias is downward for the pair, but the market will remain wary of the key support zone at 44.15-44.25," says trader. But breach of this support zone likely to trigger correction to 43.80, with inflows related to massive $3 billlion-$3.5 billion share sale of \uc2 Coal India\uc2  lined up for this month, correction may just be a matter of time, adds trader. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0348 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY lower despite BOJ's aggressive monetary easing Tuesday as investors turn attention to U.S. amid growing expectations of quantitative easing from Fed, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior analyst Sumino Kamei. "Financial markets took the BOJ's step positively, but after all, investors have a larger weight on the U.S. monetary policy outlook to trade the dollar against the yen," she says. Pair last at 83.13, may fall further; next important point 82.87. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 10-06-10 0030ET ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0430 GMT misstated the name of the brokerage in the headline. The correct version follows:) Standard Chartered expects USD/HKD to remain low near 7.7600 in coming quarters as CNY developments look set to be persistently constructive factors for HKD. Says freely convertible HKD often traded as proxy for CNY, while pipeline of new IPOs likely to draw capital inflows into HK. Adds, 3-month Hibor and USD Libor fixes roughly equal and Fed unlikely to tighten "anytime soon", interest rate influences unlikely to hurt HKD in near term. "The Hong Kong (and China) growth story is 