\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Falls Below 30.00; Next Support 29.90\b0\par\par\uc2 1,498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 06:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB falls below 30.00 for first time since July 1997 as exporters, gold traders sell, while portfolio inflows continue to support local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Pair last at 29.97 vs 30.07-30.09 earlier, 30.14 late yesterday. BOT was suspected of buying from 30.08 this morning, say dealers; think \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have bought at least a few hundred million dollars. "Since the THB30.00 support is broken, exporters are expected to sell before the pair falls further," says one dealer. Next support tipped at 29.90. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.9% at 20,630.92 in early trade, supported by gains in regional markets. Market breadth positive with all 30 index issues up; Hindalco (500440.BY) leading gains, last +2.1% at INR207.30. "The overnight rally in the U.S. has helped markets to open gap up. Metals and technology shares are witnessing fair amount of buying interest," says local dealer. Still, advises investors to partly book profits as markets look expensive at current levels. Tips 20,670 as resistance. Sterlite (500900.BY) +1.8% at INR173.85. HDFC (500010.BY) +1.1% at INR749, Larsen (500510.BY) +1.0% at INR2,074.40. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) +1.1% at INR1,034.20. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says Thailand's GDP likely to grow 7.9% in 2010, 5.3% in 2011. For 2H, external demand likely to be less robust than before though shipments to Asian counterparts may provide some buffer against slowdown in exports to Europe, U.S. "The shifts in the main growth drivers from external to internal and from government to private hands mean that a strong economic performance should still be possible," says house. Sets 3Q, 4Q GDP growth target at 7.1%, 3.8%, respectively. Such robust growth, however, implies still-low inflation on uptick, although stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps limit imported inflation to some extent. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  maintains view BOT to raise policy rate one more time this year by 25 bps to 2.0% at Oct. 20 meeting. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall below 80.00 vs last 83.13 if investors confirm \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 82.90, level at which Finance Ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, as investors believe upcoming U.S. economic indicators may turn out to be weak and expected Fed to ease policy very soon, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  senior dealer Tsutomu Soma. Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  difficult ahead of G-7 meeting this weekend because pace of current USD/JPY decline moderate, making it difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to fix excessive moves. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR opens lower as upbeat local stocks, weak global USD help boost INR against USD, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.40 vs 44.67 late Tuesday. "The bias is downward for the pair, but the market will remain wary of the key support zone at 44.15-44.25," says trader. But breach of this support zone likely to trigger correction to 43.80, with inflows related to massive $3 billlion-$3.5 billion share sale of \uc2 Coal India\uc2  lined up for this month, correction may just be a matter of time, adds trader. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0348 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY lower despite BOJ's aggressive monetary easing Tuesday as investors turn attention to U.S. amid growing expectations of quantitative easing from Fed, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior analyst Sumino Kamei. "Financial markets took the BOJ's step positively, but after all, investors have a larger weight on the U.S. monetary policy outlook to trade the dollar against the yen," she says. Pair last at 83.13, may fall further; next important point 82.87. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] Surge in global liquidity now underway is likely to fuel the next major asset bubble, which is likely to be already forming, says AMP head of investment strategy and chief economist Shane Oliver. "Potential candidates are gold and commodity prices, emerging markets and resources shares. But the absence of obvious overvaluation suggests we are still on the foothills of the next bubble, which likely has years to run, with plenty of opportunities for investors in these asset classes in the interim." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea moving ahead with trade pacts with European Union and U.S. For Japan, those two account for over one-fourth of export value. Korea has agreements in effect or signed with nations making up 36% of trade volume. That's only 17% for Japan; Talks with Brussels and Washington aren't on radar. Seoul's E.U. agreement, set to take effect July, would initially eliminate almost 80% of E.U. duties on Korean exports. Don't need but Korean competitors like \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) and \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE), already with strong brands and weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tailwind, to gain another advantage. In autos, means $25,000 Korean car won't cost a euro more in E.U. (aside from local taxes), while Japanese car would see extra $2,500 because of 10% tariff. Japan car exports and even those making cars in E.U. to feel pinch, Japan Automobile Manufacturers' Association executive says. \uc2 Mazda Motor\uc2  (7261.TO), with one-fifth unit sales from Europe, stands to lose most. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) gets more than 10% from region, while \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) gets about one-quarter of revenue from Europe. See: "=HEARD ON THE STREET: Seoul Is Outmaneuvering Tokyo -- Again" (james.simms@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippines likely to keep monetary policy rates steady for some time following lower-than-expected September inflation number, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "Short of a major spike in commodity prices or a food price shock, inflation seems to be on course to remain the in the 3%-5% range through the course of this year and the next. BSP can leave monetary policy unchanged for some time to come, as a result," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says in research note. Slower 3.5% headline inflation last month due largely to favorable food supply side conditions and ample government stocks, it notes. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting lower after failing to break through 0.7500 level overnight but dips remain well supported, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  NZ chief manager Daniel Brdanovic; "It will remain well bid on the back a weak U.S. dollar... The bigger picture is the market is looking at quantitative easing." NZD/USD at 0.7467 vs 0.7494 early this morning. Brdanovic adds, going forward market likely to test previous cycle high of 0.7635 seen in October 2009; "That's the target for the market at the moment." Tips support in range of 0.7355-0.7360 with resistance coming in at 0.7630-0.7635. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.5% at 973.89, propelled by gains in U.S. stocks overnight, regional bourses so far. Big cap energy, banking plays dominating activities. "Volatility in the Thai stock market is expected to be high as the SET index is marching towards the 1,000 psychological level again," says Phillip Securities assistant vice president Teerada Chanyingyong. Gains likely capped as investors in more cautious mood after foreigners turn net sellers of THB2.24 billion of shares yesterday, snapping 11-day net buying streak. Resistance tipped at 978, support at 961. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.3% at THB299, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.3% at THB162, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) +1.5% at THB105, Banpu (BANPU.TH) off 0.3% at THB720, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) +2.2% at THB118.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open lower as heavy foreign fund inflows expected for initial share sales beginning today likely to pressure USD, says trader with foreign bank; tips pair to open at around 44.35-44.38 vs 44.67 late yesterday, trade in 44.25-44.50 band in session. "We're expecting the share sales to draw heavy inflows, most of which should come in today." But market already positioned lightly on USD, key support for pair at 44.20 unlikely to be tested this session. Foreign funds net buyers of a provisional INR6.71 billion worth of stocks yesterday. 1-month NDFs at 44.44/44.51. INR10.29 billion IPO for \uc2 Oberoi Realty\uc2  opens this session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com) [ 10-06-10 0007ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101006e6a60002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
