ards the 1,000 psychological level again," says Phillip Securities assistant vice president Teerada Chanyingyong. Gains likely capped as investors in more cautious mood after foreigners turn net sellers of THB2.24 billion of shares yesterday, snapping 11-day net buying streak. Resistance tipped at 978, support at 961. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.3% at THB299, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.3% at THB162, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) +1.5% at THB105, Banpu (BANPU.TH) off 0.3% at THB720, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) +2.2% at THB118.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open lower as heavy foreign fund inflows expected for initial share sales beginning today likely to pressure USD, says trader with foreign bank; tips pair to open at around 44.35-44.38 vs 44.67 late yesterday, trade in 44.25-44.50 band in session. "We're expecting the share sales to draw heavy inflows, most of which should come in today." But market already positioned lightly on USD, key support for pair at 44.20 unlikely to be tested this session. Foreign funds net buyers of a provisional INR6.71 billion worth of stocks yesterday. 1-month NDFs at 44.44/44.51. INR10.29 billion IPO for \uc2 Oberoi Realty\uc2  opens this session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open higher by about 0.7%-0.9%, buoyed by gains across Asia following overnight rally on Wall Street (DJIA ended +1.8%); funds expected to buy blue chips ahead of July-September earnings season starting mid-October, says local dealer. Adds, strong liquidity on sustained foreign fund inflows will also lend support. Says metals, capital goods shares may lead gains. Still, thinks index may come off highs later as traders cash out given expensive valuations in most index stocks after about 14% rally since Sept. 1. Tips resistance at 20,700. Sensex ended down 0.3% at 20,407.71 Tuesday. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.2% at 3598.95 in moderate volume, boosted by rally on Wall Street, firmer IDR, traders say, tip resistance at 3650. But index off earlier high of 3629.421; "Swift profit taking by most local funds pulled the main index from its highs," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) +4.0% at IDR3,225, toll road operator \uc2 Jasa Marga\uc2  (JSMR.JK) +3.1% at IDR3,375. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sustains fall, at 1,120.9 vs 1,130.7 late yesterday in Seoul on selling by offshore players amid sharp Kospi gains (+1.0%), but further downside limited due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 3 Seoul-based traders suspect BOK of buying USD/KRW near 1,120 since early morning. Market quiet for now after initial slide near market open, but pair may head for lower levels in afternoon on foreign equity fund inflows, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,115-1,123 range. Despite suspected BOK smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD, "it'll be hard for the pair to stage a rebound given the strong dollar-selling interest from offshore players. I'm just watching to see if the 1,120 level is going to give way," says foreign bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 2355ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101006e6a60002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
