\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Gold, Silver Hit New Highs After Overnight Rally\b0\par\par\uc2 1,508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 03:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold hits new record high at $1,344.20/oz in early Asian trade, last $1,342.60/oz, up $2.00 since NY close. Spot silver hits new 30-year high of $22.94/oz; now at $22.88/oz, up 1 cent. Goldcore says Bank of Japan's pledge to pump more funds into the struggling economy, keep rates "virtually at zero" was aggressive move. "This surprised markets and given that the Federal Reserve and other central banks look set to soon provide fresh injections of economic stimulus is leading to demand for gold as a store of wealth," says broker. Both metals in blue sky territory technically so no obvious resistance levels with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  identifying $1,377 as trend line target for gold. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals all higher in early Asia although buying interest fairly light, reflecting ongoing China holidays; illiquid nature of LME Select trade in absence of Chinese participants means could have to wait until after 0600 GMT before any real fireworks. LME 3-month copper at $8,240/ton, up $64 since yesterday's kerb with 193 lots done, just off $8,244 (26-month high) hit earlier, after rallying 1.5% yesterday. "It seemed to be investment funds rather than consumers that contributed to the afternoon copper price run," says Sucden Financial. Better than expected U.S. ISM non-manufacturing data and bullish equity markets all helped in aftermath of BOJ's surprise easing move yesterday. LME options declaration later could see volatility for nickel, which trading close to popular $25,000 strike; LME 3-month nickel at $24,850/ton, up $135 with 70 lots done, at 5-month high. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.7% at 1481.81, highest in 33 months, boosted by rally in U.S. stocks overnight; dealers say having breached key 1480 resistance, benchmark index poised to extend gains near-term. "We expect the KLCI to gradually rise toward the 1500 psychological level within the next few days," dealer says. Broad market higher, with gains across all sectors. Among blue-chip gainers, Tenaga (5347.KU) +1.1% at MYR8.94, Axiata (6888.KU) +1.6% at MYR4.50. MMC (2194.KU) +1.0% at MYR3.18 after government said it will consider company's bid to buy state-owned UEM \uc2 Group\uc2 . Among decliners, MAS (3786.KU) down 0.4% at MYR2.35 on profit-taking after recent strong gains. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely up, may hit fresh 14-year-high as stronger overseas bourses buoy sentiment. Benchmark biased to reach 1,000 sometime this week; although foreign investors were net sellers yesterday, they haven't exited from Thailand; still-bearish USD view likely to continue attracting fund inflows, says KT Zmico Securities' First VP Charoen Iamphattanatham. "The question is, what's next when the SET Index hits 1,000? So, the volatility will be increasingly high when the benchmark approaches such level," he says. Expects energy heavyweights to lead. Initial resistance put at 986, support at 963. SET Index closed up 0.5% at 969.28 yesterday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower, tracking broad USD weakness vs EUR, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7550-7.7590 band near-term. "The upcoming IPOs will likely boost demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , putting downward pressure on the pair. We are also selling the U.S. dollar on behalf of our clients," trader says. Notes AIA \uc2 Group\uc2 , which is set to debut on Hong Kong bourse Oct. 29, seeking to raise up to US$17.1 billion. Pair last at 7.7565 vs 7.7583 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds, bond futures tad up amid ebbing market concerns over a BOK rate hike next week following BOJ's surprise easing move yesterday, and expectations for more quantitative easing from Fed. But sharp price gains limited for now with yields already hovering at lowest closing levels for 2010, analysts say; 3-year yield quoted down 1 bp at 3.28%, 5-year down 1 bp at 3.58%, December futures +3 ticks at 112.79. "With more quantitative easing from key economies, increased global market liquidity, coupled with relatively solid domestic recovery prospects and a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely attract more foreign investors to local equities and bonds," says \uc2 Daishin Securities\uc2  analyst Jeong Im-bo. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares staging broadly on back of Wall Street's overnight rally, pushing STI +1.0% at 3192.47. If 3200 fails to hold as resistance, next resistance tipped at 3214 (June 2 high). "Technically, the MACD indicator has initiated a clear rebound off its signal line over the past few sessions and this continues to suggest improving upside momentum going forward," says OCBC Investment Research analyst Philip Teo. Market breadth at 12 gainers for every decliner. \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) top percentage gainer among blue chips, +5.1% at S$4.33, as company secures contracts, options for nearly US$1 billion worth of jack-up rigs. Most active stocks in market comprise good mix of large, mid, small caps, including Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +1.4% at S$2.11, Yongnam (Y02.SG), +3.2% at S$0.32, Valuetronics (BN2.SG), +4.0% at S$0.26. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI likely to rise to fresh 2010 high, bolstered by gains in U.S. stocks. If ends day in positive territory, would mark 20th session of rise out of 24, amid HK market's liquidity-driven rally, with "a gush of liquidity" likely to bring HSI to 26,000 in 4Q10, according to CCB International; notes this underpinned by attractive stock valuations (HSI at 12X 2011 PER, 3.4% dividend yield), a market reconciled to period of prolonged low interest rates, easing concerns China will tighten credit. HKEx (0388.HK) despite already rising 31.0% since September, remains prime beneficiary of ample liquidity. HSI +0.1% at 22,639.14 yesterday; next target likely November 2009 peak of 23,099. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares likely to advance, tracking Wall Street's overnight rally, while lower inflation in September expected to lift sentiment. Bellwether PSEi may test 4200 resistance after ending down 0.4% Tuesday at 4171.87. "Given yesterday's consolidation, the market is poised to regain some strength... We see a lot of activity from foreign investors and we think that this will continue," says Ron Rodrigo, research head with DBP-Daiwa Securities; next support at 4150. Reckons property plays, banks, consumer-related stocks likely to lead gains. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.07-30.09 vs 30.14 late yesterday, tracking weakening USD vs Asians as market increasingly convinced that Fed likely to engage in another round of easing. BOT suspected to have bought USD to curb baht rise, defending at 30.06-30.07 levels, dealers say. They add \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to do what it takes to keep pair around 30.00 level today. "(The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is unlikely to let the dollar slip more than 20 satang a day," dealer says; adds firmer commodity prices, especially gold, likely to prompt gold traders to sell USD. Another