rns over TWD's strength continue to hurt tech shares. "Although the Taiex is expected to rise today, we see prices coming down in the afternoon on worries about tech shares due to the Taiwan dollar's appreciation," says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Still, expects construction shares to outperform, supported by ample liquidity, capital inflows. Taiex ended off 0.6% at 8200.43 yesterday, tips 8200-8300 range. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to move lower in line with broad USD weakness vs regionals, expected equity fund flows, overseas Filipino workers' remittances. "The market might try to test the 43.60 support today, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be guarding this level," says local bank trader. Pair at 43.76 late yesterday. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW takes out 1,130 support at open, now testing 1,120, with KRW tracking sharp overnight gains in EUR, while Kospi's 0.9% gain supports local unit. Pair trading at 5-month lows, last at 1,121.7 vs 1,130.7 late Tuesday in Seoul. But further downside likely limited; 2 Seoul based traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying near 1,120. Selling pressure strong as banks liquidate long positions, offshore players sell actively, "but its not going to be an easy way down from here, because (the authorities') seem very determined to support (the U.S. dollar)," says local bank trader. Pegs support at 1,115. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  expects Thai stock market to pause for breath in October but says liquidity could give stocks another leg-up in November, December. Thinks jitters in this month include decision on Democrat Party dissolution case, while strong bottom-up catalysts are harder to find now than even month ago, economy should slow in coming months. Still, "we believe that the court will surprise with a positive verdict and that strong liquidity could give the market more upside despite deteriorating fundamentals." Reckons there's room for more foreign investment; notes net foreign buying year-to-date only 0.66% of market cap vs 1.09% on average for non-Japan Asia countries. House cuts exposure to stocks that retail investors have pushed to overvalued levels but maintains bluechip positions for November-December rally. Recommends selling TMB Bank (TMB.TH), \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH), \uc2 Thanachart Capital\uc2  (TCAP.TH), \uc2 Italian-Thai Development\uc2  (ITD.TH) but likes Banpu (BANPU.TH), \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH), \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH), TISCO Financial Group (TISCO.TH), Bangkok Dusit Medial Services (BGH.TH) (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.9% at 1896.02 on gains in U.S. stocks overnight, with analysts expecting benchmark index to head towards key 1900 resistance; but level may not be breached with Kospi tipped in 1880-1900 range for day. "This is not about economic fundamentals pushing the Kospi higher, but strong liquidity backed by foreign players," who're betting on higher Korean rates, stronger KRW, says \uc2 Hana Daetoo Securities\uc2 ' Suh Dong-pill. Techs rally after recent falls; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE), which will report its 3Q earnings guidance tomorrow, +0.9% at KRW781,000, while LG Display (034220.SE) +1.7% at KRW39,000; Hynix (000660.SE) +2.4% at KRW23,300. Airliners also up on stronger KRW, with \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) +0.6% at KRW78,100, \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) +1.5% at KRW9,680. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.5% at 4674.6 in mostly quiet trading, although it is grinding higher on the back of some buy programs today, with traders expecting the market to be well supported this afternoon. Key resistance remains the 4700 level, near last week's high and the 61.8% retracement of the April to May fall. "It just felt as though everyone was scrambling to strap some beta (volatility in relation to the market) on this morning," says senior institutional trader at major broker. "All the copper names are strong, all the high beta names are doing well today." Adds that expectations of renewed U.S. quantitative easing at the November FOMC meeting remains the key focus for equities globally. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +0.9% at 9601.23 following sharp overnight rise in U.S. stocks and aggressive easing steps announced by BOJ (including cut in benchmark policy rate and Y5 trillion fund to buy assets). But index resistance pegged at 9700 on lingering worries about firmer yen (USD/JPY now 83.12). "In the end, the yen did not move as hoped (especially vs the dollar)" from the BOJ's measures, says Naoteru Teraoka, general manager at Chuo Mitsui Asset Management. "It's difficult to have confidence in Japanese corporate earnings (with the yen rising)." Puts today's Nikkei range at 9500-9650. 29/33 Topix subindexes higher with real estate shares best performer on board; \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 3.1% at Y1,548, J-REIT Nippon Building Fund (8951.TO) up 4.8% at Y811,000. \uc2 Suzuki Motor\uc2  (7269.TO) up 4.2% at Y1,930 following Asahi report automaker plans to shrink auto engine production in Japan. \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  (5713.TO) up 4.6% at Y1,377 on rise in gold prices. \uc2 Hitachi Koki\uc2  (6581.TO) down 5.4% at Y697 on falsified sales. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 2126ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101006e6a600011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
