\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Down; BOT Suspected Of Curbing Baht Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 03:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.07-30.09 vs 30.14 late yesterday, tracking weakening USD vs Asians as market increasingly convinced that Fed likely to engage in another round of easing. BOT suspected to have bought USD to curb baht rise, defending at 30.06-30.07 levels, dealers say. They add \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would have to do what it takes to keep pair around 30.00 level today. "(The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) is unlikely to let the dollar slip more than 20 satang a day," dealer says; adds firmer commodity prices, especially gold, likely to prompt gold traders to sell USD. Another dealer expects exporters to intensify selling pressure later. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to fresh 15-year low below 82.87, level logged just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on September 15, if U.S. employment report for September from payroll giant \uc2 Automatic Data Processing Inc\uc2 . due 1215 GMT notably worse than expected, adding to view Fed may ease monetary policy ahead, says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Japan at Barclays Bank. Dow Jones poll tips ADP report to show 20,000 private jobs added vs 10,000 shed in August. Says USD likely to continue to slide until FOMC meeting in early November as market prices in potential quantitative easing. ICE Dollar Index, which tracks greenback vs trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sank to its lowest level since January overnight. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] MAS suspected of buying USD/SGD in past 30 minutes from 1.3090 to 1.3113, says Singapore-based dealer. Another dealer says local banks were buying strongly, think it's on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Looks like it's them for sure," he says. USD/SGD last quoted in interbank market at 1.3110-1.3115. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely higher, tracking Wall Street's gains overnight, though benchmark index unlikely to rise sharply as concerns over TWD's strength continue to hurt tech shares. "Although the Taiex is expected to rise today, we see prices coming down in the afternoon on worries about tech shares due to the Taiwan dollar's appreciation," says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Still, expects construction shares to outperform, supported by ample liquidity, capital inflows. Taiex ended off 0.6% at 8200.43 yesterday, tips 8200-8300 range. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to move lower in line with broad USD weakness vs regionals, expected equity fund flows, overseas Filipino workers' remittances. "The market might try to test the 43.60 support today, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be guarding this level," says local bank trader. Pair at 43.76 late yesterday. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW takes out 1,130 support at open, now testing 1,120, with KRW tracking sharp overnight gains in EUR, while Kospi's 0.9% gain supports local unit. Pair trading at 5-month lows, last at 1,121.7 vs 1,130.7 late Tuesday in Seoul. But further downside likely limited; 2 Seoul based traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying near 1,120. Selling pressure strong as banks liquidate long positions, offshore players sell actively, "but its not going to be an easy way down from here, because (the authorities') seem very determined to support (the U.S. dollar)," says local bank trader. Pegs support at 1,115. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  expects Thai stock market to pause for breath in October but says liquidity could give stocks another leg-up in November, December. Thinks jitters in this month include decision on Democrat Party dissolution case, while strong bottom-up catalysts are harder to find now than even month ago, economy should slow in coming months. Still, "we believe that the court will surprise with a positive verdict and that strong liquidity could give the market more upside despite deteriorating fundamentals." Reckons there's room for more foreign investment; notes net foreign buying year-to-date only 0.66% of market cap vs 1.09% on average for non-Japan Asia countries. House cuts exposure to stocks that retail investors have pushed to overvalued levels but maintains bluechip positions for November-December rally. Recommends selling TMB Bank (TMB.TH), \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH), \uc2 Thanachart Capital\uc2  (TCAP.TH), \uc2 Italian-Thai Development\uc2  (ITD.TH) but likes Banpu (BANPU.TH), \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH), \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH), TISCO Financial Group (TISCO.TH), Bangkok Dusit Medial Services (BGH.TH) (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.9% at 1896.02 on gains in U.S. stocks overnight, with analysts expecting benchmark index to head towards key 1900 resistance; but level may not be breached with Kospi tipped in 1880-1900 range for day. "This is not about economic fundamentals pushing the Kospi higher, but strong liquidity backed by foreign players," who're betting on higher Korean rates, stronger KRW, says \uc2 Hana Daetoo Securities\uc2 ' Suh Dong-pill. Techs rally after recent falls; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE), which will report its 3Q earnings guidance tomorrow, +0.9% at KRW781,000, while LG Display (034220.SE) +1.7% at KRW39,000; Hynix (000660.SE) +2.4% at KRW23,300. Airliners also up on stronger KRW, with \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) +0.6% at KRW78,100, \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) +1.5% at KRW9,680. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.5% at 4674.6 in mostly quiet trading, although it is grinding higher on the back of some buy programs today, with traders expecting the market to be well supported this afternoon. Key resistance remains the 4700 level, near last week's high and the 61.8% retracement of the April to May fall. "It just felt as though everyone was scrambling to strap some beta (volatility in relation to the market) on this morning," says senior institutional trader at major broker. "All the copper names are strong, all the high beta names are doing well today." Adds that expectations of renewed U.S. quantitative easing at the November FOMC meeting remains the key focus for equities globally. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +0.9% at 9601.23 following sharp overnight rise in U.S. stocks and aggressive easing steps announced by BOJ (including cut in benchmark policy rate and Y5 trillion fund to buy assets). But index resistance pegged at 9700 on lingering worries about firmer yen (USD/JPY now 83.12). "In the end, the yen did not move as hoped (especially vs the dollar)" from the BOJ's measures, says Naoteru Teraoka, general manager at Chuo Mitsui Asset Management. "It's difficult to have confidence in Japanese corporate earnings (with the yen rising)." Puts today's Nikkei range at 9500-9650. 29/33 Topix subindexes higher with real estate shares best performer on board; \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 3.1% at Y1,548, J-REIT Nippon Building Fund (8951.TO) up 4.8% at Y811,000. \uc2 Suzuki Motor\uc2  (7269.TO) up 4.2% at Y1,930 following Asahi report automaker plans to shrink auto engine production in Japan. \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  (5713.TO) up 4.6% at Y1,377 on rise in gold prices. \uc2 Hitachi Koki\uc2  (6581.TO) down 5.4% at Y697 on falsified sales. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely higher, taking cues from U.S. Treasurys, global macro economic concerns. Yields likely down 2-3 bps, trader at local bank says. "Bonds are attracting buyers as safe havens amid the turmoil in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets caused by fears that the global economic recovery is slowing. We expect yields to remain depressed this week," trader says; 2-year Singapore government bond yield closed down 1 bp at 0.41% yesterday, 10-year down 2 bp at 1.99%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0027 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD breaks below 1.3100 support on talk of likely Fed quantitative easing measures to prop up U.S. economy. Pair touches record low 1.3081 overnight, currently at 1.3088 vs 1.3163 late Asia yesterday when greenback boosted by BOJ's surprise announcement that it's cutting its benchmark rate to near zero. "Central banks across the world are likely to announce steps to prop up their sagging economies. This week will be all about quantitative easing and the U.S. dollar will remain weak," says trader at international bank. Tips new support for pair at 1.3070; resistance 1.3150. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 2112ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101006e6a60000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
