\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:KLCI May Rise;To Test 1480 Resistance Again-Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 02:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0027 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, supported by rally in U.S. stocks overnight; benchmark index ended +0.7% at 1472.19 yesterday. "The KLCI looks set to test the 1480 resistance again," dealer says; KLCI has failed to surpass 1480 so far this year, hitting high of 1479 twice last month. Gains expected across all sectors, with construction stocks such as Gamuda (5398.KU) likely in focus on hopes government will accelerate infrastructure spending when it unveils budget for 2011 next week. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] Outlook for nickel market for next 3-6 months still positive given slow ramp-up of new nickel projects coming next year and strong recovery of stainless steel production worldwide, especially in Asia, says Macquarie Research. But adds "there are serious concerns about the substantial rise in nickel supply from the western world from 2H 2011 onwards," with this raising pricing pressures. Return of production from Vale's (VALE, VALE5.BR) Sudbury, Voisey Bay \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Canada after year-long strikes, gradual ramp-up of production from new projects will add at least a further 170,000-180,000 tons of additional supply in 2011 compared to 2010, bank says. When combined with estimated 30,000 rise in Chinese nickel pig iron production next year, it appears there will be at least 200,000 tons of incremental supply coming into global market in 2011, house says. LME 3-month nickel eases $20 early in Asia at US$24,700/ton. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0016 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures hit 144.00 for first time in more than 7 years, may extend climbs to 144.10, supported by BOJ's ambitious monetary easing steps, says trader at Tokyo securities house. Adds, JPY rising again, so without government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, demand for JGBs could grow further amid growing uncertainty over Japan's export-reliant economy. JGB futures last up 0.29 at 143.96. Benchmark 10-year JGB yield at 0.860%, down 5 bps. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0016 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower at 3.0860 vs 3.0950 late yesterday as pair tracks weaker USD vs EUR overnight amid renewed risk appetite. Dealers say dearth of fresh market-moving developments on domestic front means USD/MYR likely to continue taking cues from performance of other major crosses; pair tipped to test 3.0800 support near term. "But whether players have enough conviction to push (the dollar/ringgit) below that level remains to be seen," dealer says; adds participants may hesitate given recent suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 3.0800. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0004 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound as investors bide time until announcement of auction of 7-year benchmark MGS expected later this week; dealers say market expectations for auction size of about MYR3.0 billion-MYR3.5 billion. Dealer says move by BOJ to effectively cut rates may provide MGS slight lift, though impact likely limited as investors await release of government's 2011 budget next week for clearer outlook for domestic economy. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end mixed in subdued trade, with May 2013 down 3 bps at 3.13%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.25%, September 2017 +2 bps at 3.59%, November 2019 flat at 3.64%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares expected to advance on Wall Street's overnight rally triggered by better-than-expected U.S. September ISM data, hopes for more economic stimulus measures from central banks worldwide. STI, +0.2% at new year-to-date high of 3162.36 yesterday, may test 3200 for first time since June 2008. "With the index continuing to show its mettle, conquering one resistance after another, there is no strong case to argue that (even higher levels) could not be met," says AmFraser Securities strategist Najeeb Jarhom; tips near-term target of 3266-3270. \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) expected to attract keen interest as company secures contracts, options for nearly US$1 billion worth of jack-up rigs; +3.3% at S$4.12 yesterday. Midas Holdings (5EN.SG) may also be in focus as China-based train parts supplier dual-lists in Hong Kong today; +1.0% at S$1.03. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 5-month high $82.99/bbl yesterday, provided support at yesterday's $81.15 low doesn't break, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as 5-day & 15-day moving averages still rising; meanwhile, MACD & stochastics bullish, but latter at overbought. Resistance at psychological $83.00; breach would expose upside to psychological round-numbered levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Fall below $81.15 support would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting $80.77 (Monday's low), then $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.04 (previous cap set September 13), $77.79 (200-day moving average), $77.55 (Thursday's low) and $76.89 (55-day moving average). November crude last down 19 cents at $82.63/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely higher, supported by Wall Street's rally overnight; analysts expect benchmark index to extend recent gains as foreign equity fund inflows continue. Kospi ended steady at 1878.94 yesterday, but tipped to rise to