rates may provide MGS slight lift, though impact likely limited as investors await release of government's 2011 budget next week for clearer outlook for domestic economy. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end mixed in subdued trade, with May 2013 down 3 bps at 3.13%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.25%, September 2017 +2 bps at 3.59%, November 2019 flat at 3.64%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares expected to advance on Wall Street's overnight rally triggered by better-than-expected U.S. September ISM data, hopes for more economic stimulus measures from central banks worldwide. STI, +0.2% at new year-to-date high of 3162.36 yesterday, may test 3200 for first time since June 2008. "With the index continuing to show its mettle, conquering one resistance after another, there is no strong case to argue that (even higher levels) could not be met," says AmFraser Securities strategist Najeeb Jarhom; tips near-term target of 3266-3270. \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) expected to attract keen interest as company secures contracts, options for nearly US$1 billion worth of jack-up rigs; +3.3% at S$4.12 yesterday. Midas Holdings (5EN.SG) may also be in focus as China-based train parts supplier dual-lists in Hong Kong today; +1.0% at S$1.03. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 5-month high $82.99/bbl yesterday, provided support at yesterday's $81.15 low doesn't break, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as 5-day & 15-day moving averages still rising; meanwhile, MACD & stochastics bullish, but latter at overbought. Resistance at psychological $83.00; breach would expose upside to psychological round-numbered levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Fall below $81.15 support would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting $80.77 (Monday's low), then $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.0