.2% at new year-to-date high of 3162.36 yesterday, may test 3200 for first time since June 2008. "With the index continuing to show its mettle, conquering one resistance after another, there is no strong case to argue that (even higher levels) could not be met," says AmFraser Securities strategist Najeeb Jarhom; tips near-term target of 3266-3270. \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) expected to attract keen interest as company secures contracts, options for nearly US$1 billion worth of jack-up rigs; +3.3% at S$4.12 yesterday. Midas Holdings (5EN.SG) may also be in focus as China-based train parts supplier dual-lists in Hong Kong today; +1.0% at S$1.03. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 5-month high $82.99/bbl yesterday, provided support at yesterday's $81.15 low doesn't break, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as 5-day & 15-day moving averages still rising; meanwhile, MACD & stochastics bullish, but latter at overbought. Resistance at psychological $83.00; breach would expose upside to psychological round-numbered levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Fall below $81.15 support would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting $80.77 (Monday's low), then $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.04 (previous cap set September 13), $77.79 (200-day moving average), $77.55 (Thursday's low) and $76.89 (55-day moving average). November crude last down 19 cents at $82.63/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely higher, supported by Wall Street's rally overnight; analysts expect benchmark index to extend recent gains as foreign equity fund inflows continue. Kospi ended steady at 1878.94 yesterday, but tipped to rise toward 1900. "As proved in the BOJ's interest rates cut and monetary easing program, the necessity of further easing measures by developed countries, such as the U.S