\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Malaysia Govt Bonds Likely In Range; Auction Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 02:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0004 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound as investors bide time until announcement of auction of 7-year benchmark MGS expected later this week; dealers say market expectations for auction size of about MYR3.0 billion-MYR3.5 billion. Dealer says move by BOJ to effectively cut rates may provide MGS slight lift, though impact likely limited as investors await release of government's 2011 budget next week for clearer outlook for domestic economy. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end mixed in subdued trade, with May 2013 down 3 bps at 3.13%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.25%, September 2017 +2 bps at 3.59%, November 2019 flat at 3.64%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares expected to advance on Wall Street's overnight rally triggered by better-than-expected U.S. September ISM data, hopes for more economic stimulus measures from central banks worldwide. STI, +0.2% at new year-to-date high of 3162.36 yesterday, may test 3200 for first time since June 2008. "With the index continuing to show its mettle, conquering one resistance after another, there is no strong case to argue that (even higher levels) could not be met," says AmFraser Securities strategist Najeeb Jarhom; tips near-term target of 3266-3270. \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) expected to attract keen interest as company secures contracts, options for nearly US$1 billion worth of jack-up rigs; +3.3% at S$4.12 yesterday. Midas Holdings (5EN.SG) may also be in focus as China-based train parts supplier dual-lists in Hong Kong today; +1.0% at S$1.03. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 5-month high $82.99/bbl yesterday, provided support at yesterday's $81.15 low doesn't break, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as 5-day & 15-day moving averages still rising; meanwhile, MACD & stochastics bullish, but latter at overbought. Resistance at psychological $83.00; breach would expose upside to psychological round-numbered levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Fall below $81.15 support would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting $80.77 (Monday's low), then $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.04 (previous cap set September 13), $77.79 (200-day moving average), $77.55 (Thursday's low) and $76.89 (55-day moving average). November crude last down 19 cents at $82.63/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely higher, supported by Wall Street's rally overnight; analysts expect benchmark index to extend recent gains as foreign equity fund inflows continue. Kospi ended steady at 1878.94 yesterday, but tipped to rise toward 1900. "As proved in the BOJ's interest rates cut and monetary easing program, the necessity of further easing measures by developed countries, such as the U.S., is increasing," says \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2 ; adds, this suggests "liquidity will continue to flow into emerging economies, where rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are relatively higher than (those of developed nations)." Analysts recommend financials, techs, telecom stocks, citing attractive valuation. Investors expected to keep close eye on \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' (005930.SE) 3Q earnings guidance due Thursday; analysts say this could set positive tone for tech sector. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely higher, supported by Wall Street's rally overnight; analysts expect benchmark index to extend recent gains as foreign equity fund inflows continue. Kospi ended steady at 1878.94 yesterday, but tipped to rise toward 1900. "As proved in the BOJ's interest rates cut and monetary easing program, the necessity of further easing measures by developed countries, such as the U.S., is increasing," says \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2 ; adds, this suggests "liquidity will continue to flow into emerging economies, where rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are relatively higher than (those of developed nations)." Analysts recommend financials, techs, telecom stocks, citing attractive valuation. Investors also expected to keep close eye on \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' (005930.SE)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4663.1, hits 5-day high of 4667.1 early after S&P 500 rose 2.1% amid growing expectations of renewed quantitative easing in the U.S. Materials again leading broad-based gains, with BHP (BHP.AU) up 1.9%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 2.3%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 2.8% and \uc2 OZ Minerals\uc2  (OZL.AU) up 4.8%. Financials also strong, with major banks up 1.0%-1.3%, while in consumer discretionary sector, \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . (NWS.AU) (owner of Dow Jones Newswires) up 1.7% and Fairfax (FXJ.AU) up 3.8%. City Index Head of Dealing Michael McCarthy expects Australian equities to grind up toward key resistance at 4700 today. He remains bullish for the medium and longer-term. "Quantitative easing in the U.S. will be the big event for equities globally," says McCarthy. "I think asset prices around the globe are on the way up. If Friday's U.S. jobs data are weak, I think the market will be relying on the Bernanke put option (quantitative easing) and, if it shows strength, investors could also buy the market, so it's a win-win scenario." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to resume recent downtrend vs 1,130.7 late Tuesday, targeting low-1,120s region, helped by Wall Street surge with growing expectation of extra quantitative easing measures from Fed after BOJ's surprise rate cut yesterday, traders say. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,120-1,130 range. "The dollar/won took a breather yesterday on news that the authorities will inspect (selected) banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward positions," but risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as EUR, AUD back on rise, which likely to boost KRW in Asian trade. Market likely on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 3rd straight session Tuesday in support of USD. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may test 82.87--low marked just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-September--later in global day due to broad USD weakness on expectation Fed may announce fresh quantitative easing measures soon, lowering Treasury yields, says senior dealer at major European bank. But says in Asia trading, pair may be supported by expectation of another round of JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo. Demand for safe-haven JPY may also wane some as Nikkei expected to rise after Wall Street rose overnight. Pair last 83.26. Says EUR/USD biased up due to broad USD weakness, may trade 1.3780-1.3850 range in global day. Adds could extend beyond 1.3850 if any equity gains prompt model funds to buy risk-sensitive EUR, encouraging other players to follow, pushing pair to 1.4000. "The euro/dollar has been steadily gaining strength like a zombie." EUR/USD last 1.3836. Tips EUR/JPY may trade 114.70-115.50 band vs last 115.18.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 32.2% at HK$3.907 billion yesterday, as percentage of market total fell to 4.9% vs Monday's 6.0%, indicating bears discouraged by HSI resilience, with index rising for 19th session out of 23. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) led with HK$229.39 million, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$227.17 million, \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$199.88 million. Cnooc (0883.HK), \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$143.10 million, HK$135.49 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 10.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket buying in 3 days may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 25.6 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 14.9 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong AUD likely didn't influence RBA's decision to stay on hold, says Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist John Rothfield. Notes even though AUD is as high as it was in 2008 when RBA went into pause mode, interest rates are lower, terms of trade higher and house prices higher, while financial conditions also easier. "Second, a high \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an ambiguous effect on financial conditions--by reducing the cost of imported machinery to businesses and consumer goods to householders, it is transmitting the positive wealth impact of the resources boom to consumers." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold hits fresh high at $1,340.85/oz before easing a tad to be last up 95 cents early in Asian trading at $1,340.70. In European view of gold's spectacular rally, \uc2 Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken\uc2  says late yesterday that gold now dominated by bullish issues; notes metal rallied through September as potential for additional U.S. quantitative easing triggered a USD slump; says European sovereign debt worries increased again as Irish and Portuguese borrowing costs continue to rise; says potential global devaluation war would have a significant bullish effect on precious metals. "Gold prices in excess of $1,400/oz by year end should be expected," it says, warning of temporary setbacks on profit-taking, USD rebounds. In addition, gold miners indicate their intention to continue reducing their hedge books, a strong indication they retain bullish views, it adds. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 2004ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101006e6a600003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
