\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 7-BOJ cuts rates,eyes asset buying in surprise move\b0\par\par\uc2 1,439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 08:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 5 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan cut interest rates and pledged to keep rates at zero until prices are seen stable in a surprise move showing its concern that a strong yen and slowing growth are undermining a fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2 -- The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also decided to set up, as a temporary measure, a 5 trillion yen ($60 billion) fund to buy assets ranging from government bonds and short-term government securities to commercial paper and corporate bonds, and will also accept another 30 trillion yen of those assets as collateral under a loan scheme.\par \par \uc2 -- It cut the overnight call rate target to a range of zero to 0.1 percent from the previous 0.10 percent by unanimous vote.\par \par \uc2 -- The decision to cut interest interest rates was made by a unanimous vote, but board member Miyako Suda opposed including government bonds among the type of assets the BOJ could buy using its pool of funds.\par \par \uc2 -- It cut its assessment of Japan's economy, saying there is a greater chance of economic growth undershooting a forecast it issued in July.\par \par \uc2 -- It said Japan's economy is improving moderately but moves towards improvement are weakening.\par \par \uc2 -- Governor Masaaki Shirakawa will hold an embargoed news conference, with his comments expected to come out sometime after 4:15 p.m. (0715 GMT). **********************************************************\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2 MINORI UCHIDA, SENIOR ANALYST, BANK OF TOKYO-MITSUBISHI UFJ\par \par \uc2 "It's good that the BOJ eased before the Fed took easing steps. It will make it easier for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make a case for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "But it's probably too much to expect today's steps to fully offset the possible impact of the Fed's eventual quantitative easing. Because Japan's monetary policy has been loose for years, any additional move tends to have a limited impact."\par \par \uc2 "The dollar has so far failed to rise to 84 yen. If it cannot rise beyond the level even after today's New York trade, that could strengthen expectations of further declines in the dollar."\par \par \uc2 SEIJI SHIRAISHI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, HSBC SECURITIES JAPAN\par \par \uc2 "It was an utterly surprising and bold move. The BOJ has sent a favourable message to the markets, which had been expecting it to take only small, gradual steps.\par \par \uc2 "Still, the BOJ's move is unlikely to impact the overall economy much even though the yen weakened briefly, as the root of the problems lies in the weakness of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ may be forced to take more steps such as expanding its new fund to boost liquidity in the markets, as Japan's economy will face a more severe situation ahead with the effects of government stimulus waning and global growth slowing."\par \par \uc2 KYOHEI MORITA, CHIEF ECONOMIST, JAPAN, BARCLAYS CAPITAL JAPAN\par \par \uc2 "It's not quantitative easing but they stressed a zero-rate policy, said they would keep this policy until prices stabilise, and decided to push for credit easing by buying CPs, corporate bonds, ETFs and REITs ...\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ did most of what it could do today, and returned the ball back to the government's court.\par \par \uc2 "It did not decide to buy foreign bonds, thus it did not try to directly affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates ... But Governor Shirakawa has said he was watching the yen rise carefully and the yen is now around the level before the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. So, although the BOJ steps were not directly aimed at affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, the yen rise, along with the BOJ tankan and industrial output figures, was behind their decision today."\par \par \uc2 JUNKO NISHIOKA, CHIEF ECONOMIST FOR JAPAN, RBS SECURITIES\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Japan took a distinct monetary easing stance. The BOJ included most of the credit easing measures that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take.\par \par \uc2 "It was an important decision by the BOJ to say that it will continue monetary easing until prices stablise. These measures will have a major impact on the markets.\par \par \uc2 "Today's action shows the BOJ's strong desire to fight the economic slowdown. The BOJ needed to act quickly as the economy was expected to slow down in the fourth quarter. The Federal Reserve is also expected to ease credit in the near term, so the BOJ had to act now to have a major impact on the market."\par \par \uc2 HIROSHI WATANABE, SENIOR ECONOMIST, DAIWA INSTITUTE OF RESEARCH\par \par \uc2 "Although the actual impact of a rate cut is unclear, it certainly sends out a strong message that the BOJ is willing to work with the government in economic policy.\par \par \uc2 "The new asset purchase scheme also underscores the BOJ's more aggressive easing stance.\par \par \uc2 "The latest steps have prevented Japan from falling far behind the United States in monetary easing, probably helping mitigate pressure to push up the yen further. But they are not likely to cause the yen to weaken."\par \par \uc2 JOHN VAIL, CHIEF STRATEGIST, NIKKO ASSET MANAGEMENT, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "I think it will help stabilise or even weaken the yen. The creation of this fund seems like a semi rinban increase and I think the threat of future rinban is implicit.\par \par \uc2 "I think everyone was a bit surprised by this. A lot of people were saying the BOJ never does anything. I think a lot of people expected something but didn't really know what to expect, something a little bit stronger but not too major, and this is on the side of major."\par \par \uc2 KOJI FUKAYA, CHIEF \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, CREDIT SUISSE\par \par \uc2 "This went beyond what markets have expected. It seems quite comprehensive in that it is going to buy government bonds as well as credit products such as commercial paper etc.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ also cut interest rates to 0-0.1 percent, which I take to mean that they intend to flood the market with cash.\par \par \uc2 "This could set off yen-carry trades, as opposed to dollar-carry trades. The BOJ's decision will reduce the risk of dollar/yen falling below 83 yen, although I still expect it will meet heavy offers around 85 yen."\par \par \uc2 NAOMI HASEGAWA, SENIOR FIXED-INCOME STRATEGIST, MITSUBISHI UFJ MORGAN STANLEY SECURITIES\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ is bringing its monetary policy closer to quantitative easing, allowing market rates to hover near zero and pledging to keep a near-zero interest rate policy in the longer term until prices stabilise.\par \par \uc2 "These steps are more aggressive than markets had expected. The BOJ's decision is a surprise and will have an impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the message it delivers. How effective it will be depends on how low the BOJ will allow the overnight call rate to fall through its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Further steps are possible as risks are clearly to the downside. The next step will likely be expanding the purchases of assets by the newly created fund and they will likely be taken when risks to the economy grow."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 -- The dollar rose against the yen, climbing 0.7 percent on the day to 83.90 yen from about 83.55 before the decision. For yen updates click <FXNEWS>; for prices click <JPY=>\par \par \uc2 -- JGB 10-year futures rose about 0.3 point to 143.77. For JGB updates, click <MMNEWS>, for prices click <2JGBv1>\par \par \uc2 -- The Nikkei average turned positive on the day and climbed 1.5 percent. For stocks click [.T], for the Nikkei click <.N225>\par \par \uc2 LINKS:\par \par \uc2 <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Graphic in BOJ balance sheet/JGB buying:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/ger94p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/ger94p}}}\par \par \uc2 Graphic on BOJ policy rate and JGB yield rate:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/syz76p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/syz76p}}}\par \par \uc2 Graphic on BOJ current account deposits, bank lending:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/ryz76p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/ryz76p}}}\par \par \uc2 Stories on the Japanese economy: [ID:nECONJP]\par \par \uc2 PDF report on yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/fac44p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/fac44p}}}\par \par \uc2 Chronology of BOJ policy moves: [ID:nTOE685013]\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2 -- The BOJ had kept interest rates at 0.1 percent since late 2008. In the wake of sharp gains in the yen it eased monetary policy last December by setting up a facility offering cheap funds to banks, which was expanded in March and in August.\par \par \uc2 -- The BOJ's step in August did little to deter yen gains or falls in share prices as investors saw it as a symbolic gesture that had hardly any effect in supporting the economy.\par \par \uc2 -- Tokyo stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last month for the first time in more than six years to stem a rise in the yen seen as threatening Japan's fragile economic recovery. (Reporting by Rie Ishiguro and Leika Kihara; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (INSTANT VIEW 7)|LANGEN|Table|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101008e6a5000bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
