\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-UPDATE 1-Fed, ECB throwing world into chaos - Stiglitz\b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 23:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Ultra-loose monetary policies destabilizing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , other countries right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * QE won't make up for weak demand, fiscal stimulus needed (Adds quotes, details and byline)\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 5 (Reuters) - Ultra-loose monetary policies by the U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are throwing the world into "chaos" rather than helping the global economic recovery, Nobel Prize-winning economist Joseph Stiglitz said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A "flood of liquidity" from the Fed and the ECB is bringing instability to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, forcing countries such as Japan and Brazil to defend its exporters, Stiglitz told reporters in a conference at Columbia University.\par \par \uc2 "The irony is that the Fed is creating all this liquidity with the hope that it will revive the American economy," Stiglitz said. "It's doing nothing for the American economy, but it's causing chaos over the rest of the world. It's a very strange policy that they are pursuing."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has weakened about 6.5 percent against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the beginning of September as prospects for further monetary easing by the Fed have led investors to seek higher returns elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 That flow of dollars caused \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate in many emerging market countries such as Brazil, which offers strong growth prospects. The Japanese yen has also hit record highs against the dollar on expectation of additional greenback weakness.\par \par \uc2 Recent actions by those countries to curb the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were "necessary," Stiglitz added.\par \par \uc2 "It's natural in that context for them to say -- we can't just let our exchange rates appreciate and destroy our exports," he said.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Brazil doubled a tax on foreign investment in local government bonds, while Japan lowered the target for its benchmark interest rate to a range between zero and 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan also pledged to buy 5 trillion yen ($60 billion) worth of assets, in a strategy similar to the one adopted by the Fed to pump funds into the economy.\par \par \uc2 But additional monetary stimulus will "clearly" not solve the problems caused by lack of global aggregate demand, Stiglitz said.\par \par \uc2 "Lowering the interest rates may help a little bit, but that's much too weak to address the problems facing the United States and Europe," Stiglitz said. "We need fiscal stimulus." (Reporting by Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Jan Paschal)\par \par \uc2 STIGLITZ-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|GNN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101005e6a5001ey\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
