\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Rally To Five-Month Highs, Led By Materials\b0\par\par\uc2 2,600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 00:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China's markets are closed Wednesday for the National Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10944.72  +193.45  +1.80% \par Nasdaq     2399.83   +55.31  +2.36% \par S&P 500    1160.75   +23.72  +2.09% \par FTSE 100   5635.76   +79.79  +1.44% \par Xetra DAX  6215.83   +81.62  +1.33% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 30/32 Unch  0.407% -0.4BP \par 5Y Tsy  100 7/32 +4/32 1.201% -2.8BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 8/32 +1/32 2.476% -0.1BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.19-24     -0.24% \par Range         83.98-82.97 \par EUR/USD       1.3835-38    +1.10% \par Range         1.3859-1.3639 \par AUD/USD       0.9712-14    +0.37% \par Range         0.9725-0.9542 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.90-11.20% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    12.60-12.90% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    13.95-14.25% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,340.30 +$23.50 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,313.30-$1,342.60 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $82.82   +$1.35 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rallied to a five-month high Tuesday, boosted by encouraging services-sector data and hopes that global central banks will follow Japan's lead in stimulating economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average finished up 193 points, or 1.8%, to 10945, its highest close since May 3. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  fueled the Dow's gains, rising 3.4%, while \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  increased 3.1% and DuPont rose 3%. \uc2 American Express\uc2  was the only Dow component in the red as investors digested the company's decision to fight the Justice Department's civil antitrust suit. Shares fell 2%.\par \par \uc2 The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite rose 2.4% to 2400. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-share index gained 2.1% to 1161. All 10 of its sectors traded in positive territory, led by the materials, industrials and financial sectors.\par \par \uc2 Investor optimism comes ahead of some key data later this week. Dow-component \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  unofficially kicks off earnings season after the close Thursday. The closely watched nonfarm payrolls report follows Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 Bruce Bittles, chief investment strategist at Robert W. Baird, said he thinks Friday's jobs report will be a "win-win" for the market. Regardless of what it says about the economic recovery, a weak report will give the Fed more firepower to engage in quantitative easing, while a strong report will signal an improving economy, he said.\par \par \uc2 "As long as the Fed is willing to print money, all these economic reports are going to be considered favorable for the markets," Bittles said. "This will last for as long as the Fed is willing to backstop the stock market."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. services sector expanded at a better-than-expected pace in September, according to the Institute for Supply Management. The ISM report is comprised mainly of comments from service-sector companies that make up the bulk of the U.S. economy, but it also includes construction and public administration.\par \par \uc2 Also boosting sentiment, the Bank of Japan unexpectedly announced it will buy more bonds and cut its key overnight call rate to a range of zero to 0.1%, with rates to remain this low until prices begin to stabilize from deflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 "The effort of reflation is not merely coming from the U.S. and [quantitative-easing] potential, but is also a policy that appears will be taken more widely," said Stephen Lieber, chief investment officer at Alpine Mutual Funds. "Any major stimulus in world trading nations should be regarded positively."\par \par \uc2 Adding to recent calls for aggressive action from the Federal Reserve, Charles Evans, president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 , told The Wall Street Journal that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should do "much more" monetary easing to spur economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Tuesday after the Bank of Japan took drastic easing steps which served to weaken the yen and boost banks as well as exporters.\par \par \uc2 Shares rose sharply after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  exceeded market expectations by cutting its key overnight call rate to a range of 0.0%-0.1% and establishing a Y5 trillion fund to buy assets, including long-term government bonds and commercial paper.\par \par \uc2 "It (the BOJ's move) left an impression that it faced a significant degree of pressure (from the government)," said Fumiyuki Takahashi, equity strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . "There is a reduced risk that the yen will accelerate further from here."\par \par \uc2 But Takahashi added that the Nikkei may not be able to immediately top 9700 this week amid concerns about the earnings of Japanese firms.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended at their highest level this year Tuesday after a choppy session in which insurance firms rose after AIA set the price range for its Hong Kong initial public offering, and developers slid on profit-taking.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect AIA's huge initial public offering to continue to generate interest in the local market in the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 "The index looks likely to test the next target at 23,000 in the near term as sentiment remains strong," said Tanrich Securities investment manager Jackson Wong.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures climbed to a five-month high in U.S. trading Tuesday, spurred by weakness in the dollar and strength in equities.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures settled above $1,340 for the first time as investors fled paper \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of the metal as a perceived store of value.\par \par \uc2 Tin rallied to a record high and copper to a 26-month high Tuesday on the London Metal Exchange, boosted by a stronger euro and fund buying.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell sharply Tuesday against the euro and dropped to its lowest point against the yen since \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as investors' attention turned to global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actions targeted at propping up slowing economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors have fled the dollar as they gauge the Federal Reserve to be increasingly likely to engage in another round of economy-stimulating asset purchases, known as quantitative easing. Heightened chances for a second round of asset purchases by the Fed have weighed on the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Clearly this day has revolved around quantitative easing," said Andrew Busch, global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose 1.2% to its highest mark since February, extending its gains as far as $1.3860. The dollar fell below the Y83 mark for the first time since the Japanese government's Y2-trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sept. 15 to hinder the yen's appreciation. The greenback came within striking distance of its pre-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  15-year low at Y82.87, though the scale of its move lower was modest, dipping less than 0.2% on the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also fell close to its all-time low against the Swiss franc and a two-month low against the U.K. pound. The breadth of the greenback's slide was evident as the ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , sank to its lowest level since January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.3835, up from $1.3690 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.18, down from Y83.44, while the euro strengthened to Y115.11 from Y114.20. The U.K. pound was at $1.5896, above $1.5837. The dollar weakened to CHF0.9671 from CHF0.9721.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index traded at 77.811, down from 78.439 late Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several traders said the dollar's bounce from its intraday low of Y82.96 to Y83.34 over a period of 90 minutes may have been attributable to buying by Japanese sources, some of whom were believed to be close to the government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, North American traders couldn't confirm the identities of the Japanese entities but said there had been no indication of any official Japanese presence in the market. The dollar steadied around Y83.20 in the afternoon session. Volatile moves in late-morning New York trading aren't uncommon, as the timeframe coincides with London traders exiting active trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro remains the leader among major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because "the [European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] looks the most likely not to restart quantitative easing," said Aroop Chatterjee, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  quantitative strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro's gains kickstarted a move for other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  away from the dollar, as investors ignored a better-than-expected reading of a U.S. non-manufacturing index released Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc gained strongly against the dollar because investors are banking on a weaker buck after Fed easing and are heading for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that promise stability, said Lena Komileva, head of G-7 market economics at Tullett Prebon in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Swiss franc is the gold equivalency of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During Asian trading, the Bank of Japan engaged in its own stimulus, cutting its policy interest rate to virtually zero and announcing "comprehensive monetary easing" to spur financial growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Bank of Japan is acting preemptively ahead of the Federal Reserve, because they believe they will make a move on Nov. 3 or 4," said Busch.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda indicated Tuesday he will try to rally support from other nations at the upcoming Group of Seven meeting in Washington for the government's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jose Vinals, director of the International Monetary Fund's monetary and capital market division, repeated calls on governments to continue mending their banking sectors, branding the global financial system as the "Achilles' heel" of the global recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys maturing in the next three to 10 years rose modestly Tuesday as signs continued to pop up that the global economic recovery is slowing, leaving investors convinced the Federal Reserve will have to extend more support to the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, 30-year Treasurys came under pressure as market participants adjusted their quantitative-easing bets. Investors are now almost fully convinced the Fed will have to buy more Treasurys to help the economy this year and they believe buying will be focused on five- to seven-year Treasurys, not on longer-term ones. [ 05-10-10 2201GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A lot of trading now is designed around QE, and the concept of how much the Fed will buy, and where the Fed will buy," said Ira Jersey, director in the U.S. interest-rate strategy team at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  In New York. "That's going to be a big driver" of trading, Jersey said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Tuesday afternoon trading in New York, the three-year note was up 1/32 at 0.592%, the five-year was up 4/32 at 1.201% and the seven-year was up 5/32 at 1.830%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note was up 1/32 to yield 2.476%, not far from its low yield of the year, 2.418%, which it hit in late August. The 30-year bond was off 17/32 to 3.739%. The two-year was flat to yield 0.407% after hitting a record low of 0.395% earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A surprise rate move from Japan helped three- to seven-year Treasurys Tuesday. The country cut its overnight call rate, to a range of 0.0%-0.1%, in an attempt to ease the crippling impact of the surging yen and deflation on the economy. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also said it planned to expand its current asset-purchase plan by $60 billion to add Japanese government bonds and other assets. The move supported what is now a consensus view that central bankers in the U.S. will have to buy more bonds to help the economy heal, in what is known as a quantitative-easing program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With unemployment in the U.S. still weak and inflation low, investors have become wholeheartedly convinced the Fed will have to launch another larger-scale buying program this year to help the economy right itself. Many expect the Fed will announce more buying at its next policy meeting in early November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Additional quantitative easing from the [Federal Open Market Committee] remains the market's pre-occupation," said David Ader, head of government bond strategy at CRT Capital Group LLC in Stamford, Conn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He cited a range for the 10-year Treasury yield of 2.45% to 2.60% that will likely hold into Friday, when the government is set to release its latest report on U.S. employment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds rose Tuesday after the Bank of Japan announced a surprise rate cut and further monetary easing measures including bond purchases, sending the five-year JGB yield to its lowest level in more than seven years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was such a surprising step," said RuiXue Xu, a strategist at RBS Securities Japan. "The Bank of Japan maximized the effect of its announcement by doing everything it could do now."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The five-year yield fell to 0.220%, its lowest since June 2003, and yields could stay under pressure as investors react to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move in coming sessions, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In essence the BOJ is re-establishing quantitative easing, inasmuch as it is expanding its balance sheet against asset purchases by Y5 trillion," said Christian Carrillo, senior rates strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the government hashes out further stimulus spending to be funded by a supplementary budget for the fiscal year ending March 2011, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move essentially restores the positive demand/supply balance seen in the first half of this fiscal year, Carrillo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Bank said the new funds' JGBs buying is a temporary measure and different from the Rinban (outright buying of JGBs from the market) and therefore will be excluded from the banknote rule," said RBS Securities' Xu. "We think this is important and meaningful as the Bank has made a new card and left room to expand JGB buying via boosting the amount of the new funds without restriction within the banknote rule."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Services Sector Expands In September \par Chicago Fed's Evans Calls For Aggressive Action \par Bank Of America To Stop Wholesale Mortgage Lending \par Treasury Releases Report On Tarp \par AIG Chief Risk Officer Retiring \par AIA Forms Board Ahead Of Planned IPO \par GM Unsecured Creditors, U.S. Spar Over $1.5B Suit \par US Treasury Official Sees Borrowing Falling \par Buyout Firms Interested In RBS GBP3B Loan Portfolio \par IMF: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Turmoil Not Yet Major Concern For Stability \par =Fonterra: Whole-Milk Powder Prices Down 2.1% In Auction \par Yum Raises FY10 View As Profit Rises \par Germany, China Say Sustainable Growth Is Needed \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 1301  Australia    Sep SEEK Employment Index \par 0200  Japan        Sep Imported Vehicle Sales \par 0300  Australia    Oct DEEWR Leading Indicator of Employment \par 0500  Japan        Oct Bank of Japan Monthly Report released \par 0800  UK           Sep Car registration \par 0830  UK           2Q  Profitability of UK companies \par 0900  EU           2Q  GDP - 2nd release \par 1000  Germany      Aug Manufacturing orders \par 1100  US           Oct 1 MBA Weekly Mortgage Applications Survey \par 1130  US           Sep Challenger Job-Cut Report \par 1215  US           Sep ADP National Employment Report \par 1300  US           Tsy Secy Geithner speaks on 'The Path to Global Recovery' \par                    in Washington \par 1400  Germany      1Q  Ifo Euro-zone Economic Outlook \par 1430  US           Oct 1 EIA Petroleum Status Report \par N/A   UK           Sep Halifax House Price Index \par N/A   UK           Oct Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee meeting \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-1