\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI Likely Up, May Test 3200; SembMarine In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 1,653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 01:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares expected to advance on Wall Street's overnight rally triggered by better-than-expected U.S. September ISM data, hopes for more economic stimulus measures from central banks worldwide. STI, +0.2% at new year-to-date high of 3162.36 yesterday, may test 3200 for first time since June 2008. "With the index continuing to show its mettle, conquering one resistance after another, there is no strong case to argue that (even higher levels) could not be met," says AmFraser Securities strategist Najeeb Jarhom; tips near-term target of 3266-3270. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sembcorp Marine\f28 \uc2 \f2  (S51.SG) expected to attract keen interest as company secures contracts, options for nearly US$1 billion worth of jack-up rigs; +3.3% at S$4.12 yesterday. Midas Holdings (5EN.SG) may also be in focus as China-based train parts supplier dual-lists in Hong Kong today; +1.0% at S$1.03. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 5-month high $82.99/bbl yesterday, provided support at yesterday's $81.15 low doesn't break, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as 5-day & 15-day moving averages still rising; meanwhile, MACD & stochastics bullish, but latter at overbought. Resistance at psychological $83.00; breach would expose upside to psychological round-numbered levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Fall below $81.15 support would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting $80.77 (Monday's low), then $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.04 (previous cap set September 13), $77.79 (200-day moving average), $77.55 (Thursday's low) and $76.89 (55-day moving average). November crude last down 19 cents at $82.63/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely higher, supported by Wall Street's rally overnight; analysts expect benchmark index to extend recent gains as foreign equity fund inflows continue. Kospi ended steady at 1878.94 yesterday, but tipped to rise toward 1900. "As proved in the BOJ's interest rates cut and monetary easing program, the necessity of further easing measures by developed countries, such as the U.S., is increasing," says \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2 ; adds, this suggests "liquidity will continue to flow into emerging economies, where rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are relatively higher than (those of developed nations)." Analysts recommend financials, techs, telecom stocks, citing attractive valuation. Investors expected to keep close eye on \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' (005930.SE) 3Q earnings guidance due Thursday; analysts say this could set positive tone for tech sector. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely higher, supported by Wall Street's rally overnight; analysts expect benchmark index to extend recent gains as foreign equity fund inflows continue. Kospi ended steady at 1878.94 yesterday, but tipped to rise toward 1900. "As proved in the BOJ's interest rates cut and monetary easing program, the necessity of further easing measures by developed countries, such as the U.S., is increasing," says \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2 ; adds, this suggests "liquidity will continue to flow into emerging economies, where rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are relatively higher than (those of developed nations)." Analysts recommend financials, techs, telecom stocks, citing attractive valuation. Investors also expected to keep close eye on \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' (005930.SE)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4663.1, hits 5-day high of 4667.1 early after S&P 500 rose 2.1% amid growing expectations of renewed quantitative easing in the U.S. Materials again leading broad-based gains, with BHP (BHP.AU) up 1.9%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 2.3%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 2.8% and \uc2 OZ Minerals\uc2  (OZL.AU) up 4.8%. Financials also strong, with major banks up 1.0%-1.3%, while in consumer discretionary sector, \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . (NWS.AU) (owner of Dow Jones Newswires) up 1.7% and Fairfax (FXJ.AU) up 3.8%. City Index Head of Dealing Michael McCarthy expects Australian equities to grind up toward key resistance at 4700 today. He remains bullish for the medium and longer-term. "Quantitative easing in the U.S. will be the big event for equities globally," says McCarthy. "I think asset prices around the globe are on the way up. If Friday's U.S. jobs data are weak, I think the market will be relying on the Bernanke put option (quantitative easing) and, if it shows strength, investors could also buy the market, so it's a win-win scenario." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to resume recent downtrend vs 1,130.7 late Tuesday, targeting low-1,120s region, helped by Wall Street surge with growing expectation of extra quantitative easing measures from Fed after BOJ's surprise rate cut yesterday, traders say. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,120-1,130 range. "The dollar/won took a breather yesterday on news that the authorities will inspect (selected) banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward positions," but risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as EUR, AUD back on rise, which likely to boost KRW in Asian trade. Market likely on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 in