 be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 25.6 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 14.9 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong AUD likely didn't influence RBA's decision to stay on hold, says Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist John Rothfield. Notes even though AUD is as high as it was in 2008 when RBA went into pause mode, interest rates are lower, terms of trade higher and house prices higher, while financial conditions also easier. "Second, a high \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an ambiguous effect on financial conditions--by reducing the cost of imported machinery to businesses and consumer goods to householders, it is transmitting the positive wealth impact of the resources boom to consumers." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold hits fresh high at $1,340.85/oz before easing a tad to be last up 95 cents early in Asian trading at $1,340.70. In European view of gold's spectacular rally, \uc2 Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken\uc2  says late yesterday that gold now dominated by bullish issues; notes metal rallied through September as potential for additional U.S. quantitative easing triggered a USD slump; says European sovereign debt worries increased again as Irish and Portuguese borrowing costs continue to rise; says potential global devaluation war would have a significant bullish effect on precious metals. "Gold prices in excess of $1,400/oz by year end should be expected," it says, warning of temporary setbacks on profit-taking, USD rebounds. In addition, gold miners indicate their intention to continue reducing their hedge books, a strong indication they retain bullish views, it adds. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.885% vs Tuesday's 0.895% given BOJ's decision to implement drastic monetary easing measures, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Naomi Hasegawa. But adds declines in long-term yields should be limited as some investors will likely start selling cash bonds to lighten positions ahead of 10-year JGB tender on Thursday. Lead December JGB futures may move in 143.65-143.90 range vs Tuesday's 143.67. Tokyo traders say demand for JGBs may increase if JPY rises further, pushing Tokyo shares lower amid growing uncertainty over Japan's export-driven economy. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 1957ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101005e6a5000ds\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
