\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Up 1.2% Early; Materials Lead\b0\par\par\uc2 1,624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 01:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4663.1, hits 5-day high of 4667.1 early after S&P 500 rose 2.1% amid growing expectations of renewed quantitative easing in the U.S. Materials again leading broad-based gains, with BHP (BHP.AU) up 1.9%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 2.3%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 2.8% and \uc2 OZ Minerals\uc2  (OZL.AU) up 4.8%. Financials also strong, with major banks up 1.0%-1.3%, while in consumer discretionary sector, \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . (NWS.AU) (owner of Dow Jones Newswires) up 1.7% and Fairfax (FXJ.AU) up 3.8%. City Index Head of Dealing Michael McCarthy expects Australian equities to grind up toward key resistance at 4700 today. He remains bullish for the medium and longer-term. "Quantitative easing in the U.S. will be the big event for equities globally," says McCarthy. "I think asset prices around the globe are on the way up. If Friday's U.S. jobs data are weak, I think the market will be relying on the Bernanke put option (quantitative easing) and, if it shows strength, investors could also buy the market, so it's a win-win scenario." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to resume recent downtrend vs 1,130.7 late Tuesday, targeting low-1,120s region, helped by Wall Street surge with growing expectation of extra quantitative easing measures from Fed after BOJ's surprise rate cut yesterday, traders say. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,120-1,130 range. "The dollar/won took a breather yesterday on news that the authorities will inspect (selected) banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward positions," but risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as EUR, AUD back on rise, which likely to boost KRW in Asian trade. Market likely on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 3rd straight session Tuesday in support of USD. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may test 82.87--low marked just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-September--later in global day due to broad USD weakness on expectation Fed may announce fresh quantitative easing measures soon, lowering Treasury yields, says senior dealer at major European bank. But says in Asia trading, pair may be supported by expectation of another round of JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo. Demand for safe-haven JPY may also wane some as Nikkei expected to rise after Wall Street rose overnight. Pair last 83.26. Says EUR/USD biased up due to broad USD weakness, may trade 1.3780-1.3850 range in global day. Adds could extend beyond 1.3850 if any equity gains prompt model funds to buy risk-sensitive EUR, encouraging other players to follow, pushing pair to 1.4000. "The euro/dollar has been steadily gaining strength like a zombie." EUR/USD last 1.3836. Tips EUR/JPY may trade 114.70-115.50 band vs last 115.18.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 32.2% at HK$3.907 billion yesterday, as percentage of market total fell to 4.9% vs Monday's 6.0%, indicating bears discouraged by HSI resilience, with index rising for 19th session out of 23. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) led with HK$229.39 million, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$227.17 million, \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$199.88 million. Cnooc (0883.HK), \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$143.10 million, HK$135.49 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 10.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket buying in 3 days may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 25.6 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 14.9 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong AUD likely didn't influence RBA's decision to stay on hold, says Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist John Rothfield. Notes even though AUD is as high as it was in 2008 when RBA went into pause mode, interest rates are lower, terms of trade higher and house prices higher, while financial conditions also easier. "Second, a high \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an ambiguous effect on financial conditions--by reducing the cost of imported machinery to businesses and consumer goods to householders, it is transmitting the positive wealth impact of the resources boom to consumers." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold hits fresh high at $1,340.85/oz before easing a tad to be last up 95 cents early in Asian trading at $1,340.70. In European view of gold's spectacular rally, \uc2 Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken\uc2  says late yesterday that gold now dominated by bullish issues; notes metal rallied through September as potential for additional U.S. quantitative easing triggered a USD slump; says European sovereign debt worries increased again as Irish and Portuguese borrowing costs continue to rise; says potential global devaluation war would have a significant bullish effect on precious metals. "Gold prices in excess of $1,400/oz by year end should be expected," it says, warning of temporary setbacks on profit-taking, USD rebounds. In addition, gold miners indicate their intention to continue reducing their hedge books, a strong indication they retain bullish views, it adds. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.885% vs Tuesday's 0.895% given BOJ's decision to implement drastic monetary easing measures, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Naomi Hasegawa. But adds declines in long-term yields should be limited as some investors will likely start selling cash bonds to lighten positions ahead of 10-year JGB tender on Thursday. Lead December JGB futures may move in 143.65-143.90 range vs Tuesday's 143.67. Tokyo traders say demand for JGBs may increase if JPY rises further, pushing Tokyo shares lower amid growing uncertainty over Japan's export-driven economy. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2312 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may trade near 10,500 by year-end as BOJ's unexpected move to cut interest rates and set up Y35 trillion monetary easing program helps to ease upward pressure on yen, says Fumiyuki Takahashi, equity strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . "The lowering of interest rates will have a certain impact," he says. "There is a possibility that negative factors--such as the firmer yen and deflation--that have been putting (downward) pressure on stocks may have been removed." But Takahashi adds Nikkei unlikely to easily top 9700 this week with investors still weighing impact of BOJ move on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, especially overseas reaction to its aggressive easing steps. "The worst-case-scenario of the dollar falling below Y80 has been avoided," he says. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Importers taking advantage of strong AUD will cap gains today, coupled with nervous traders, says NAB Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist John Kyriakopolous. "Traders are looking for a more circumspect day today, with market sentiment still bullish but the top side is likely to be capped by importer supply, taking advantage of the high AUD." AUD/USD last 0.9716, hit a low in Europe of 0.9541. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise following sharp overnight rise in U.S. stocks and continued improvement in investor sentiment from series of BOJ easing steps announced Tuesday (including cut in benchmark policy rate and Y5 trillion fund to buy assets). "There are growing expectations that (globally executed) moves to carry out additional easing will help to support the global economic recovery," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Adds BOJ's steps need to be followed by aggressive government measures on supplementary budget. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9450-9650 vs 9518.76 yesterday close (+1.5%). But analysts say index unlikely to immediately break above 9700 resistance with gains in exporter shares likely to be capped after USD/JPY fell below 83 overnight. Banks and real estate shares expected to get further boost from BOJ measures. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9600 vs Osaka close at 9520. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Aluminium stockpiles on LME, SFE trending lower in recent months, with total stocks falling 13% from 12-month highs, while LME cash prices are up 34% from 12-month lows, says \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 . Higher aluminium prices partly a function of weaker USD but downward trend in stocks an encouraging sign demand may be recovering, CBA says in daily note. Improving aluminium stock draws good news for a market characterised by oversupply for past few years, it says in daily note. Test will come if recent trends result in a flood of refinery restarts that swamp improving demand, particularly as Chinese output may be restricted by recent power cuts, it says. "We need to see ongoing and sustained falls in inventories while production lifts before shifting to a more positive view on the light metal," bank says. LME 3-month metal settled $17 higher overnight at $2,380/metric ton, up 30% from early July low, up 20% from Aug. 25. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 1942ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101005e6a5000do\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
