\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Summary Of US Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 23:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2108 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Stocks rallied to 5-month high, boosted by encouraging services-sector data, hopes global central banks will follow Japan's lead in stimulating economic growth. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  +3.4%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  +3.1% and DuPont +3%. Investor optimism comes ahead of some key data later this week. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2  unofficially kicks off earnings season after close Thursday, closely watched nonfarm payrolls report follows Friday morning. Bruce Bittles, chief investment strategist at Robert W. Baird, says Friday's jobs report will be a "win-win" for the market. Regardless of what it says about economic recovery, a weak report will give the Fed more firepower to engage in quantitative easing, while strong report will signal improving economy. Dow +1.8%, Nasdaq +2.4%, Philly semicons +2.3%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2105 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD fell sharply against EUR, dropped to lowest point vs JPY since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors' attention turned to global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions targeted at propping up slowing economies; "Clearly this day has revolved around quantitative easing," said Andrew Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago. EUR/USD at 1.3835 late Tuesday from 1.3690 late Monday, USD/JPY at 83.18 vs 83.44, EUR/JPY strengthened to 115.11 vs 114.20, GBP/USD at 1.5896, vs 1.5837, USD/CHF at 0.9671 vs 0.9721. USD Index at 77.811 vs 78.439. Stocks rallied to 5-month high, boosted by encouraging services-sector data, hopes global central banks will follow Japan's lead in stimulating economic growth. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  +3.4%, while \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  +3.1% and DuPont +3%. Dow +1.8%, Nasdaq +2.4%, Philly semicons +2.3%. Treasurys maturing in next three to 10 years rose modestly as signs continued to pop up that global economic recovery is slowing, leaving investors convinced Fed will have to extend more support to economy; 3-year yields down 1.6 bps to 0.592%, 10-year yields down 0.1 bp to 2.476%. December Comex gold rose $23.50 to $1,340.30/oz as investors fled paper \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of metal as a perceived store of value. Crude oil futures climbed, spurred by weakness in dollar, strength in equities. November Nymex crude settled at $82.82/bbl. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2101 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD should test further downside, targeting 0.9300 on a three-month basis, say \uc2 UBS\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists. \uc2 UBS\uc2  says RBA's on-hold decision yesterday will accelerate losses. "For now, the most recent development, extreme positioning and unstable risk sentiment are all factors supporting the case for additional AUD/USD downside by trend." Pair last 0.9714. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2100 GMT [Dow Jones] It's too early to get overly bearish AUD and fresh cyclical highs are around the corner, with any dip to 0.9335 a buying opportunity, says CBA chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Richard Grace. Notes in particular Australia-U.S. two-year bond spread is widening and medium-term economic outlook suggests should push out further. "If market volume remains low, and there are good reasons to suggest it will, there will be fewer market disruptions to push the AUD lower. In an environment of firm Asian and global growth, this suggests appreciation for the AUD." Forecasts AUD/USD parity by March 2011, trading 1.0200. Latest 0.9715. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2058 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to claw back yesterday's loss following improved global sentiment, says Craig's Investment Partners broker Bryon Burke; "New Zealand's small sell off will be reversed on today's market given the strength of overseas bourses." Adds bluechips Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), Telecom (TEL.NZ) likely the first to move higher as led losses yesterday. Strong NZD/USD may weight on exporters such as Rakon (RAK.NZ), Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ). NZX-50 shed 0.3% yesterday to close at 3218.32. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2018 GMT [Dow Jones] US stocks surge, as does just about every "risk" asset, after the BofJ tries again to arrest the yen's rising, the most note-worth move among several from different central banks that have "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" written all over them. DJIA jumps 193 (1.8%) to 10945, its highest close since May 3. S&P 500 rises 24 (2.1%) to 1161, Nasdaq Comp surges 55 (2.4%) to 2400. It's not just stocks: gold, crude, the euro all rise sharply, as investors are betting on a widespread bout of competitive devaluations among central banks. That's good for nominal asset prices right now, but seems to us it's bad for everybody in the long run. (paul.vigna@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2011 GMT [Dow Jones] The question is: how much longer will Beijing tolerate the USD devaluation by Ben Bernanke? From its June highs, the USD has dropped 12.4% and expressed in real terms from the perspective of China's State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this means that our biggest creditor has lost over $100 billion when adjusted for the purchasing power loss in the dollar, says Tyler Durden at ZeroHedge. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.zerohedge.com/article/china-has-lost-over-100-billion-dollar-adjusted-terms-its-ust-holdings-few-short-months?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+zerohedge%2Ffeed+%28zero+hedge+-+on+a+long+enough+timeline%2C+the+survival+rate+for+everyone+drops+to+zero%29"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.zerohedge.com/article/china-has-lost-over-100-billion-dollar-adjusted-terms-its-ust-holdings-few-short-months?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+zerohedge%2Ffeed+%28zero+hedge+-+on+a+long+enough+timeline%2C+the+survival+rate+for+everyone+drops+to+zero%29}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 2008 GMT [Dow Jones] Buoyant risk appetite, expectations of additional Fed easing likely to keep USD heavy on day after BoJ's surprise announcement to cut policy rate, expand its asset buying programme buoyed hopes central banks "would ride to the rescue of the global economic recovery," says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones. Also notes generally upbeat global service sector data, spurried strong gains in equities and commodity prices. USD Index last 77.815. tips resistance on rallies toward 78.50, initial support at 76.80. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2004 GMT [Dow Jones] Mixed prices for long-term Tsy futures reflects very large spread trade -- selling almost 12K Dec 30-yr Tsy bond contracts and buying about 23K Dec 10-yr Tsy note contracts. Dec 30-yrs settled 7/32 lower at 133-22. Dec 10-yrs settled up 5+/32 at 126-20+. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2000 GMT [Dow Jones] Triggered by heightened quantitative easing speculation, fed-funds futures continued to shed market odds for 1st tightening to occur next autumn. Nov 2011 contract priced in only 18% chance for FOMC to raise funds rate to 0.5%, down from 24% chance Mon, and 28% chance Fri. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1948 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ government bonds tad higher, largely tracking moves in Australia after RBA took markets by surprise, held cash rate steady. Interest rate swaps tad lower than yesterday's close; "We didn't really follow Australia as hugely," says local bond trader. Says Australia, NZ "two different kettles of fish at the moment." Local bond trader expects quiet session given lack of data both locally and in Australia; November 2011 bond yields down 2 bps at 3.47%, May 2021's down 2 bps at 5.03%, 2-year swaps down 2 bps at 3.72% from yesterday's close. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1923 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD, AUD/USD benefiting after "bullish sentiment stormed back to life overnight" aided by BoJ's earlier decision to implement new stimulus measures, stronger than expected U.S. PMI data, says Westpac Bank. Notes both Australian, NZ data calendars thin today. NZD/USD last 0.7494, looks set to test October 2009 high of 0.7635. AUD/USD last 0.9720, on course to test recent 0.9750 highs. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 1708ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101005e6a5000cm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
