\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Next Step - SocGen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 12:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] On its own the BOJ's latest easing moves will not turn USD/JPY around says SocGen as the driver of its downtrend has been falling U.S. rates and not rising Japanese rates. However, the increased commitment to fight deflation is clear and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will very likely be the next step says SocGen. USD/JPY now at 83.41 within the day's range of 83.99-83.24. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1017 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan's benchmark KSE-100 +0.1% at 10,058.84, led by oil stocks. "Foreign investment in the local stock market helped yield positive results and the index comfortably maintained the five-figure mark," says dealer. Adds, index hit 9,993 low, but fresh buying from foreign funds, financial institutions helped market recover. Another dealer says market "is in a state of uncertainty" following last week's decision by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise benchmark interest rate by 50 bps to 13.5%; "Companies' profitability could be hit as most textile and cement makers had taken loans." Adds, much will depend on relationship with foreign donor agencies, assuring support to country's economy following flash floods. Pakistan Oilfields (POL.KA) +0.6% at PKR238.50, \uc2 Attock Petroleum\uc2  (ACPL.KA) +1.3% at PKR353.90. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1012 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF posted an all-time closing low Monday, and both the short and long-term trends remain bearish, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . The spot is closing in on its all-time low of 0.9640, seen in March 2008, and risk below here is towards 0.9325. Now at 0.9664, the outlook remains bearish while below 0.9880, says BarCap. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1012 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades higher at 24.504-24.554 from 24.49 late Monday as EUR rebounds in part driven by the continued weakening of local stocks. The cross is seen staying in the narrow range just above 24.500 as investors wait for the Thursday's Czech economic data releases, a local trader says. (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end +0.5% at 969.28 led by gains in heavyweight blue chips. Portfolio inflows likely to have continued as USD/THB keeps descending toward the 30.00 psychological mark. After 1.5% correction yesterday, "the SET Index seems ready to resume its upward momentum, at least for the near-term," says Bualuang Securities strategist Pongrat Ratanatawanananda. Initial resistance at 975, support at 960. Among most actives, PTT (PTT.TH) flat at THB297, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +0.3% at THB325, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.9% at THB134.50, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) +1.7% at THB17.10. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD fell sharply after the RBA no-rate change decision confirming last week's bearish momentum divergences says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Now at 0.9595 the bank pegs near-term support at 0.9550 which it says now appears vulnerable and a deeper pullback to 0.9450 cannot be excluded. However, BarCap looks for a this area to hold and for renewed strength to follow for a test of the 2008 peak at 0.9850. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0953 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR falls to 85.15 late from 85.25 late Monday on capital inflows into local stock market; foreign funds invested $14.7 million into local stocks, according to data from National Clearing Company of Pakistan. However, pair could resume up move on expected demand to repay external debt and for oil-import payments, says analyst; tips 85.10-85.20 range Wednesday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0951 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  lowers its recommendation on Gas Natural's (GAS.MC) bonds to hold from add due to a variety of unknown variables in the near term that could increase spread volatility. BNPP adds that the court decision in France over \uc2 Sonatrach\uc2 's right to boost the price of gas supplied since 2007 could continue to pressure bonds. However, the brokerage says there is long-term value in cash bonds beyond 2012. "While we do think the bonds offer good long-term value, we note that risks to the downside are increasing and we would gain more comfort if management reiterated its commitment to reducing debt and improving the overall credit profile," BNPP says. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premium rises; September 2011 premium at 2.49 vs 2.45 late Monday. Dealer with state-run bank says trade mostly dominated by importers while worries of more rate hikes by RBI fueling paying interest. "Eight out of 10 participants are importers. Inflation, too, continues to worry authorities and could lead to more aggressive monetary action," says dealer. However, ongoing rally may meet psychological resistance at 2.50, may attract receiving interest by exporters who have so far been conspicuous by their absence, adds dealer. (anant.kala@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB drops to 30.14 vs 30.17-30.19 late yesterday, set to maintain weak bias, traders say. USD selling by exporters, gold traders partly offset by suspected buying by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say 2 dealers. "We saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying quite actively at around 30.14 level, possibly in the range of hundreds of million dollars," says one dealer. Caution about potential new measures to halt THB's rise prevent players from shorting USD aggressively. BOT's senior director Wongwwatoo Potirat says bank studying whether additional measures to curb capital inflows is needed. Most participants still expect THB to remain on uptrend near-term, unless BOT comes up with some draconian measures. Next support at 30.10, then 30.00, resistance at 30.20. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT [Dow Jones] The rate of Swiss unemployment is expected to stagnate at 3.6% in September, according to an average of six economist estimates compiled by DJN. The jobless rate has held at 3.6% since July, after peaking at a 12-year high of 4.5% in January. The data are due October 8 at 0545 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds lower as recent sharp gains, easing fund inflows prompt some players to take profit in early trade; but downside limited as baht gaining vs USD, inspiring some buy back, especially by foreign names, says local dealer. Adds, baht appreciation may spur continued fund inflows, which could push yields lower, but some players have awaited chance to sell to lock in profit. Eyes on MOF's auction of tenor due June 2023 worth THB8 billion Wednesday. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.47%/2.43% vs 2.44%/2.42% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.61%/2.58% vs 2.61%/2.59%, June 2019 at 2.77%/2.75% vs 2.74%/2.71%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com) [ 10-05-10 0623ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101005e6a500071\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
