\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Rise As Downside Hedges Sought\b0\par\par\uc2 1,666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 04:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options rise to 10.95%/11.65% from 10.80%/11.50% in NY yesterday, as investors buy short-term hedges, particularly against spot downside risks. Options dealer at major Japan bank says demand for downside hedges up as spot earlier falls to 83.35, close to 83.15 low marked Friday, which lowest level since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on September 15. Market expects BOJ to announce more monetary easing steps at end of 2-day policy-setting meeting in coming hours. But trader says if measures don't exceed market expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to expand liquidity-provision measures - possibly by increasing supply of 6-month loans or buying more short-term government notes - USD/JPY will likely fall as such steps have already been priced in. Pair last 83.44. But trader says as long as spot doesn't fall below 83.00 level, vols unlikely to change much later in global day. Says one player sells USD-call/JPY-put contract with strike price of 86.50, implied volatility of 10.95%, to expire end of November, in unknown amount. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down at 31.100 vs 31.250 yesterday as market stabilizes after pair hit intraday low of 30.976 yesterday. "The weakness in the Taiex today is reflected more widely in Asia, so there's been less foreign inflows than in the past few days," says trader. Tips pair to trade in 31.035-31.310 band today with some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in day. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. bank Dahlman Rose says after visit to BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) South American mines including Antamina in Peru and Escondida and Spence in Chile that copper supply challenges are obvious. "These mines are some of the largest copper ore bodies in the world, but they are also a microcosm of what is going on in the broader copper mining industry. These facilities are all experiencing decreasing ore grades, elevated capital costs, and increasing labor costs, all of which should be supportive of copper prices over the medium-term." Dahlman Rose analysts says that BHP believes its Olympic Dam copper mine in Australia may eventually rival Escondida in terms of scale but says this is a multi-decade project, "with Olympic Dam not reaching its full capacity until the middle portion of this century." Bank reiterates its buy rating on BHP Billiton with target price of US$100 based on New York listed shares which closed Monday at US$76.38, down $1.31. "We would view BHP shares as a cornerstone investment in any commodities/materials portfolio," says report. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI barely needs breather, last down 0.1% at 22,595.35, as profit-taking pressures absorbed by investors eagerly waiting for market pullback to buy. Volume robust at HK$9.51 billion in first 15 minutes, could be matter of days (maybe even today) that HK market volume cracks HK$100 billion mark. Taifook Securities says HK "acting as mainland liquidity outlet," noting liquidity flow into HK's property, financial markets partially mirrored by drastic upsurge in CNY deposits in August, up 25.8% vs July. HKEx (0388.HK) obvious beneficiary of ample liquidity, +0.5% at HK$157.60; notable underperformer \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK), down 1.2% at HK$134.10 on concerns of escalating family feud. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares off 0.7% at 8186.05 early on Wall Street losses overnight, expectations that recent TWD strength will hurt exporters. "In particular, fears over the strong Taiwan dollar are driving electronics shares down today," says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. Adds shares likely to trade in 8150-8250 range, tips market to follow U.S. market cues closely overnight again as China stock markets closed through October 7 for National Day holiday. TSMC (2330.TW) down 1.1% at NT$61.2 HTC (2498.TW) off 1.7% at NT$685, Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 0.86% at NT$116.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD heading for parity for first time since floating, but pair at significant risk of correction, which means it's time to take profit, says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Greg Gibbs. Notes long positions in pair nearing April highs again, suggests quick unwind is possible. "In the short term, the AUD appears to be overbought"; expects AUD/USD to rise to between 1.02 and 1.05 in coming year. AUD/USD last 0.9669. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei near flat midday, up just 0.02% (1.53 points) at 9382.59 as investors await outcome of BOJ's 2-day policy board meeting today. \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro says Nikkei likely to rise to 9500-9550 level if BOJ announces additional monetary easing measures, including increasing buying of long-term JGBs and asset-backed securities. But adds positive impact likely short-lived with U.S. jobs data due Friday. "Deflation is at the root of the stronger yen," Ishiguro says. "Unless measures to stem deflation are outlined, the trend will not change even if the BOJ supplies ample liquidity." 16/33 Topix subindexes higher; SMFG (8316.TO) up 1.9% at Y2,423, \uc2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \uc2 up 0.9% at Y113 after recent sharp falls on worries over global capital rules. Auto shares underperform; \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) down 1.2% at Y2,970. \uc2 Japan Tobacco\uc2  (2914.TO) down 3.8% at Y260,400 on sales concerns following tax hike on Friday. \uc2 KDDI\uc2  (9433.TO) up 4.0% at Y404,500 after announcing launch of latest Android smartphone.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW briefly hits 1,135.5, extending gains on news government will inspect \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward positions of some banks from October 19 to check whether they're following new rules introduced earlier this year. In June, government said local banks should limit their forward positions to 50% of their capital, while foreign banks operating in Korea - whose forward positions averaged 301% as of end-April - should lower their positions to 250% of capital. "When the rules were first introduced, the understanding was that the rules limit banks from building their long positions in forwards, which means that exporters and offshore players won't be able to sell as much dollar-forwards as they intend to," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; adds news of inspection having similar negative impact on KRW. But expects pair likely peaked at 1,135.5 for today, with exporter selling, foreign-equity fund inflow likely to weigh. Tips 1,126-1,135 range. Pair now up at 1,132.2 vs 1,122.3 yesterday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil little changed; rally slows on stronger USD, weaker U.S. equities, but some analysts still optimistic; closure of Houston Ship Channel until later today, though likely to have limited impact on fundamentals, could reduce crude imports next week, Jim Ritterbusch of Ritterbusch & Associates says; adds "large speculative community is looking for only minor supportive headlines." Still, says possibility remains that stronger USD, weaker share markets could push oil down to $78-$79/bbl. November Nymex crude contract down 7 cents at $81.40/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.7% at 442.53, off 440.63 earlier low, in thin trade after senior government economist Vo Tri Thanh tells state media earlier today that economy facing risks of high inflation, and therefore government will have to tighten monetary policy. "Thanh's comments hurt investor sentiment because many players are waiting for the government to act to boost cash liquidity," says brokerage analyst. Adds a mild correction likely because of support from buying interest ahead of 3Q earnings season starting later this week. Tips key index in 440-445 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper market participants "eagerly awaiting" news on producer premiums for 2011, says Triland, as it acts as an important indicator of market's fundamental strength. Chilean copper giant Codelco usually agrees premium with global customer base around this time with news often emerging from Japan or China. Many participants expecting increase on this year's $80/ton level and this probably needed to confirm widespread perception that copper market is tightening into 2011 on the back of strong demand, stagnant supply. Spot premiums in Singapore have been steady around $90/ton in recent months with copper to be delivered to Shanghai recently quoted at $120/ton including freight. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares higher, with investor interest on utilities, mining and holding firms lending support; benchmark PSEi +0.2% at new all-time high of 4196.84; support tipped at 4150, resistance at 4200. Market breadth negative though, with losers beating gainers 38 to 31. "The low inflation rate in September is good news that will continue to fuel the market's advance. Although we'll likely start to see profit-taking fairly soon," says Astro del Castillo, managing director at First Grade Holdings. Among gainers, Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH) +3.2% at PHP9.85, \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH) +5.2% at PHP30.40, coal producer Semirara Mining (SCC.PH) +5.3% to PHP150. Among losers, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) down 0.3% to PHP6.18, shopping mall operator SM Prime Holdings (SMPH.PH) down 0.2% at PHP13.06. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 2240ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101005e6a50001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
