\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:KLCI May Fall;1455-1480 Band Likely To Persist N/T\b0\par\par\uc2 1,634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 02:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may slip, taking cue from weaker U.S. stocks overnight. "Given the lack of new developments on the local front, the KLCI will continue to follow the direction of overseas markets, but stay within a narrow range," dealer says. KLCI ended 0.3% lower at 1462.27 yesterday; benchmark index, which has been confined to 1455-1480 range for past 1.5 weeks, unlikely to break out of band anytime soon as investors await release of government's 2011 budget on Oct. 15, dealer says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 MMC Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2194.KU) may be in focus after announcing it's leading consortium that has bid to takeover state-owned \f28 \uc2 \f2 UEM Group Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0023 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher at 3.0940 vs 3.0900 yesterday, tracking broad USD recovery vs EUR, most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; profit-taking after MYR's recent run-up to fresh 13-year high also supporting greenback, dealer says. Reckons players hesitating to buy MYR as they see chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in support of USD) increasing. Dealer tips pair to consolidate in narrow 3.0900-3.1000 range today. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0020 GMT [Dow Jones] STI could gain another 5%-10% in 4Q10 after 9% rise in 3Q10, with large-cap laggards like banks catching up in current quarter, says Nomura. Forecasts year-end target of 3200-3300; "seasonally, the market tends to be firmer towards December as investors take more active views ahead of year-end." Expects Singapore banks to re-rate on "attractive valuations and underperformance relative to ASEAN peers", with concerns over margin pressure "more than priced in". Notes banks trade at P/B multiples of around 1.3X, dividend yields of 4%. In contrast, says Singapore's gaming sector could disappoint in 4Q10 given lofty, unsustainable valuations; "gaming could be vulnerable to disappointment." Remains upbeat on office plays given rising rents in central business district, higher prices of recent office property transactions. STI +0.9% at 3157.45 yesterday. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures open up, may extend gains to 143.65 amid growing speculation that BOJ could take aggressive monetary easing measures, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Testuya Miura. Tokyo traders say if BOJ decides to increase size of long-term JGB buying, that could prompt more investors to buy cash bonds, pushing yields lower sharply. BOJ expected to announce policy decision around 0330 GMT. JGB futures, which briefly touched 7-year high of 143.59 earlier in session, now at 143.52, +0.07. Benchmark 10-year JGB yield at 0.925%, down 1 bp. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says since last week the closer to YTD lows stocks trade, the weaker the price performance they deliver. Attributes such movements to level of potential selling pressure in high price territory. Thinks approaching deadline for squaring long margin positions established around April highs should bring an end to the recent surge in selling pressure, possibly laying groundwork for broader-based rally. Also notes 100-day advance-decline ratio for TSE's 1st section has reached overbought levels (122.1% as of September 29), underscoring likelihood of short-term setback. "Still, we recommend viewing such a correction as a meaningful entry opportunity for the medium to long term," writes analyst in report to clients. Thus far today, \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO), SMFG (8316.TO) have both hit fresh 2010 lows. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to rise, tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, while expected losses in local, regional stock markets likely to lend support, says trader with local bank. But bonds won't move sharply, market participants still looking for cues from broader financial markets, reflecting outlook for global economy, says trader. Tips yields to fall by 1-2 bps. Yesterday, 2-year bond yield ended steady at 0.42%, 10-year down 3 bps at 2.01%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT [Dow Jones] Another rangebound, subdued trading day likely in store for Malaysia government bonds in absence of fresh leads as participants stay cautious, awaiting unveiling of government's 2011 budget on October 15; in near term, benchmark 7-year bond may be in focus, with slight downward bias, as investors brace for additional supply; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to announce reopening of September 2017 MGS late this week. "Market expectations are for an auction size of MYR3.0 billion to MYR3.5 billion, which will add to the bond's existing outstanding amount of MYR7.0 billion," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end higher, with May 2013 +1 bp at 3.16%, August 2015 +3 bps at 3.26%, September 2017 +1 bp at 3.55%, November 2019 +2 bps at 3.64%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Wall Street's fall overnight may provide investors excuse to take profit in Singapore shares after yesterday's run-up to new 28-month highs. STI expected to hold above resistance-turned-support of 3100 after closing +0.9% at 3157.45, highest since June 2008. "With no clear sign of improvement in (U.S. economic) fundamentals, sentiment could turn easily. Any significant lower-than-expected economic or corporate releases could be the main catal