\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: JGB Futures May Rise To 143.65 - Mizuho Sec\b0\par\par\uc2 1,783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 02:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures open up, may extend gains to 143.65 amid growing speculation that BOJ could take aggressive monetary easing measures, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Testuya Miura. Tokyo traders say if BOJ decides to increase size of long-term JGB buying, that could prompt more investors to buy cash bonds, pushing yields lower sharply. BOJ expected to announce policy decision around 0330 GMT. JGB futures, which briefly touched 7-year high of 143.59 earlier in session, now at 143.52, +0.07. Benchmark 10-year JGB yield at 0.925%, down 1 bp. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says since last week the closer to YTD lows stocks trade, the weaker the price performance they deliver. Attributes such movements to level of potential selling pressure in high price territory. Thinks approaching deadline for squaring long margin positions established around April highs should bring an end to the recent surge in selling pressure, possibly laying groundwork for broader-based rally. Also notes 100-day advance-decline ratio for TSE's 1st section has reached overbought levels (122.1% as of September 29), underscoring likelihood of short-term setback. "Still, we recommend viewing such a correction as a meaningful entry opportunity for the medium to long term," writes analyst in report to clients. Thus far today, \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO), SMFG (8316.TO) have both hit fresh 2010 lows. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to rise, tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, while expected losses in local, regional stock markets likely to lend support, says trader with local bank. But bonds won't move sharply, market participants still looking for cues from broader financial markets, reflecting outlook for global economy, says trader. Tips yields to fall by 1-2 bps. Yesterday, 2-year bond yield ended steady at 0.42%, 10-year down 3 bps at 2.01%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT [Dow Jones] Another rangebound, subdued trading day likely in store for Malaysia government bonds in absence of fresh leads as participants stay cautious, awaiting unveiling of government's 2011 budget on October 15; in near term, benchmark 7-year bond may be in focus, with slight downward bias, as investors brace for additional supply; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to announce reopening of September 2017 MGS late this week. "Market expectations are for an auction size of MYR3.0 billion to MYR3.5 billion, which will add to the bond's existing outstanding amount of MYR7.0 billion," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end higher, with May 2013 +1 bp at 3.16%, August 2015 +3 bps at 3.26%, September 2017 +1 bp at 3.55%, November 2019 +2 bps at 3.64%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Wall Street's fall overnight may provide investors excuse to take profit in Singapore shares after yesterday's run-up to new 28-month highs. STI expected to hold above resistance-turned-support of 3100 after closing +0.9% at 3157.45, highest since June 2008. "With no clear sign of improvement in (U.S. economic) fundamentals, sentiment could turn easily. Any significant lower-than-expected economic or corporate releases could be the main catalyst," says Asiasons WFG Securities. Stocks likely in focus include JES International (EG0.SG), for signing new contracts to build 8 vessels for undisclosed sum, \uc2 Suntec REIT\uc2  (T82U.SG), for securing S$700 million term loan to service debt, CNA Group (5GC.SG), for proposed placement of shares to unnamed Asian conglomerate. JES +2.9% at S$0.36 yesterday, Suntec +0.7% at S$1.53, CNA flat at S$0.225. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD higher at 1.3142 vs 1.3133 late Asia Monday tracking overnight USD gains vs EUR, says trader with foreign bank; but gains might be limited given people are still keen to "seek risk" in Asia. "We could see some profit taking after recent gains in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but overall, people remain optimistic about Asian economies." Tips resistance at 1.3170, support 1.3120. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi likely to open lower, tracking slump in U.S. market overnight and as market takes breather after recent sustained rise, analysts say; but expect market still on track toward 1900 mark, on back of ample liquidity, foreign interest in local shares. "Foreign buying in Korean shares is expected to be sustained for a while because global liquidity is likely to increase amid recent market expectations the Fed will ease further, while investor preference on riskier assets will also stay strong following the continued weakening in the dollar," says \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  in note. Although KRW continues to rise sharply, relatively stronger JPY will also buffer any negative impact on Korean exporters, says Meritz Securities. Kospi ended +0.1% at 1879.29 Monday, initial support tipped at 1870. Analysts favor banks, insurers this week as investors have been showing strong interest for those stocks on low-valuation. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate after setting 2-month high of $82.38/bbl but settling 11 cents lower at $81.47/bbl yesterday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD bullish, 5-, 15-day moving averag