\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Stocks May See Short-Term Setback-Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 02:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says since last week the closer to YTD lows stocks trade, the weaker the price performance they deliver. Attributes such movements to level of potential selling pressure in high price territory. Thinks approaching deadline for squaring long margin positions established around April highs should bring an end to the recent surge in selling pressure, possibly laying groundwork for broader-based rally. Also notes 100-day advance-decline ratio for TSE's 1st section has reached overbought levels (122.1% as of September 29), underscoring likelihood of short-term setback. "Still, we recommend viewing such a correction as a meaningful entry opportunity for the medium to long term," writes analyst in report to clients. Thus far today, \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO), SMFG (8316.TO) have both hit fresh 2010 lows. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to rise, tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, while expected losses in local, regional stock markets likely to lend support, says trader with local bank. But bonds won't move sharply, market participants still looking for cues from broader financial markets, reflecting outlook for global economy, says trader. Tips yields to fall by 1-2 bps. Yesterday, 2-year bond yield ended steady at 0.42%, 10-year down 3 bps at 2.01%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT [Dow Jones] Another rangebound, subdued trading day likely in store for Malaysia government bonds in absence of fresh leads as participants stay cautious, awaiting unveiling of government's 2011 budget on October 15; in near term, benchmark 7-year bond may be in focus, with slight downward bias, as investors brace for additional supply; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to announce reopening of September 2017 MGS late this week. "Market expectations are for an auction size of MYR3.0 billion to MYR3.5 billion, which will add to the bond's existing outstanding amount of MYR7.0 billion," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end higher, with May 2013 +1 bp at 3.16%, August 2015 +3 bps at 3.26%, September 2017 +1 bp at 3.55%, November 2019 +2 bps at 3.64%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Wall Street's fall overnight may provide investors excuse to take profit in Singapore shares after yesterday's run-up to new 28-month highs. STI expected to hold above resistance-turned-support of 3100 after closing +0.9% at 3157.45, highest since June 2008. "With no clear sign of improvement in (U.S. economic) fundamentals, sentiment could turn easily. Any significant lower-than-expected economic or corporate releases could be the main catalyst," says Asiasons WFG Securities. Stocks likely in focus include JES International (EG0.SG), for signing new contracts to build 8 vessels for undisclosed sum, \uc2 Suntec REIT\uc2  (T82U.SG), for securing S$700 million term loan to service debt, CNA Group (5GC.SG), for proposed placement of shares to unnamed Asian conglomerate. JES +2.9% at S$0.36 yesterday, Suntec +0.7% at S$1.53, CNA flat at S$0.225. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD higher at 1.3142 vs 1.3133 late Asia Monday tracking overnight USD gains vs EUR, says trader with foreign bank; but gains might be limited given people are still keen to "seek risk" in Asia. "We could see some profit taking after recent gains in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but overall, people remain optimistic about Asian economies." Tips resistance at 1.3170, support 1.3120. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi likely to open lower, tracking slump in U.S. market overnight and as market takes breather after recent sustained rise, analysts say; but expect market still on track toward 1900 mark, on back of ample liquidity, foreign interest in local shares. "Foreign buying in Korean shares is expected to be sustained for a while because global liquidity is likely to increase amid recent market expectations the Fed will ease further, while investor preference on riskier assets will also stay strong following the continued weakening in the dollar," says \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  in note. Although KRW continues to rise sharply, relatively stronger JPY will also buffer any negative impact on Korean exporters, says Meritz Securities. Kospi ended +0.1% at 1879.29 Monday, initial support tipped at 1870. Analysts favor banks, insurers this week as investors have been showing strong interest for those stocks on low-valuation. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate after setting 2-month high of $82.38/bbl but settling 11 cents lower at $81.47/bbl yesterday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD bullish, 5-, 15-day moving averages rising; but stochastics turned bearish, while spinning-top candlestick pattern reflecting indecision of traders completed yesterday. Support at $80.77 (yesterday's low); breach would target $80.45 (hourly chart swing low), then $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.04 (previous cap set Sept. 13), $77.55 (Thursday's low, near 200-day moving average), $76.77 (55-day moving average) and $75.52 (Sept. 27 low, near 100-day moving average). But rise above $82.38 resistance would reinstate near-term positive outlook, exposing upside to $82.97 (Aug. 4 top), then psychological levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). November crude last down 15 cents at $81.32/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 84.00 if BOJ decides at policy board meeting to buy more long-term JGBs or asset-backed securities, says Akira Hoshino, senior dealer at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. Adds move would send message that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking steps in quantitative easing. Nikkei reports today that BOJ may take bolder-than-anticipated steps, such as buying ABSs. Hoshino adds that if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, coordinating with BOJ's moves, pair may rise above 85.00. On the other hand, says that with expectations for some more modest steps already factored in pair may fall to below 83.00, approaching level that triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mid-September, if BOJ meeting only comes up with easing measures such as expanding 3-month, 6-month lending facilities introduced last December, August respectively. Pair last at 83.57 on small rising trend during early Asia morning. Says EUR/USD may fall to 1.3550 as correction comes in recent USD weakness. EUR/USD falls Monday after hitting highest level since March at 1.3809 as extent of Ireland's budget deficit revealed; pair last 1.3679. Says EUR/JPY may trade in 113.00-115.00 range vs last 114.33. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 1.2% at 4569.1, hit 2-day low of 4559.2 in thin trading after Wall Street's S&P 500 fell 0.8%. Materials underperforming after U.S. peer declines, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) down 1.5%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 2.1%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) down 1.5%. Financials, energy and consumer discretionary also weak, with major banks down 1.0%-1.5%, Woodside (WPL.AU) down 1.4%, \uc2 News Corp\uc2  (NWS.AU) (owner of Dow Jones Newswires) down 1.9% and \uc2 David Jones (DJS.AU) \uc2 down 5.1% ex-div. Index is near critical short-term support from last week's low at 4559.0. But the early fall could be exaggerated by light volumes and nerves before today's RBA meeting. Traders say support could emerge after RBA decision, with 25 bps rate hike widely expected. "Yesterday we outperformed on thin volume and today we're underperforming on light volume," says RBS Morgans investment adviser Danny Dreyfus. "Most people think the RBA will hike today, so the market is vulnerable beforehand. There's also been some profit-taking on Wall Street after strong gains on light volume, but there's a lot of cash on the sidelines." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.5% at 3211.78 on offshore weakness, sharply lower business confidence data eyed in NZ Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Dave Schaper; "it won't move into positive ground but the question is whether it will be mildly weak or if the sells off will gain strength." NZIER survey shows a net 6.0% of surveyed firms expect general conditions to improve over next 6 months vs 18% in 2Q; Schaper says probably hadn't surprised people but gave another reason for people to sit on sidelines. Adds Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) down 4.2% to NZ$1.85 as stock went ex-dividend earlier. Says Warehouse (WHS.NZ) down 1.3% to NZ$3.92 following 3.7% move higher yesterday; "the company came out this morning and said it was not aware of any information (moving the stock)." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to rise vs 1,122.3 late yesterday in Seoul, finally taking breather following 7 straight sessions of losses after Wall Street ends down. But other than taking profits on USD shorts, buying unlikely to be aggressive as majority in market remain biased towards weaker pair, foreign-equity fund inflows likely to cap upside, traders say. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,120-1,130 range. "Dominant views for a broadly weaker dollar and high expectations for a BOK rate hike will make it difficult for the market to anticipate any strong rebound in the pair," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 19.4% at HK$5.76 billion yesterday, 6.0% of total market volume vs Thursday's 8.3%, suggesting bears remained hesitant to step up bets, with HSI's rise yesterday marking 18th session of gains out of 22. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) grabbed lion's shares with HK$643.97 million as shares appeared very overbought, in 6-day, 11.5% rally. Cnooc (0883.HK) in second place with HK$251.81 million, then Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$249.89 million. \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK), A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$200.50 million, HK$200.25 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 2012ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101005e6a500009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
