\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  May Wait For Lull In Dollar Fall To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Monahan and Takashi Mochizuki  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 931 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 10:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are on high alert for another move by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but many expect the authorities will bide their time before another attack on the market.\par \par \uc2 Since dumping a massive Y2 trillion on the market Sept. 15, the authorities have kept the markets guessing on their next move, even as the yen has again climbed back near its 15-year highs against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 While surprise is a key element in this contest with market traders, traders think the government will wait a bit longer.\par \par \uc2 Officials from Prime Minister Naoto Kan on down have repeatedly expressed concern over the yen, but market moves recently are more a story of broad dollar weakness than of yen strength. That suggests it would largely be a waste of money to try to stop the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, so the government may be forced to tolerate falls in the dollar below the 15-year low marked at Y82.87 just before the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The government "won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the midst of the dollar's fall, but will wait for the storm to pass," said Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Japan for \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Doing so would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which runs \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, to get more bang for its buck, Yamamoto said.\par \par \uc2 Such thinking may have been a factor in the Finance Ministry's decision not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, dealers say, despite the dollar falling Friday to Y83.15, its lowest since Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the euro and yen, fell to 78.029 Friday, its lowest since Jan. 25. And even as the dollar fell against the yen, the euro rose against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "To have a consistent policy, the MOF would have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar falls more sharply against the yen, but the line of defense is likely to gradually move lower," said Masaaki Kanno, chief economist at J.P. Morgan Securities Japan. "Especially if we have very weak U.S. economic indicators again driving the fall in the dollar-yen, the MOF would have to tolerate a rate even below Y83."\par \par \uc2 Comments from the Japanese government's chief spokesman shortly after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fueled speculation Tokyo may try to keep the dollar above that level. Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku said he thought the ministry was defending the Y82 level, although Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said there was no set level that might prompt further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The resulting uncertainty has made investors hesitant to push the dollar down more sharply. At 0800 GMT Monday, the dollar was at Y83.25.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar at Y82 or Y83 is not the line in the sand as many media reported or as Sengoku said," said \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 's Yamamoto. Comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which tightly controls its script on the topic, may not reflect the reality within the ministry, Yamamoto said.\par \par \uc2 Keeping short-term investors guessing is likely an important tactic for the Japanese government, as it attempts to prevent further yen appreciation, while limiting the money and diplomatic capital it must spend to do so.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be very selective and the Japanese would want to maximize the effect of every yen," \uc2 UBS\uc2  said in a recent report. That is in part because the U.S., which appears to have given the green light to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may not remain tolerant for long as Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could complicate its push for China to let the yuan rise, \uc2 UBS\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers say Japan may also be forced to change tactics on how it actually conducts any further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sept. 15, it did so through orders placed directly on EBS, a widely used electronic trading platform. That made for a dramatic splash across trading rooms, where the Bank of Japan's name was visible on EBS screens.\par \par \uc2 But given that the government may prefer to keep speculators guessing on any further moves, it could opt to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  through orders placed with individual banks, as it had done previously in its 2003-2004 drive to bring down a strong yen.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the government's tactics, some prominent observers say any further efforts ultimately will be for naught. "Yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may push down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly, but it can't change a long-term trend," former senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara recently told Dow Jones Newswires. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is like giving morphine to a patient: You could end up making the patient worse," said Sakakibara, who earned the nickname "Mr. Yen" in the 1990's for his energetic campaign to control the yen's rate through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The inability to reverse broad market trends through unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  like \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's is another reason it would be unwise for Tokyo to target times of the day when volume is low, analysts said. While doing so could briefly lead to a sharper initial hit to the yen, "the end result could be simply that once the liquidity comes back, the yen is likely to come back to its initial level," said \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 's Yamamoto.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 04, 2010 04:48 ET (08:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101004e6a4001d3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
