bunds are up 0.10 at 131.41 and the 10-year bund is up at 99.78 to yield 2.275%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] Swiss GDP growth this year will reach 3%, well above other European economies, while inflation will stay low, according to \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . "Despite strong GDP growth, core inflation is at zero and the economy should have enough spare capacity to keep inflation low for a while," says economist Michael Saunders. "As a result, we still expect a prolonged period of low and stable rates." (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia government bonds mixed, with 5-year paper falling on profit-taking, dealers say. "Investors are taking profit on short-end notes after the 5-year reached a new all-time high on Friday. But they'll buy again tomorrow as yields are still higher than U.S. Treasury yields and other regional bond yields," dealer at local brokerage says. 5-year yield up at 7.04% vs 6.98% Friday. 20-year yield at 8.39% vs 8.36%, 10-year at 7.49% vs 7.50%, 15-year at 8.0% vs 8.02%, 28-year at 8.74% vs 8.77%. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are up 0.10 at 131.41 and \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  expects the contract to continue trading sideways on Monday. "At the start of the week with no strong reading from the trend strength indicator look for choppy price action. Wait for range extremes before trying a punt," the bank says. Resistance at 131.57 and 132.14, while support is at 130.83 and 130.63. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is set to rise against the Swiss franc amid the planned introduction of a new tax treaty between Switzerland and Germany, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "With CHF deposits accounting for almost half of all foreign deposits in Switzerland, tax amnesties are generally CHF-negative," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says, adding that "EUR/CHF should see some upside." Euro currently trades at CHF1.3444. (goran.mijuk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY, EUR/JPY falling as European banks selling, taking cue from Japanese share prices, which fell into negative territory after giving up all gains in morning session, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. USD/JPY last 83.36, says target at 82.90, where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds, not expecting MOF action at that level as its money to conduct JPY-selling limited, MOF may act at psychological 82.50 level. Says attention on BOJ meeting ending tomorrow; many analysts expect additional easing measures. But dealer disagrees with mainstream view; says ahead of Friday's U.S. jobs data release, "tomorrow isn't the best timing for the BOJ to act." If that's the case, dealers say USD/JPY may fall sharply on disappointment. EUR/JPY last at 114.70, tipped support at 114.20. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares hit 8-month high of 8300.04 earlier but end flat at 8246.10 on profit-taking, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. Expects Taiex to condoliate in 8200-8300 range Tuesday. TWD strength "also weighed on electronics exporters such as TSMC (2330.TW) and HTC (2498.TW)." TSMC off 0.2% at NT$61.90, HTC down 1.3% at NT$698.00, \uc2 Nan Ya Plastics\uc2  (1303.TW) down 1.5% at NT$67.50 after fire hits company's plant Sunday, which will cause estimated NT$150 million in revenue losses, according to company's spokesman. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0605 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei down 0.3%, S&P/ASX 200 +1.0%, Kospi +0.1%, HSI +1.4%, STI +1.1%, KLCI +0.4%, SET +0.2%, NZX-50 +0.5%, Taiex flat, PSEi +1.9%, Sensex +0.6%. Markets in China closed for holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY weaker vs USD, on speculation BOJ will loosen monetary policy further at its Tuesday meeting; may expand programs to pump more cash into money market. USD/JPY at 83.40 vs 83.32 in late NY trade; EUR/JPY at 114.71 vs 114.83, EUR/USD at 1.3753 vs 1.3784. Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rebounded in September from previous month to hit highest level on record; reserves totaled $289.78 billion, topping previous high of $285.96 billion set in July; reserves rose by $4.42 billion from August, when reserves fell by $600 million on-month. Spot gold at $1,316.50/oz, down $2.10 cents vs NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures down 19 cents at $81.39/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures down 0.3% at 9360, intraday low, on likely selling by foreign investors, Japanese brokerage manager says. "The market is going to be volatile this month as funds' earnings (reports) are coming up in November," he says. Adds funds may choose to focus on short-term trading to secure profits quickly for October. Puts index support at 25-day moving average of 9300. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT [Dow Jones] Speculation increasing BOJ will introduce additional easing measure by expanding its fund-provision programs at its 2-day meeting ending Tuesday. Such move has already been priced into current level, meaning if BOJ's action meets view, investors resume selling USD/JPY on "buy-on-rumor, sell-on-fact", dealers say. Pair last 83.51; possible for USD/JPY to fall below 83.00 in near term; focus now on whether Japan steps into market again when USD/JPY below 83.00. "If it doesn't act there, investors may start buying the yen massively," says Shinichi Hayashi, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.2% at 980.65 at midday pause in active trade. Rises dominated by firmer energy heavyweights, which drive benchmark to fresh 986.75 intrasession high in 14 years, though profit-taking trims gains. "There's no negative cue but just that baht is so strong (against the dollar). So, some investors are cashing in profit as they think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not hike (at Oct 20 meeting) as previouly expected," says SCB Securities Senior Vice President Sukit Udomsirikul. Adds approaching technical resistance of 990 also prompts some selling. Support put at 975. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +2.7% at THB305, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +3.2% at THB163 though off from almost 1-year-high of THB163.50, Thai Oil (TOP.TH) +4.6% at THB56.50 though off from almost 28-month-high of THB57, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.5% at THB137. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 0247ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a400041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
